WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.229 --> 00:00:05.800
When an anomaly is first detected by an SCP
Foundation Field Agent, it’s up to the Foundation’s

00:00:05.800 --> 00:00:11.969
Mobile Task Forces to tag and bag the impossible
entities before they can do any more harm.

00:00:11.969 --> 00:00:17.570
Sometimes these retrievals are uneventful,
other times... not so much - especially when

00:00:17.570 --> 00:00:24.890
they’re dealing with brutal forces of nature
like SCP - 096, also known as, The Shy Guy.

00:00:24.890 --> 00:00:30.449
A creature that, from its very first interaction
with the Foundation, had a reputation for

00:00:30.449 --> 00:00:33.690
being dangerous and needed to be feared.

00:00:33.690 --> 00:00:37.940
A series of vague sightings and mysterious
disappearances up in the frosty mountains

00:00:37.940 --> 00:00:42.219
of the Yukon first sparked the Foundation’s
interest. When they were certain that they

00:00:42.219 --> 00:00:48.720
had an anomaly on their hands, two retrieval
teams - Zulu 9-A and Zulu 9-B - were dispatched

00:00:48.720 --> 00:00:54.000
to secure and contain the entity. Zulu 9-A
took the lead in a heavy duty chopper, equipped

00:00:54.000 --> 00:01:00.769
with .50 caliber GAU-19 Heavy Machine-Guns
and carrying an AT-4 Anti-Tank launcher. They

00:01:00.769 --> 00:01:06.570
were prepared for anything - or so they thought
- as they established a visual on SCP - 096

00:01:06.570 --> 00:01:08.860
while two clicks away from the target.

00:01:08.860 --> 00:01:13.940
They couldn’t get a clear line of sight
on the creature, but it appeared to be stationary,

00:01:13.940 --> 00:01:18.800
docile, and was making no attempt to flee.
Piece of cake, right? Little did they know

00:01:18.800 --> 00:01:24.810
that SCP - 096 was just looking away from
them. If it was facing towards them, it’d

00:01:24.810 --> 00:01:29.650
be a whole different horror story, as Zulu
9-A were about to find out.

00:01:29.650 --> 00:01:33.530
The team landed their helicopter next to the
creature and were shocked to see that it was

00:01:33.530 --> 00:01:38.250
completely naked, in spite of the subzero
temperatures all around them. The creature

00:01:38.250 --> 00:01:43.060
was unnaturally thin, as though it’d been
starved for weeks, with bone-white skin and

00:01:43.060 --> 00:01:47.391
unnaturally long limbs. The team guessed that
the creature’s arms must have been at least

00:01:47.391 --> 00:01:52.890
1.5 metres long, but its docile nature and
insubstantial body mass gave the impression

00:01:52.890 --> 00:01:58.250
that it wouldn’t prove too difficult to
contain. That is, until they saw its face.

00:01:58.250 --> 00:02:02.880
Zulu 9-A’s Captain was the lone survivor
of the incident, as he was lucky enough to

00:02:02.880 --> 00:02:07.590
be looking away when the creature turned towards
his team. The rest of the squad ended up staring

00:02:07.590 --> 00:02:14.290
eye to eye with SCP - 096, and from that moment
on it wasn’t docile anymore. The creature

00:02:14.290 --> 00:02:20.950
began to whimper, then cry, then sob uncontrollably
in a way that sounded eerily human. This sudden

00:02:20.950 --> 00:02:25.740
change in temperament startled the rest of
Zulu 9-A and they opened fire on the creature.

00:02:25.740 --> 00:02:31.299
Under the hail of gunfire, SCP - 096 entered
a murderous frenzy and began tearing into

00:02:31.299 --> 00:02:36.040
the hapless squad of soldiers. While its flesh
and organs did seem to take damage as a result

00:02:36.040 --> 00:02:41.250
of the barrage of .50 caliber rounds from
the helicopter mounted machine guns, its skeletal

00:02:41.250 --> 00:02:46.430
structure remained intact and it continued
its onslaught, tearing the team limb from

00:02:46.430 --> 00:02:51.150
limb even after they’d blown practically
all the flesh from the creature.

00:02:51.150 --> 00:02:55.390
The AT-4 Anti-Tank launcher proved equally
ineffective at stopping SCP - 096 while it

00:02:55.390 --> 00:03:00.819
was in attack mode, and it was only after
slaughtering the entire team that it returned

00:03:00.819 --> 00:03:05.510
to its docile state. Nobody knows exactly
what the creature did to Zulu 9-A after the

00:03:05.510 --> 00:03:09.540
gunfire started, but no trace of the team
was left behind.

00:03:09.540 --> 00:03:14.590
Zulu 9-B touched down soon after, and with
a grave warning from the Captain not to look

00:03:14.590 --> 00:03:19.939
at the creature’s face, they were finally
able to subdue it. A bag was placed over SCP

00:03:19.939 --> 00:03:25.239
- 096’s face, which seemed to soothe it
enough to move it to a nearby Foundation facility.

00:03:25.239 --> 00:03:29.329
Little did they know, they’d just obtained
one of the deadliest SCPs of all time, and

00:03:29.329 --> 00:03:33.939
while it may have been under lock and key
for now, it seems inevitable that it would

00:03:33.939 --> 00:03:36.579
get out and cause more violence and chaos.

00:03:36.579 --> 00:03:41.349
Research and containment procedures for the
SCP - 096 were put under the command of Dr.

00:03:41.349 --> 00:03:46.090
Dan, a senior researcher at the site. It was
his job to find out exactly what this being

00:03:46.090 --> 00:03:50.879
was capable of, and the more he tested, the
more he realized that they were dealing with

00:03:50.879 --> 00:03:53.359
something truly terrifying.

00:03:53.359 --> 00:03:57.330
Disposable D-Class personnel were used to
figure out exactly what it was that caused

00:03:57.330 --> 00:04:02.359
the creature to enter its attack mode. Just
as it had during the initial retrieval mission,

00:04:02.359 --> 00:04:08.230
SCP - 096 went berserk when any of the attending
personnel saw its face. In this stage, it

00:04:08.230 --> 00:04:13.620
would enter a period of considerable and unstoppable
distress for one to two minutes - covering

00:04:13.620 --> 00:04:18.590
its face and wailing loudly. When the period
of distress ended, the creature would mercilessly

00:04:18.590 --> 00:04:24.130
slaughter every D-Class that had seen its
face, and just like with Zulu 9-A, no trace

00:04:24.130 --> 00:04:26.810
of their bodies would be left behind.

00:04:26.810 --> 00:04:31.700
Dr. Dan was horrified and intrigued by this
phenomenon. The creature killed anyone that

00:04:31.700 --> 00:04:36.360
saw its face directly, but could the same
be said for indirect depictions of the creature’s

00:04:36.360 --> 00:04:41.500
face - such as images and videos? Dr. Dan
was desperate to find out.

00:04:41.500 --> 00:04:46.660
More D-Class personnel were brought in to
test this, to frightening results. Dr. Dan

00:04:46.660 --> 00:04:50.890
found that the creature did indeed still enter
attack mode when people saw pictures and videos

00:04:50.890 --> 00:04:56.070
of SCP - 096’s face. The creature seemed
to have an innate sense of when people were

00:04:56.070 --> 00:05:00.750
viewing these representations even when it
should have had no conceivable way of knowing.

00:05:00.750 --> 00:05:04.670
It didn’t matter how far away or how many
barriers were in place between the viewer

00:05:04.670 --> 00:05:10.120
and the creature, the attack mode would still
activate. And once it did, it seemed as though

00:05:10.120 --> 00:05:14.810
nothing could stop the creature from hunting
down the one who saw its face.

00:05:14.810 --> 00:05:18.750
With all of this new data, special containment
procedures were devised to keep the creature

00:05:18.750 --> 00:05:25.840
safely under lock and key. Its cell was a
5 m x 5 m x 5 m airtight steel cube, fitted

00:05:25.840 --> 00:05:32.030
with advanced pressure sensors and laser detectors
to ensure that SCP - 096 remained in its cell

00:05:32.030 --> 00:05:36.660
without risking anyone having visual contact
with the creature’s face. All cameras and

00:05:36.660 --> 00:05:40.930
video equipment were strictly forbidden, and
weekly checks for any cracks or holes in the

00:05:40.930 --> 00:05:45.680
containment cell were mandatory. Of course,
none of this would stop the creature if anyone

00:05:45.680 --> 00:05:51.210
somehow saw its face. In order to solve that
little problem, Dr. Dan would need to continue

00:05:51.210 --> 00:05:53.120
his research.

00:05:53.120 --> 00:05:57.600
To find a method of subverting the creature’s
deadly glance, they needed to know exactly

00:05:57.600 --> 00:06:02.630
what they were dealing with - but how could
they, when even a glance at a photo or video

00:06:02.630 --> 00:06:08.130
of the being meant certain death? A potential
solution was proposed - creating an artistic

00:06:08.130 --> 00:06:12.780
representation of the creature’s face, something
that hadn’t yet been attempted. But how

00:06:12.780 --> 00:06:17.620
would they achieve such a feat? Simple - they’d
procure a D-Class prisoner with some artistic

00:06:17.620 --> 00:06:21.790
talent, and they found one who had been a
tattoo artist before becoming a Foundation

00:06:21.790 --> 00:06:22.820
guinea pig.

00:06:22.820 --> 00:06:28.470
Dr. Dan formulated an ingenious plan for keeping
this D-Class alive for long enough to accurately

00:06:28.470 --> 00:06:34.540
draw an image of SCP - 096’s face: He would
be placed in a Bathysphere diving bell several

00:06:34.540 --> 00:06:39.700
kilometers underwater, and tens of kilometers
away from the containment cell where the SCP

00:06:39.700 --> 00:06:43.870
was being held. The D-Class was made to look
at a photograph of the creature’s face,

00:06:43.870 --> 00:06:49.330
and then replicate that image in a pencil
sketch. Dr. Dan first confirmed that the creature’s

00:06:49.330 --> 00:06:53.830
attack mode is only activated by the creature’s
face by having the D-Class look at a series

00:06:53.830 --> 00:06:59.960
of photos of the SCP’s body parts one by
one, finally finishing with its face. The

00:06:59.960 --> 00:07:04.770
D-Class began drawing and even remarked on
how creepy the SCP’s facial features were,

00:07:04.770 --> 00:07:06.920
despite not knowing the deadly context.

00:07:06.920 --> 00:07:13.210
Meanwhile, back in its containment cell SCP
- 096 sensed someone viewing its face, and

00:07:13.210 --> 00:07:17.890
entered its inconsolable crying state followed
by its attack mode. It broke out of containment

00:07:17.890 --> 00:07:22.640
easily, and began making a beeline for the
D-Class, traversing the miles between it and

00:07:22.640 --> 00:07:27.270
its prey. The D-Class didn’t know it as
he locked the finished drawing into a separate,

00:07:27.270 --> 00:07:32.220
autonomous submersible, but he was already
dead. As the drawing made its way up to a

00:07:32.220 --> 00:07:37.340
researcher on the surface, SCP - 096 dived
into the water, and started swimming down

00:07:37.340 --> 00:07:44.740
towards the artist. Minutes later, the bathysphere
was breached, and the D-Class was torn to

00:07:44.740 --> 00:07:45.740
shreds.

00:07:45.740 --> 00:07:51.180
SCP - 096 was recaptured without issue by
surface recovery team Foxtrot-303-A, and further

00:07:51.180 --> 00:07:55.890
testing on the drawing showed that artistic
representations of SCP - 096’s face were

00:07:55.890 --> 00:08:00.780
in fact harmless. From this experience, we
now know that the creature has a gaunt face

00:08:00.780 --> 00:08:06.720
with totally white eyes, possibly indicating
blindness, and a grossly extended jaw. Nevertheless,

00:08:06.720 --> 00:08:12.140
Dr. Dan was adamant that SCP - 096 was too
dangerous to be left alive, and requested

00:08:12.140 --> 00:08:16.120
permission from the upper echelons of the
Foundation to terminate the creature by any

00:08:16.120 --> 00:08:21.890
means necessary. However, the doctor’s request
would fall on deaf ears, until...

00:08:21.890 --> 00:08:25.680
It all started with a seemingly innocent image.
(Use this image: http://scp-wiki.wdfiles.com/local--files/incident-096-1-a/G9zmJ.jpg)

00:08:25.680 --> 00:08:29.900
While it’s now been redacted for your safety,
the black spec inside the yellow circle was

00:08:29.900 --> 00:08:37.120
once a minuscule image of SCP - 096, taken
unknowingly in the 1990s by a semi-professional

00:08:37.120 --> 00:08:40.550
mountaineer. One day, they were looking at
old photographs when his eyes passed over

00:08:40.550 --> 00:08:46.810
the tiny spec without even noticing he had
seen anything. But SCP - 096 noticed, and

00:08:46.810 --> 00:08:49.199
began entering its attack mode.

00:08:49.199 --> 00:08:53.000
It tore through its steel containment unit
like tissue paper, causing the release of

00:08:53.000 --> 00:08:57.300
a nerve agent that killed a number of attending
Foundation staff. The monster then fled the

00:08:57.300 --> 00:09:02.769
base and began pursuing its prey, with Mobile
Task Force Tau-1 in hot pursuit. Dr. Oleksei,

00:09:02.769 --> 00:09:07.029
who was helping to manage the site where the
SCP was contained, was in dismay over the

00:09:07.029 --> 00:09:11.250
situation. Dr. Dan was out of the country
at the time, trying to discover more about

00:09:11.250 --> 00:09:15.779
the creature’s origins. However, he did
leave the Mobile Task Force with a new secret

00:09:15.779 --> 00:09:19.990
weapon against the rampaging Shy Guy… Project
SCRAMBLE.

00:09:19.990 --> 00:09:24.221
SCRAMBLE were state of the art goggles featuring
a new technology created by Dr. Dan, which

00:09:24.221 --> 00:09:29.350
- using artistic renditions of 096’s facial
features - could detect and scramble the features

00:09:29.350 --> 00:09:35.300
of SCP - 096 into an unrecognizable form,
preventing the normally deadly effect of gazing

00:09:35.300 --> 00:09:41.839
upon on its face. In theory, this would allow
MTF Tau-1 to engage safely with 096 once its

00:09:41.839 --> 00:09:46.959
prey had been eliminated and bring it back
into containment. But disaster struck on two

00:09:46.959 --> 00:09:52.069
fronts. First, the prey in question was located
in a population center, creating the potential

00:09:52.069 --> 00:09:53.670
for a huge loss of life.

00:09:53.670 --> 00:09:58.379
And the second bigger problem was that the
SCRAMBLE technology didn’t work, as stray

00:09:58.379 --> 00:10:01.949
pixels of the creature’s face would reach
the eyes of the task force before the internal

00:10:01.949 --> 00:10:06.779
microprocessor had time to scramble them.
The mission turned into a death sentence,

00:10:06.779 --> 00:10:12.170
as SCP - 096 slaughtered almost the entire
task force, as well as a number of civilians

00:10:12.170 --> 00:10:18.189
in town - including an infant and its entire
family. It was a monumental disaster, made

00:10:18.189 --> 00:10:23.370
even worse by a final revelation: Dr. Dan
and Dr. Oleksei had themselves facilitated

00:10:23.370 --> 00:10:28.389
the entire containment breach and allowed
the resulting massacre to happen, with Dr.

00:10:28.389 --> 00:10:32.720
Dan hoping it would be enough motivation for
Foundation Command to green-light his research

00:10:32.720 --> 00:10:35.100
into destroying the creature.

00:10:35.100 --> 00:10:39.379
Anything that would give him the opportunity
to kill this thing would be worth the bloodshed.

00:10:39.379 --> 00:10:44.540
His plan worked and the SCP Foundation saw
it his way, approving his request to neutralize

00:10:44.540 --> 00:10:51.970
SCP - 096. However, success comes at a cost
for Dr. Dan. Once he figures out a way to

00:10:51.970 --> 00:10:56.980
finally kill the creature, though done in
the line of duty, he himself will be terminated

00:10:56.980 --> 00:11:00.279
by the Foundation for his crimes against humanity.

00:11:00.279 --> 00:11:05.110
But considering how much damage SCP - 096
is capable of causing if it ever got to a

00:11:05.110 --> 00:11:10.190
major population center, or - even worse - was
ever caught on camera and broadcast to a worldwide

00:11:10.190 --> 00:11:15.699
audience, the doctor himself would likely
deem his own death a justifiable cost. To

00:11:15.699 --> 00:11:20.050
this day, the Foundation is researching ways
to kill the creature, and they’re still

00:11:20.050 --> 00:11:24.930
looking for their silver bullet. And the pressure
is on. They hadn’t known about the seemingly

00:11:24.930 --> 00:11:30.170
innocent picture that sparked the last containment
breach. The one taken decades ago, in which

00:11:30.170 --> 00:11:36.129
the Shy Guy had only occupied four tiny pixels.
Four tiny pixels that resulted in multiple

00:11:36.129 --> 00:11:38.829
innocent lives lost.

00:11:38.829 --> 00:11:43.390
So be careful where you look because who knows
how many other photos of the creature are

00:11:43.390 --> 00:11:48.059
lurking out there, photos with an innocent
dot in the background. Your eyes glance over

00:11:48.059 --> 00:11:53.630
it, not even noticing the little blip, until
you hear a distant wailing that seems to be

00:11:53.630 --> 00:12:00.829
getting closer, and closer, and closer. And
then... it’s already too late.

00:12:00.829 --> 00:12:06.100
Well hello there, SCP Fans! Let’s get this
out of the way first - You’ve seen the title

00:12:06.100 --> 00:12:11.570
and thumbnail, and you’re probably wondering:
Wait, is Siren Head an SCP? And the answer

00:12:11.570 --> 00:12:17.959
is No. Siren Head is not an SCP, it’s a
freaky internet monster created by renowned

00:12:17.959 --> 00:12:21.069
Canadian Horror Artist Trevor Henderson.

00:12:21.069 --> 00:12:26.610
Your next question is probably: What’s with
this crazy-looking server bank behind me?

00:12:26.610 --> 00:12:32.350
Allow me to introduce you to the Anom-O-Tron
6000, the latest advancement of supercomputing

00:12:32.350 --> 00:12:37.520
owned and operated exclusively by the SCP
Foundation. It’s capable of a whole bunch

00:12:37.520 --> 00:12:42.240
of interesting features - calculating ideal
containment methods, running state of the

00:12:42.240 --> 00:12:47.569
art Foundation webcrawler software, making
and editing fun SCP YouTube videos - but the

00:12:47.569 --> 00:12:52.689
most interesting feature that the Anom-O-Tron
has to offer is running hyper-advanced simulations

00:12:52.689 --> 00:12:58.310
regarding all things anomalous, with data
fed into it by thousands of real-life experiments

00:12:58.310 --> 00:13:00.819
conducted by the SCP Foundation.

00:13:00.819 --> 00:13:05.270
It’s going to save countless dollars and
lives for Foundation researchers, but way

00:13:05.270 --> 00:13:10.589
more importantly, it’s going to let us explore
all kinds of weird and wacky What If scenarios

00:13:10.589 --> 00:13:16.829
that would never happen organically. Could
SCP-343 beat Goku, or Superman? Who’s a

00:13:16.829 --> 00:13:22.880
deadlier supernatural killer, SCP-106 or Freddy
Kreuger? Would the world simply explode from

00:13:22.880 --> 00:13:29.300
adorableness if SCP-999 and Baby Yoda ever
crossed those multiversal barriers and met?

00:13:29.300 --> 00:13:35.290
We can explore all of these questions - Along
with any others you may have down in the comments.

00:13:35.290 --> 00:13:39.949
Seriously, comment it. We dare you! - but
to get things started, we figured we’d make

00:13:39.949 --> 00:13:46.480
the Anom-O-Tron pit the Foundation’s lankiest
nightmare monster, SCP-096, against the internet’s

00:13:46.480 --> 00:13:48.249
- Trevor Henderson’s Siren Head.

00:13:48.249 --> 00:13:51.749
Sorry, Slender Man, you’re just not cool
anymore.

00:13:51.749 --> 00:13:56.649
So, without further adieu, let’s kick the
Anom-O-Tron into gear and see how this wild

00:13:56.649 --> 00:14:02.449
battle plays out. It’s time for SCP-096
Vs Siren Head!

00:14:02.449 --> 00:14:07.310
Ricky and Eliot, a pair of aspiring travel
YouTubers from California, were taking a vacation

00:14:07.310 --> 00:14:12.920
to the Pacific Northwest to spend some time
in the area’s beautiful, lush forests. However,

00:14:12.920 --> 00:14:16.811
while the duo were both big fans of travel
for its own sake, they weren’t just here

00:14:16.811 --> 00:14:22.199
for the trees and fresh air. You see, despite
their jetsetting lifestyle, Ricky and Eliot’s

00:14:22.199 --> 00:14:27.019
subscribers and views hadn’t exactly been
phenomenal lately. Turns out, hiking vlogs

00:14:27.019 --> 00:14:31.149
and GoPro footage of the two of them jumping
into absurdly blue water in Bali weren’t

00:14:31.149 --> 00:14:34.839
exactly that interesting to people who weren’t
actually there.

00:14:34.839 --> 00:14:40.320
But Ricky had formulated an ingenious plan
to pull the two of them out of obscurity.

00:14:40.320 --> 00:14:45.429
They would hunt down and film real footage
of the legendary Siren Head, a supernatural

00:14:45.429 --> 00:14:50.639
monster said to haunt forests and isolated
graveyards. There were plenty of fakers out

00:14:50.639 --> 00:14:55.560
there on YouTube, but if the two of them could
get real footage of Siren Head - and also,

00:14:55.560 --> 00:14:59.929
preferably, survive the experience - they’d
be framing their Golden Play Buttons in no

00:14:59.929 --> 00:15:00.929
time.

00:15:00.929 --> 00:15:05.259
But they were already encountering certain
issues. For starters, the two Californians

00:15:05.259 --> 00:15:10.519
had severely underestimated just how cold
it was in the Pacific Northwest, and as such,

00:15:10.519 --> 00:15:15.790
were already shivering from bone-deep cold.
The second issue was food: While the two of

00:15:15.790 --> 00:15:20.119
them had thought ahead and brought a selection
of tasty protein bars, they’d also gotten

00:15:20.119 --> 00:15:24.910
hungry during the early hours of their walk,
and eaten all of them, leaving them feeling

00:15:24.910 --> 00:15:28.480
severely out of luck by the time dinner rolled
around.

00:15:28.480 --> 00:15:32.929
And the third and perhaps most problematic
issue of all was the fact that Ricky and Eliot

00:15:32.929 --> 00:15:38.179
didn’t exactly have a great sense of direction.
During all of their previous trips, they’d

00:15:38.179 --> 00:15:43.040
been on guided tours with local experts. In
this instance, wanting to take all the Siren

00:15:43.040 --> 00:15:48.360
Head glory and clout for themselves, they’d
decided to come in alone, with the incredibly

00:15:48.360 --> 00:15:54.199
janky Google Maps app to guide them. As such,
as the night was creeping in, the two aspiring

00:15:54.199 --> 00:15:57.459
YouTube celebrities had no idea where they
were.

00:15:57.459 --> 00:16:01.429
Needless to say, it wasn’t exactly an ideal
situation.

00:16:01.429 --> 00:16:06.059
And to make matters worse, Ricky and Eliot
had been experiencing some strange feelings

00:16:06.059 --> 00:16:11.260
for the last couple of hours, when they’d
gotten particularly deep into the forest.

00:16:11.260 --> 00:16:16.410
That dread-inducing sensation of being watched
by something lurking just out of sight. A

00:16:16.410 --> 00:16:22.730
gaze with some unknowable and darkly malicious
intent boiling behind it. These two poor saps

00:16:22.730 --> 00:16:27.949
had no idea what was in store for them tonight,
and wouldn’t you know it, the one watching

00:16:27.949 --> 00:16:32.230
them right now wouldn’t even be the only
monster after them.

00:16:32.230 --> 00:16:37.170
After an expectable amount of bumbling and
buffoonery, Ricky and Eliot managed to gather

00:16:37.170 --> 00:16:42.410
up enough tinder to make a semi-respectable
campfire. They could sleep out here tonight,

00:16:42.410 --> 00:16:48.589
then set off in search of Siren Head or civilization
- whichever came first - in the daylight the

00:16:48.589 --> 00:16:54.380
next day. The duo did what they could to remain
chipper, in spite of the ominous circumstances.

00:16:54.380 --> 00:16:58.569
Sure, they were trapped in the depths of the
woods, alone, but think of the amazing vlog

00:16:58.569 --> 00:16:59.879
footage they’d get!

00:16:59.879 --> 00:17:04.380
They’d be like the next Blair Witch crew,
except they’d live, hopefully!

00:17:04.380 --> 00:17:09.809
But as the duo sat next to their campfire,
they felt that ominous sensation again. The

00:17:09.809 --> 00:17:14.529
hairs on the back of their necks started standing
up. They felt shivers that didn’t just come

00:17:14.529 --> 00:17:19.089
from the cold. More and more, they started
to feel certain that something in this forest

00:17:19.089 --> 00:17:25.199
was watching them. Something ancient and powerful
and evil. That’s when one of them heard

00:17:25.199 --> 00:17:30.220
the telltale crackle of radio static, and
their skeletons almost jumped out of their

00:17:30.220 --> 00:17:31.220
skin.

00:17:31.220 --> 00:17:36.269
Could it be him? The Patron Saint of Going
Missing Without a Trace, of Creeping Dread,

00:17:36.269 --> 00:17:40.440
of Bad Things Coming? Siren Head himself?

00:17:40.440 --> 00:17:45.760
Both men fell silent. All they could hear
was the crackling of their own fire. Had the

00:17:45.760 --> 00:17:51.059
sound before just been an illusion? A product
of a pair of paranoid minds also predisposed

00:17:51.059 --> 00:17:55.830
to sense Siren-Headed monsters lurking amongst
the trees? They’d been reading about a higher

00:17:55.830 --> 00:18:00.529
rate of disappearances in this area, along
with a number of frightening sightings of

00:18:00.529 --> 00:18:06.200
tall, thin beings and radio static that seemed
perfectly consistent with Siren Head activity.

00:18:06.200 --> 00:18:11.470
That’s why they’d come here in the first
place. But had they come here to their deaths?

00:18:11.470 --> 00:18:16.269
The two needed to distract themselves or they’d
go mad from the paranoia. They decided to

00:18:16.269 --> 00:18:21.240
share a few fun little anecdotes of the travels
they’d had before meeting one another, showing

00:18:21.240 --> 00:18:27.049
the photos they’d saved on their smartphones.
Ricky had been to Hawaii, Tahiti, Bora Bora,

00:18:27.049 --> 00:18:33.710
Fiji, St. Lucia, and the Maldives. Clearly,
he liked his vacations warm and sandy. Eliot,

00:18:33.710 --> 00:18:38.809
on the other hand, tended to like his trips
to be a little more adventurous. He’d seen

00:18:38.809 --> 00:18:44.580
volcanoes, waterfalls, deep and treacherous
cave systems, and of course, a little guided

00:18:44.580 --> 00:18:45.580
mountaineering.

00:18:45.580 --> 00:18:50.679
One photo that Eliot showed Ricky was particularly
breathtaking: He was standing in the foreground

00:18:50.679 --> 00:18:56.240
of a snowy landscape, with an incredible mountain
range behind him. When Ricky asked Eliot where

00:18:56.240 --> 00:19:01.620
exactly he’d taken this picture, strangely
enough, he drew a complete blank. Maybe somewhere

00:19:01.620 --> 00:19:07.020
in Canada, or Siberia, or something like that…
But it was a hell of a trip, whatever the

00:19:07.020 --> 00:19:09.940
case, he remembered having a great time.

00:19:09.940 --> 00:19:14.890
It was such a tiny detail, but when looking
at the photo, Ricky couldn’t help but notice

00:19:14.890 --> 00:19:20.980
something in the background. A speck of white.
Sure, not exactly newsworthy in a snowy mountain

00:19:20.980 --> 00:19:26.690
range, but this wasn’t snow. It was a speck
of white that seemed to be a slightly different

00:19:26.690 --> 00:19:31.450
shade than the rest of the mountain. Strange.
Perhaps it was just some tiny particle stuck

00:19:31.450 --> 00:19:33.770
on the lens whenever the photo was taken.

00:19:33.770 --> 00:19:38.510
The two continued to chat and warm their hands
against the fire, having no idea of the horror

00:19:38.510 --> 00:19:40.929
they’d just awoken in doing so.

00:19:40.929 --> 00:19:46.440
Over a hundred miles away, at an SCP Foundation
containment site, researchers and guards walked

00:19:46.440 --> 00:19:51.930
around the perimeter of a huge, airtight metal
cube, reading off diagnostics and audio recordings.

00:19:51.930 --> 00:19:55.950
For reasons that should be obvious to everyone
who’s a fan of this channel, there were

00:19:55.950 --> 00:20:01.370
no camera feeds coming from the inside of
the great metal cube. Only a faint whimpering

00:20:01.370 --> 00:20:06.870
sound, like a sad, wounded animal, and the
clanking of pacing feet moving back and forth

00:20:06.870 --> 00:20:09.979
over the metal, dragging forlorn knuckles.

00:20:09.979 --> 00:20:14.409
It turned every head in the room when the
crying started to get louder. Soon, it wasn’t

00:20:14.409 --> 00:20:21.850
just quiet weeping, it was a series of horrendous,
slobbering sobs, wails of pure pain and despair.

00:20:21.850 --> 00:20:25.649
Someone in the room screamed “Close your
eyes and hit the deck!”, knowing exactly

00:20:25.649 --> 00:20:31.240
what had happened. They’d learned well enough
from the infamous Incident 096-1-A Containment

00:20:31.240 --> 00:20:35.850
Breach that there was no way of stopping this
thing until it’d run its course. The best

00:20:35.850 --> 00:20:40.299
they could do was minimize their own fatalities
in the process.

00:20:40.299 --> 00:20:44.960
Everyone in the room did as they were told,
hitting the ground, closing their eyes, and

00:20:44.960 --> 00:20:49.610
covering them with their hands, just to be
safe. That’s when the solid steel of the

00:20:49.610 --> 00:20:56.090
cube was torn open like crepe paper, and the
howling, raging SCP-096 broke free, galloping

00:20:56.090 --> 00:21:00.660
on all fours through the nearest wall, shattering
it and spraying debris everywhere.

00:21:00.660 --> 00:21:05.930
As Mobile Task Forces were dispatched to track
the rampaging abomination and try their best

00:21:05.930 --> 00:21:10.059
to clear the path ahead as it sought out its
target, one thought was going through the

00:21:10.059 --> 00:21:16.179
minds of everyone in the room: “Woe betide
the poor fools who saw that monster’s face…”

00:21:16.179 --> 00:21:20.720
Back in the deep, dark forests of the Pacific
Northwest, the presence of some unnerving

00:21:20.720 --> 00:21:25.299
force was becoming far harder to deny for
Ricky and Eliot. They heard the sounds of

00:21:25.299 --> 00:21:31.120
garbled voices out in the woods. The squeals
and crackles of radio static. They saw shapes

00:21:31.120 --> 00:21:36.990
moving amongst the trees, tall and thin, like
long, spindly limbs. While neither wanted

00:21:36.990 --> 00:21:42.570
to voice their fear, they were both thinking
similar things: If there really was a monster

00:21:42.570 --> 00:21:47.420
out here, why hadn’t it killed them? It
had all the time in the world to do it.

00:21:47.420 --> 00:21:53.159
But that was just it, wasn’t it? It had
all the time in the world, so why rush? It

00:21:53.159 --> 00:21:59.450
had every advantage. It could take its time,
and do this exactly how it wanted to. Ricky

00:21:59.450 --> 00:22:04.279
and Eliot had made themselves the perfect
prey. And as they saw a huge, lithe figure

00:22:04.279 --> 00:22:08.620
suddenly separate itself from the trees, they
began to wonder if it was really worth it

00:22:08.620 --> 00:22:14.850
to risk both of their lives for YouTube clout.
Either way, it was now probably too late for

00:22:14.850 --> 00:22:15.850
regrets.

00:22:15.850 --> 00:22:20.030
Ricky and Eliot’s jaws fell in horror as
the monster emerged from the darkness before

00:22:20.030 --> 00:22:24.840
them. They recognized the awful, mottled skin
stretched over the monster’s forty-foot

00:22:24.840 --> 00:22:30.321
frame. They saw the grasping hands, and up
above, the nightmarish, faceless head, split

00:22:30.321 --> 00:22:35.760
into a pair of sirens pointing in different
directions, each one filled with gnashing

00:22:35.760 --> 00:22:41.500
teeth and slithering tongues. It was even
more terrifying than they possibly could have

00:22:41.500 --> 00:22:42.500
imagined.

00:22:42.500 --> 00:22:50.830
Siren Head’s speakers crackled into life,
and hissed out, “NINE. EIGHTEEN. ONE. CHILD.

00:22:50.830 --> 00:22:53.559
SEVENTEEN. REMOVE. VILE.”

00:22:53.559 --> 00:22:58.330
Ricky and Eliot stumbled backwards as Siren
Head advanced, its speakers still bleeding

00:22:58.330 --> 00:23:04.210
hateful static. Neither could move or speak,
only watch as its long, gnarled fingers reached

00:23:04.210 --> 00:23:06.100
from the dark to grasp them.

00:23:06.100 --> 00:23:11.220
There was a terrible pained wail in the woods
after that. But it wasn’t coming from Ricky,

00:23:11.220 --> 00:23:18.019
Eliot, or Siren Head. It was distant at first,
but soon became louder and louder as the force’s

00:23:18.019 --> 00:23:20.590
point of origin came barreling towards them.

00:23:20.590 --> 00:23:26.830
Even Siren Head couldn’t help but turn to
see SCP-096, a flash of terrible white fury,

00:23:26.830 --> 00:23:32.210
come charging towards them in the dark, knowing
down all the trees in its path. Leaves and

00:23:32.210 --> 00:23:37.710
pine needles scattered. Trunks exploded into
splinters. Nothing would stop the Shy Guy

00:23:37.710 --> 00:23:43.960
from claiming its prey. Nothing would stop
it from destroying Ricky, and now, as it approached,

00:23:43.960 --> 00:23:47.270
Eliot made the fatal mistake of looking at
its face, too.

00:23:47.270 --> 00:23:52.080
096 lunged, ready to go in for the kill, when
Siren Head’s hands closed around the Shy

00:23:52.080 --> 00:23:57.210
Guy’s slender torso. In one fluid motion,
with surprising strength for its scrawny build,

00:23:57.210 --> 00:24:02.730
it hurled the Shy Guy into the distance, smashing
another several trees in its path.

00:24:02.730 --> 00:24:10.929
The words, “MY PREY. MY PREY. MINE. MINE.
MINE,” hissed out of its speakers.

00:24:10.929 --> 00:24:17.159
But 096 was only down for mere moments. It
sprung back up on its hind and forelegs and

00:24:17.159 --> 00:24:22.429
started galloping towards Ricky and Eliot
again, who were still frozen in place. This

00:24:22.429 --> 00:24:26.590
time, just before the Shy Guy’s terrible
claws could close around the two hapless wannabe

00:24:26.590 --> 00:24:32.460
YouTubers, down came Siren Head’s clenched
fist, pounding 096’s head into the dirt.

00:24:32.460 --> 00:24:36.570
Overhead, Mobile Task Force helicopters circled,
wearing their SCRAMBLE goggles and trying

00:24:36.570 --> 00:24:40.860
to get a visual on the situation unfolding
below. They could hardly believe what they

00:24:40.860 --> 00:24:46.620
were seeing down there. Was some other unclassified
anomaly managing to intercept SCP-096 in its

00:24:46.620 --> 00:24:51.679
rage state? Only a handful of anomalies on
record had ever been shown as being capable

00:24:51.679 --> 00:24:56.440
of such a feat. It seemed they were dealing
with something serious here.

00:24:56.440 --> 00:25:01.570
Down below, the battle continued. Siren Head
had a unique advantage over most other combatants

00:25:01.570 --> 00:25:09.059
facing SCP-096: It was an extradimensional
being with no eyes, merely an innate psychic

00:25:09.059 --> 00:25:15.070
awareness of its victims around it - not unlike
how SCP-096 detects people seeing its face

00:25:15.070 --> 00:25:20.429
in photographs or videos without need for
any kind of direct contact. As such, it was

00:25:20.429 --> 00:25:25.480
impossible for Siren Head to see the Shy Guy’s
face, and thus, impossible for the Shy Guy

00:25:25.480 --> 00:25:28.450
to ever actually direct its aggression against
it.

00:25:28.450 --> 00:25:33.090
Whether or not 096 would kill-steal Siren
Head’s intended prey was another matter

00:25:33.090 --> 00:25:34.090
entirely.

00:25:34.090 --> 00:25:38.419
In the night’s first minor miracle, Ricky
and Eliot remembered that they’d been born

00:25:38.419 --> 00:25:42.850
with working legs, and decided that right
now would be an excellent time to use them.

00:25:42.850 --> 00:25:48.170
They turned and began to run while Siren Head
continued beating the Shy Guy into the dirt,

00:25:48.170 --> 00:25:52.230
sprinting with all their might. However, the
growing distance between them only aggravated

00:25:52.230 --> 00:25:53.230
the Shy Guy further.

00:25:53.230 --> 00:25:58.940
It sprang out from the dirt with unstoppable
force and broke away from Siren Head’s grip.

00:25:58.940 --> 00:26:04.090
The Shy Guy never wasted time, ever, it always
made a beeline for its intended victim with

00:26:04.090 --> 00:26:10.919
one directive in mind: Total annihilation.
Tonight is no different. Ricky and Eliot screamed

00:26:10.919 --> 00:26:13.899
as they heard its pounding gallop on the ground
behind them.

00:26:13.899 --> 00:26:16.169
Closer, closer, closer…

00:26:16.169 --> 00:26:21.559
Then suddenly, defying all spatial reasoning,
Siren Head emerged out of the darkness between

00:26:21.559 --> 00:26:27.570
Ricky, Eliot, and 096. Being forty feet tall,
Siren Head dwarfed even the Shy Guy. It lunged

00:26:27.570 --> 00:26:32.090
and grabbed the wailing white monstrosity,
holding it as one would a particulary vicious

00:26:32.090 --> 00:26:36.950
cat and trying to keep it in place. Siren
Head wanted to crush this monster’s bones

00:26:36.950 --> 00:26:41.480
and carry on stalking its feeble human prey
- who were currently in the process of getting

00:26:41.480 --> 00:26:45.090
away - but somehow, that was impossible.

00:26:45.090 --> 00:26:48.420
It was almost like the monster had an indestructible
skeleton…

00:26:48.420 --> 00:26:53.049
Up above, the Mobile Task Forces continued
observing this clash of the titans from their

00:26:53.049 --> 00:26:58.190
two helicopters. Seeing how Siren Head was
somehow doing a serviceable job of subduing

00:26:58.190 --> 00:27:03.220
the Shy Guy, one of the task force members
joked, “Maybe we should hire this guy.”

00:27:03.220 --> 00:27:07.350
Another chimed in, “I don’t think we have
any ballistic vests in his size.”

00:27:07.350 --> 00:27:11.759
The laughter soon turned to screams when Siren
Head hurled SCP-096’s flailing body up at

00:27:11.759 --> 00:27:16.840
one of the helicopters with incredible force,
knocking it out of the sky. It careened down

00:27:16.840 --> 00:27:21.799
to the ground, a ball of fear and violence,
before exploding. Anyone who survived the

00:27:21.799 --> 00:27:26.960
initial blast was unlucky enough to have seen
the Shy Guy’s face. Their screams rang out,

00:27:26.960 --> 00:27:32.390
briefly, before being abruptly silenced by
the monster. Helicopter Alpha had no survivors.

00:27:32.390 --> 00:27:36.269
Meanwhile, Ricky and Eliot had kept running
until their lungs burned and their muscles

00:27:36.269 --> 00:27:41.220
pumped thumbtacks. Seeing that the chaos was
so far behind them now, they took a moment

00:27:41.220 --> 00:27:46.130
to stop and breathe, recomposing themselves.
In the second minor miracle of the night,

00:27:46.130 --> 00:27:51.309
it looked like the two of them had actually
survived this ordeal. Those two horrific monsters

00:27:51.309 --> 00:27:53.139
had canceled each other out.

00:27:53.139 --> 00:27:57.889
But Ricky and Eliot were about to learn a
harsh lesson: They really weren’t that lucky.

00:27:57.889 --> 00:28:02.230
The last thing the two ever saw was Siren
Head emerging from the darkness once more.

00:28:02.230 --> 00:28:06.149
The last thing they ever felt was the monster’s
terrible, gnarled fingers curling around their

00:28:06.149 --> 00:28:11.870
bodies, too fast for them to escape. And the
last thing they ever heard was each other’s

00:28:11.870 --> 00:28:15.460
screams, followed by a sickeningly meaty crunch.

00:28:15.460 --> 00:28:21.260
Elsewhere in the forest, SCP-096’s wails
died down to its usual maudlin sob. The rage

00:28:21.260 --> 00:28:25.710
state had ended; all of its targets had been
destroyed. It just hadn’t been the one to

00:28:25.710 --> 00:28:28.340
do all of the destroying, per se.

00:28:28.340 --> 00:28:33.040
Not long after that, Helicopter Bravo descended
safely and the remaining MTF members closed

00:28:33.040 --> 00:28:37.220
around the blubbering beast. Someone black
bagged it and everyone breathed a sigh of

00:28:37.220 --> 00:28:40.720
relief, knowing that was at least one monster
taken care of tonight.

00:28:40.720 --> 00:28:43.750
“What about the other one?” the MTF Lieutenant
asked.

00:28:43.750 --> 00:28:47.730
The team’s commander shook his head and
said, “Let’s worry about that freak later

00:28:47.730 --> 00:28:52.240
and get 096 back to containment. This place
gives me the creeps…”

00:28:52.240 --> 00:28:59.230
This video is about two things: The first
is SCP – 096, also known as The Shy Guy,

00:28:59.230 --> 00:29:04.350
a horrific monster who murders anyone and
anything unfortunate enough to look at its

00:29:04.350 --> 00:29:10.840
face, directly or through photos or videos.
The second is you – because we asked you

00:29:10.840 --> 00:29:17.211
for your favourite questions, theories, and
hypothetical situations involving 096. We

00:29:17.211 --> 00:29:23.009
even asked you if you thought someone would
be safe from 096 on the moon, and you delivered.

00:29:23.009 --> 00:29:27.279
If you haven’t watched our first video on
the infamous Shy Guy, we recommend checking

00:29:27.279 --> 00:29:31.629
that out first, but feel free to stick around
even if you haven’t.

00:29:31.629 --> 00:29:36.460
First of all, let’s take a look at the questions
you had about the SCP Foundation’s most

00:29:36.460 --> 00:29:40.549
dangerous introvert, and see if we can find
some answers.

00:29:40.549 --> 00:29:45.540
“How close would you have to be to trigger
it? Like, if it was 5km away, would it still

00:29:45.540 --> 00:29:48.429
count?”
The short answer is that 096’s homicidal

00:29:48.429 --> 00:29:52.930
rage can be triggered from pretty much any
distance. We’ll get into some of the potentially

00:29:52.930 --> 00:29:57.680
crazy extents of this later on, but if you
see the Shy Guy’s face while you’re sharing

00:29:57.680 --> 00:30:02.480
a dimension with him, you’re in serious
trouble. People who’ve viewed the creature’s

00:30:02.480 --> 00:30:08.570
face in different countries, or even miles
underwater have met gruesome ends 5 kilometers

00:30:08.570 --> 00:30:13.260
would definitely not keep you safe.
“What were his origins and how strong is

00:30:13.260 --> 00:30:19.340
he compared to SCP-682?”
The origins of SCP – 096 are still shrouded

00:30:19.340 --> 00:30:24.570
in mystery – all we know for sure is that
he was discovered by the Foundation in a snowy,

00:30:24.570 --> 00:30:30.330
mountainous region. As for physical durability,
he’s about even with 682 as both survived

00:30:30.330 --> 00:30:36.779
their encounter. We know 682 has the psychological
advantage though, as 096 has been terrified

00:30:36.779 --> 00:30:42.779
of the lizard ever since they met in cross
testing. However, you could argue that 096

00:30:42.779 --> 00:30:49.549
is technically more dangerous, since unlike
682, 096 has never been pacified before killing

00:30:49.549 --> 00:30:54.631
its intended target while on a rampage.
“If he goes into an "unstoppable attack

00:30:54.631 --> 00:31:00.280
mode" when someone sees his face, shouldn’t
you NOT show his face in the thumbnail?

00:31:00.280 --> 00:31:04.830
Thankfully, according to the official documents,
artistic representations, like paintings or

00:31:04.830 --> 00:31:10.419
digitally-drawn YouTube thumbnails have no
effect. Only actual photographs or videos

00:31:10.419 --> 00:31:16.110
of the creature can be deadly, so you’re
safe – unless we decide to do an 096 Face

00:31:16.110 --> 00:31:22.340
Reveal to celebrate our next 100,000 subscribers,
of course. This leads into our next question.

00:31:22.340 --> 00:31:26.370
“If it attacks you when you look at it,
then how did the scientist draw the picture

00:31:26.370 --> 00:31:30.159
of the thing without looking at it?”
That’s a good question, and also one that

00:31:30.159 --> 00:31:35.049
occurred to the Foundation scientists, who
came up with an extremely elaborate solution.

00:31:35.049 --> 00:31:40.370
They put a D-Class tattoo artist inside a
diving bell miles underwater, and had him

00:31:40.370 --> 00:31:45.419
unseal a photograph of the creature before
drawing a copy. This copy was then released

00:31:45.419 --> 00:31:51.140
from the diving bell in a sealed container,
before 096 inevitably got to the poor D-Class.

00:31:51.140 --> 00:31:56.830
“Does the shy guy have to be viewed through
a good quality image to enter a rage state?”

00:31:56.830 --> 00:32:03.059
Unfortunately no, even the most poorly rendered
or miniscule image of 096’s face is a death

00:32:03.059 --> 00:32:08.320
sentence. One of the most infamous incidents
resulted from a man seeing a small dot that

00:32:08.320 --> 00:32:15.009
was only a few pixels wide in a photo from
an old ski trip. This dot was 096, taken from

00:32:15.009 --> 00:32:21.139
a mile away. No true image of 096 is safe,
regardless of size.

00:32:21.139 --> 00:32:27.009
“In some canons, despite 096 being referred
to as indestructible, the Foundation managed

00:32:27.009 --> 00:32:31.230
to terminate it after many attempts and even
more bureaucracy. How did they do it?”

00:32:31.230 --> 00:32:37.180
While a lot of 096’s body has been blown
away by conventional arms fire, its hyper-tough

00:32:37.180 --> 00:32:43.570
skeleton is what has given it such an indestructible
reputation. In one tale though, Foundation

00:32:43.570 --> 00:32:47.909
researchers actually used this to their advantage,
and employed the use of the neck-snapping

00:32:47.909 --> 00:32:55.820
killer sculpture, SCP-173. After using 173
to damage 096’s bones, powerful acid was

00:32:55.820 --> 00:33:01.169
injected into the skeleton, destroying it
from within. Though of course, this isn’t

00:33:01.169 --> 00:33:06.399
the official ending of the 096 story, because
the SCP Foundation doesn’t have a single

00:33:06.399 --> 00:33:10.549
unifying canon.
That’s enough questions. Now, it’s time

00:33:10.549 --> 00:33:16.009
for you to school us – let’s look at some
of your favorite theories about the Shy Guy.

00:33:16.009 --> 00:33:19.420
Starting with…
“I sometimes like to imagine that 096 is

00:33:19.420 --> 00:33:23.090
nice, but he can’t control when someone
sees him and after he kills the person he

00:33:23.090 --> 00:33:26.029
starts to cry because he didn’t want to
kill them.”

00:33:26.029 --> 00:33:32.029
There is actually some evidence to suggest
that SCP – 096 might not want to kill. A

00:33:32.029 --> 00:33:38.340
great example is its cross test with SCP – 978,
a camera which reveals the deepest desires

00:33:38.340 --> 00:33:44.679
of whoever or whatever is in its photos. The
photo it took of 096 showed that it had completely

00:33:44.679 --> 00:33:50.070
disappeared from the photo – showing its
deepest desire was just to be invisible and

00:33:50.070 --> 00:33:54.480
unseen. Maybe it wants to disappear because
it doesn’t want to hurt anybody?

00:33:54.480 --> 00:33:59.750
“Regarding the Moon question, I think SCP-096
would probably stay in that hostile state

00:33:59.750 --> 00:34:03.850
until you came back, at which point it would
promptly rip you to shreds and do whatever

00:34:03.850 --> 00:34:09.750
it does to your mutilated corpse”
Based on what we know about prior 096 incidents,

00:34:09.750 --> 00:34:15.139
this feels incredibly likely. It’s also
possible that it might enter its docile state,

00:34:15.139 --> 00:34:20.000
before becoming aggressive again when you
return to its murder range. You really don’t

00:34:20.000 --> 00:34:24.520
want to find out, either way.
“He can jump to the moon easy, he does squats

00:34:24.520 --> 00:34:27.870
in containment all the time.”
While its scrawny physique doesn’t make

00:34:27.870 --> 00:34:34.740
it seem like 096 understands the concept of
exercise, there aren’t any cameras in 096’s

00:34:34.740 --> 00:34:39.260
cell – so technically, we can’t prove
it isn’t doing squats to pass the time in

00:34:39.260 --> 00:34:41.359
there.
“I really doubt a blind person would be

00:34:41.359 --> 00:34:46.360
safe from his effect, since 096 has the IQ
of a new born baby, I think he will count

00:34:46.360 --> 00:34:50.481
it as looking at him”
This is an interesting theory. However, seeing

00:34:50.481 --> 00:34:56.149
as 096 is able to sense when people see its
face from entire continents away, the way

00:34:56.149 --> 00:35:01.359
it knows if you can see its face might be
a little more psychic in nature. Therefore,

00:35:01.359 --> 00:35:05.910
it’s probable that it wouldn’t mistakenly
kill someone who hasn’t actually seen its

00:35:05.910 --> 00:35:10.880
face – such as a person who is blind.
“To deal with him in my opinion… simply

00:35:10.880 --> 00:35:15.599
freeze him. Either in space or the mountains.
His body shuts down and when looked at it

00:35:15.599 --> 00:35:21.450
will not react correctly. It is simply asleep.”
While we don’t know how SCP – 096 would

00:35:21.450 --> 00:35:26.990
react to a zero-g environment, for a creature
that’s always nude, it seems extremely tolerant

00:35:26.990 --> 00:35:32.141
to the cold. When it was first found, it was
lurking in icy mountains and a Foundation

00:35:32.141 --> 00:35:38.150
agent commented on the fact it wasn’t even
shivering, despite the incredibly low temperatures.

00:35:38.150 --> 00:35:42.620
But who knows if there’s a temperature out
there that’s too low even for the Shy Guy?

00:35:42.620 --> 00:35:46.500
“If someone looks at it in China and it’s
the US, then you get on its back, you’ll

00:35:46.500 --> 00:35:50.119
get a free piggy back ride to China. It’s
a free trip to China, and all it costs is

00:35:50.119 --> 00:35:54.140
a human life. That’s my theory”
Technically, it’d be at the cost of two

00:35:54.140 --> 00:35:59.960
human lives, unless you wore a diving suit
with plenty of Oxygen. 096 has proven to be

00:35:59.960 --> 00:36:04.690
an extremely proficient swimmer, but you’d
have to maintain your grip across the entire

00:36:04.690 --> 00:36:10.100
Pacific Ocean while it journeys to China.
In other words, it’s technically possible,

00:36:10.100 --> 00:36:15.350
but we here at SCP Explained would rather
pay for the roundtrip flight. To each their

00:36:15.350 --> 00:36:18.270
own though.
But that’s enough theories for now. Let’s

00:36:18.270 --> 00:36:24.240
move on to the main event: Hypotheticals.
You posed some great possible scenarios involving

00:36:24.240 --> 00:36:29.030
SCP – 096, so we’re going to see if we
can find the answers.

00:36:29.030 --> 00:36:33.190
“What would happen if someone looked at
a picture of it while in or before entering

00:36:33.190 --> 00:36:36.589
another SCP’s pocket dimension or mirror
dimension?”

00:36:36.589 --> 00:36:41.680
There aren’t any canon examples to prove
either way, but if there’s any kind of consistent

00:36:41.680 --> 00:36:47.260
entry point into this dimension, it’s likely
that 096 would find a way to follow you in.

00:36:47.260 --> 00:36:53.380
If not, it’s very possible that it may just
wait for you to eventually return, before

00:36:53.380 --> 00:36:58.770
bringing the metaphorical hammer down on you.
So technically, if you were pulled into the

00:36:58.770 --> 00:37:03.550
Old Man’s pocket dimension and he closed
the portal, you’d be safe from 096, but

00:37:03.550 --> 00:37:06.930
then you’ve got a whole other problem to
deal with.

00:37:06.930 --> 00:37:07.930
[ANIMATION NOTE: Hypothetical proposed by
“😊”]

00:37:07.930 --> 00:37:12.380
“What happens if a person sees his face
then gets their memory of the creature wiped?”

00:37:12.380 --> 00:37:16.230
Considering how good the Foundation seems
to be at wiping people’s memories and their

00:37:16.230 --> 00:37:21.460
access to amnestics, if this worked, they
probably would have made it standard procedure

00:37:21.460 --> 00:37:25.569
by now – so it’s unlikely.
“What happens if a person looked at it and

00:37:25.569 --> 00:37:30.430
the person then went on a plane? Or just staying
in the air or space what would it do?”

00:37:30.430 --> 00:37:36.089
096 has shown the ability to jump up and destroy
low-flying aircraft, but otherwise, it’s

00:37:36.089 --> 00:37:40.520
likely that the creature would probably run
to a point directly below you and remain there

00:37:40.520 --> 00:37:45.270
until you touched back down. Since you’ll
need food and water at some point and aircrafts

00:37:45.270 --> 00:37:49.079
need fuel, you’d probably just be prolonging
the inevitable.

00:37:49.079 --> 00:37:53.859
“What if the young girl was around it so
it tried to attack her but died trying?”

00:37:53.859 --> 00:38:01.100
This is another interesting possibility. SCP-053
is a girl capable of causing homicidal urges,

00:38:01.100 --> 00:38:05.530
but causes fatal heart attacks in those who
attack her. While we can’t know for sure

00:38:05.530 --> 00:38:12.930
how a meeting between 096 and 053 would go,
we can use her encounter with 682 as a potential

00:38:12.930 --> 00:38:18.180
template. Aggressive SCPs like the hard to
kill reptile, perhaps having an innate knowledge

00:38:18.180 --> 00:38:24.440
of the danger that comes from attacking 053,
tend to become strangely docile around her.

00:38:24.440 --> 00:38:30.619
It’s possible that 096 would do the same.
“Would we be able to control SCP-096 with

00:38:30.619 --> 00:38:38.430
the help of SCP-035?”
The Possessive Mask, aka SCP – 035, is undeniably

00:38:38.430 --> 00:38:44.859
a lot more intelligent than 096, and has even
been shown to respond to reason on occasion.

00:38:44.859 --> 00:38:50.309
It’s also been able to control almost every
other entity it’s been placed on. The problem

00:38:50.309 --> 00:38:56.630
is, if the Mask was placed on 096, and it
was somehow effective, the result may be even

00:38:56.630 --> 00:39:02.559
more dangerous than 096 – An intelligent
monster with an indestructible and highly

00:39:02.559 --> 00:39:08.950
dangerous body. There’s no telling whether
035 would slowly break down 096 into sludge

00:39:08.950 --> 00:39:16.190
the way it does to humans, in which case we
could be left with an immortal 096 - 035 combo.

00:39:16.190 --> 00:39:21.730
So maybe it’s best that we never find out.
“If someone were to be in the infinite Ikea,

00:39:21.730 --> 00:39:25.530
and looked at a picture of its face, could
it be finally contained, and if someone else

00:39:25.530 --> 00:39:30.730
looked at a picture of it outside of the Infinite
Ikea, could it escape and kill them, if so,

00:39:30.730 --> 00:39:35.520
how long would it take it?”
This is a great question, and while the infinite

00:39:35.520 --> 00:39:42.119
ikea is a terrifying maze, SCP -096 has an
advantage here that we don’t: Not only can

00:39:42.119 --> 00:39:46.980
it rip through anything in the way with its
super strength, it has an innate sense of

00:39:46.980 --> 00:39:54.490
where its victim is. Therefore, regardless
of whether you’re inside and out, SCP -096

00:39:54.490 --> 00:39:58.980
will still come straight to you to exact your
horrible fate.

00:39:58.980 --> 00:40:02.490
But at least you can enjoy the meatballs while
you wait, right?

00:40:02.490 --> 00:40:05.890
And finally:
“What would happen if 096 were led into

00:40:05.890 --> 00:40:10.920
a room full of mirrors and allowed or made
to look at its own face? Would it smash the

00:40:10.920 --> 00:40:17.059
mirrors or try to rip itself to pieces?”
This is still a hotly debated issue to this

00:40:17.059 --> 00:40:21.520
day, which – thanks to the “no canon”
nature of the SCP Foundation – doesn’t

00:40:21.520 --> 00:40:26.859
have a definitive answer. Some theorize that
this would at least be an effective deterrent

00:40:26.859 --> 00:40:32.460
against SCP – 096, while others suggest
that it wouldn’t be effective, because many

00:40:32.460 --> 00:40:38.869
believe that 096 is actually blind and thus
cannot see its own face. It can only sense

00:40:38.869 --> 00:40:44.260
when other people see it. Again, we don’t
quite know for sure, but it’s likely to

00:40:44.260 --> 00:40:50.390
remain one of the most contentious issues
around the Shy Guy for a long time to come.

00:40:50.390 --> 00:40:57.430
What does a monster look like to you? What
figures slither and claw their way into your

00:40:57.430 --> 00:41:04.109
nightmares, chasing you down endless halls
and stalking you through the dark until you

00:41:04.109 --> 00:41:09.040
wake up screaming?
Maybe you imagine something tall and lean,

00:41:09.040 --> 00:41:16.170
bony arms reaching for you from atop impossibly
long, slender legs, its featureless face showing

00:41:16.170 --> 00:41:22.950
no mercy. Maybe you think of a man in a striped
sweater with knives for fingers, or a serial

00:41:22.950 --> 00:41:30.170
killer in a hockey mask, wielding a machete.
Maybe it’s something more inhuman, a cosmic

00:41:30.170 --> 00:41:35.910
horror of tentacles and eyes that can see
into your very soul. You probably don’t

00:41:35.910 --> 00:41:41.700
think of something with no arms, no legs,
no body at all. Just a face.

00:41:41.700 --> 00:41:46.541
What’s so scary about that?
A face can’t run after you, can’t grab

00:41:46.541 --> 00:41:53.309
you by the ankles and pull you under the bed.
A face can only look. It may be unsettling

00:41:53.309 --> 00:41:58.690
to behold, it might send a chill down your
spine, but the worst it can do is make you

00:41:58.690 --> 00:42:04.060
a little uncomfortable. And if you can’t
stand it any longer, you could always just

00:42:04.060 --> 00:42:08.250
close your eyes, or walk away and be done
with it.

00:42:08.250 --> 00:42:11.519
Right?
If that’s what you think, if you don’t

00:42:11.519 --> 00:42:15.860
believe in monsters that can hurt you without
lifting a finger, then you’re the type to

00:42:15.860 --> 00:42:22.000
fall victim to a very special, very intelligent
mask.

00:42:22.000 --> 00:42:28.180
In the hallowed halls of the SCP Foundation,
there is a containment cell outfitted with

00:42:28.180 --> 00:42:34.951
a hermetically sealed glass case, surrounded
by steel, iron, and lead. There are guards

00:42:34.951 --> 00:42:41.430
posted outside, along with a trained psychologist.
If you are ever brave, and foolish, enough

00:42:41.430 --> 00:42:47.790
to enter that room, you’ll see a simple
white porcelain comedy mask, with a peculiar

00:42:47.790 --> 00:42:51.170
black substance leaking from its eyes and
mouth.

00:42:51.170 --> 00:42:57.650
Wherever this slime touches, surfaces begin
to corrode, to rot away into puddles of black

00:42:57.650 --> 00:43:03.260
liquid. You might notice the same liquid trickling
slowly down the walls of the room, corrupting

00:43:03.260 --> 00:43:09.390
everything in its path. As unsettling of a
sight as it is, if you approach the mask to

00:43:09.390 --> 00:43:15.490
take a closer look, you will find yourself
overcome by an intense, nearly irresistible

00:43:15.490 --> 00:43:22.170
urge to pick it up, and put it on. To wear
it, to let it consume you from the inside

00:43:22.170 --> 00:43:28.619
out and puppet your body while your brain
simply…turns off.

00:43:28.619 --> 00:43:35.670
Like an extinguished flame, you’ll simply
be gone. Then, who knows what the mask will

00:43:35.670 --> 00:43:39.349
do? It won’t be your concern anymore, that’s
for sure.

00:43:39.349 --> 00:43:44.220
But thankfully, you haven’t gone into the
room with the porcelain mask. You haven’t

00:43:44.220 --> 00:43:49.950
let it cast its spell on you. Not yet, at
least. It’s waiting for you there, in the

00:43:49.950 --> 00:43:57.350
room with black slime oozing down its walls.
And it will wait patiently, for as long as

00:43:57.350 --> 00:44:01.960
it needs to.
After all, what’s a mask without a face

00:44:01.960 --> 00:44:07.310
to wear it?
SCP-035, better known as the Possessive Mask,

00:44:07.310 --> 00:44:13.930
sat in its containment cell, immobile as always.
It didn’t really have much of a choice in

00:44:13.930 --> 00:44:19.910
the matter. The Foundation had chosen, selfishly,
to revoke its host privileges. Once upon a

00:44:19.910 --> 00:44:25.059
time, they had offered it bodies to choose
from, mannequins, dummies, and wooden dolls

00:44:25.059 --> 00:44:30.690
it could adorn and pilot. They didn’t last
as long as an organic, living host, of course.

00:44:30.690 --> 00:44:44.240
But it was something. It was a taste of freedom.
This dreadful new situation was almost enough

00:44:44.240 --> 00:44:50.390
to make the mask want to change its expression
from comedy to tragedy, but it was determined

00:44:50.390 --> 00:44:56.059
to still find something to laugh about.
Even without a body, trapped in this infernal

00:44:56.059 --> 00:45:02.589
box, there had been some delicious opportunities
for entertainment. Human minds were fragile

00:45:02.589 --> 00:45:09.290
things. The mask had learned this over the
infinite years of its life, if one could call

00:45:09.290 --> 00:45:15.380
it a life. Apply the right kind of pressure
to the right weak points, whisper an enticing

00:45:15.380 --> 00:45:21.670
word or two, find the right emotional wound
to sprinkle a pinch of salt into, and humans

00:45:21.670 --> 00:45:25.470
would buckle completely in almost no time
at all.

00:45:25.470 --> 00:45:31.760
It had tried all sorts of methods since being
confined to this boring little box. Sometimes

00:45:31.760 --> 00:45:37.400
it would charm someone, pour honeyed flattery
into their heads until the person felt like

00:45:37.400 --> 00:45:43.430
the mask was a dear friend, a confidante.
Once suitable trust had been built up, the

00:45:43.430 --> 00:45:48.589
mask could persuade the person to bring it
a host, or perhaps even offer up themselves

00:45:48.589 --> 00:45:54.869
in sacrifice. If flattery didn’t work, there
were other potent approaches to take.

00:45:54.869 --> 00:46:00.540
For a being without ears, the mask was a good
listener. It picked up on things that no human

00:46:00.540 --> 00:46:07.079
ever could, the darkest secrets buried in
a person’s mind. If it caught wind of something

00:46:07.079 --> 00:46:14.290
especially juicy and ruinous, it could leverage
that. Threaten to expose an affair, a crime,

00:46:14.290 --> 00:46:22.940
or perhaps something even worse. Something
unspeakable. If praise failed, and so did

00:46:22.940 --> 00:46:29.020
blackmail, there was always good old supernatural
intimidation. All the mask needed was for

00:46:29.020 --> 00:46:34.640
someone to be left alone with it for a long
enough period of time. Then, its invisible

00:46:34.640 --> 00:46:42.420
tendrils could snake out into their defenseless
mind, weaving and poking around, leaving a

00:46:42.420 --> 00:46:49.900
lingering sense of cold, dread, of incomprehensible
whispers in long-dead tongues.

00:46:49.900 --> 00:46:56.170
What a delight containment had been in the
early days, when the Foundation had not yet

00:46:56.170 --> 00:47:01.359
figured out the mask’s true capabilities.
When they would leave security personnel with

00:47:01.359 --> 00:47:07.400
weak wills and minimal training standing guard
in the mask’s field of influence for hours

00:47:07.400 --> 00:47:13.040
at a time, as the entity played with their
thoughts and chipped away at their free will.

00:47:13.040 --> 00:47:16.740
It had almost escaped more than once, thanks
to the helpers it had been able to mold out

00:47:16.740 --> 00:47:23.700
of those hapless victims. They had been there
to break open its case and carry it to freedom.

00:47:23.700 --> 00:47:29.799
But every time, the other guards thwarted
the attempts, shooting its helpers and rendering

00:47:29.799 --> 00:47:35.369
them utterly useless.
Then, the Foundation increased its security.

00:47:35.369 --> 00:47:39.180
Something about the unacceptably high suicide
and homicide rates among staff assigned to

00:47:39.180 --> 00:47:49.049
SCP-035. How utterly boring. How truly…pathetic.
Still, the mask had found ways to occupy itself

00:47:49.049 --> 00:47:54.809
even without any more playthings. It had grown
stronger with its boredom, stretching its

00:47:54.809 --> 00:48:02.780
influence beyond organic beings and into the
very room itself, its evil enriching and deepening

00:48:02.780 --> 00:48:09.809
like a fine wine in the depths of a cold cellar.
Over the months, the walls of SCP-035’s

00:48:09.809 --> 00:48:15.380
containment cell had begun to secrete the
same black, slimy substance that would pour

00:48:15.380 --> 00:48:21.960
from the mask’s eyes and mouth. The liquid
dripped down the walls in deliberate formations,

00:48:21.960 --> 00:48:28.330
patterns that became increasingly easy to
recognize. Phrases in Italian, Latin, Ancient

00:48:28.330 --> 00:48:35.039
Greek, all detailing ritual sacrifices and
mutilations. The mask took time to describe

00:48:35.039 --> 00:48:42.270
the sacrificial victims in great detail, borrowing
identifying traits from various staff members

00:48:42.270 --> 00:48:47.740
that it knew would read the translations.
The walls were slick with blood and harrowing

00:48:47.740 --> 00:48:55.329
imagery, and the glass case around the mask
was growing more and more fragile by the day.

00:48:55.329 --> 00:48:59.890
Anyone within 10 meters of the mask could
feel this strength, too. They would leave

00:48:59.890 --> 00:49:06.869
the area complaining of unintelligible whispers,
of loud, cruel laughter, and a lingering sense

00:49:06.869 --> 00:49:14.369
of nearly insurmountable despair. It was as
if they knew, on some level, that no one was

00:49:14.369 --> 00:49:18.400
truly safe.
Eventually, the mask would find a way to come

00:49:18.400 --> 00:49:24.130
for them all. The glass was weakening, and
soon the mere thought of escape would make

00:49:24.130 --> 00:49:32.099
it shatter into pieces. Then, perhaps, the
mask could finally get its deepest desire:

00:49:32.099 --> 00:49:38.770
revenge. It wanted nothing more than to make
the Foundation pay for imprisoning it, for

00:49:38.770 --> 00:49:45.039
taking away its host privileges, for trying
harder and harder to contain the kind of power

00:49:45.039 --> 00:49:51.180
that should have had them falling to their
knees in worship. The mask seethed with hatred

00:49:51.180 --> 00:49:59.690
day in and day out. It had seen the crumbling
of the Roman Empire, the beheading of kings,

00:49:59.690 --> 00:50:07.160
the decimation of armies. It was not going
to be captured by a bunch of rats in lab coats

00:50:07.160 --> 00:50:13.270
without dire consequences befalling them.
Maybe it couldn’t move from its prison cell

00:50:13.270 --> 00:50:19.339
at the moment, but it also knew that it was
surrounded on all sides by dangerous beasts

00:50:19.339 --> 00:50:25.670
capable of reducing the site, and all who
had dared to oppose the mask, to a pile of

00:50:25.670 --> 00:50:31.700
smoldering rubble. If it could only find its
way onto one of their faces…it would show

00:50:31.700 --> 00:50:37.400
them all just what it was capable of. As the
piercing sound of an alarm echoed down the

00:50:37.400 --> 00:50:43.170
hall, the sound of screams and chaos following
shortly after, the mask’s frowning face

00:50:43.170 --> 00:50:50.480
curved into a broad, menacing smile.
What was it that had escaped? The lizard,

00:50:50.480 --> 00:50:55.849
perhaps? The giant, grinning man? Whatever
it was, it seemed that the action was heading

00:50:55.849 --> 00:51:06.460
right toward 035’s containment cell. Perhaps
today was the day. Finally, the SCP Foundation

00:51:06.460 --> 00:51:10.589
would fall.
Outside the mask’s cell, Security Officer

00:51:10.589 --> 00:51:16.110
Harper was running for dear life. Though his
more rational mind knew he was living on seconds,

00:51:16.110 --> 00:51:21.510
not even minutes, of borrowed time, his animal
brain kept his legs pumping - desperately

00:51:21.510 --> 00:51:25.859
trying to avoid the screaming, howling predator
hot on his heels.

00:51:25.859 --> 00:51:33.170
Harper looked over his shoulder and screamed
as a long, white arm reached for him. SCP-096:

00:51:33.170 --> 00:51:39.331
The Shy Guy, its tooth-lined jaw hanging low
and foaming with spittle. That face, that

00:51:39.331 --> 00:51:45.020
terrible, terrible face - an absolute death
sentence to all who saw it. He’d seen what

00:51:45.020 --> 00:51:50.470
he thought was a crack in the otherwise perfect
seal of 096’s containment chamber, but it

00:51:50.470 --> 00:51:53.430
could have just as easily been a trick of
the light.

00:51:53.430 --> 00:51:57.420
Not even thinking, he’d stepped forward
and looked at the vulnerability in the chamber.

00:51:57.420 --> 00:52:03.180
All it took was one misplaced ray of light,
and he made out the vague shape of a face

00:52:03.180 --> 00:52:08.480
in the darkness. That’s when the weeping
started. Harper knew, in that moment, his

00:52:08.480 --> 00:52:12.760
life was over. The correct thing to do would
have been to order everyone else in the room

00:52:12.760 --> 00:52:17.410
to close their eyes while he stood there and
accepted his fate, minimizing the risk of

00:52:17.410 --> 00:52:22.670
spreading the damage further. But humans rarely
have perfect reasoning, even less so when

00:52:22.670 --> 00:52:26.670
facing mortality.
Back in the present, the Shy Guy made a perfect

00:52:26.670 --> 00:52:31.809
lunge, grabbing Harper in its iron clutches
and barreling through the adjoining wall.

00:52:31.809 --> 00:52:36.710
The nearby guards scattered, terrified, keeping
their eyes on the floor. They might get a

00:52:36.710 --> 00:52:41.140
slap on the wrist for temporarily abandoning
their posts, but they weren’t going to die

00:52:41.140 --> 00:52:47.380
guarding some stupid evil mask.
Speaking of, the possessive mask was surprised

00:52:47.380 --> 00:52:53.440
to feel two new presences enter its chamber
- through the now-destroyed wall. These two

00:52:53.440 --> 00:53:01.500
presences soon became just one, as SCP-096
quickly and totally annihilated Security Officer

00:53:01.500 --> 00:53:08.730
Harper, leaving nothing left. The mask couldn’t
see, per se, seeing as it had no actual sensory

00:53:08.730 --> 00:53:14.520
organs, but it felt around this new guest
with its many psychic tendrils, taking in

00:53:14.520 --> 00:53:20.119
its strange totality.
The creature was powerful, no doubt about

00:53:20.119 --> 00:53:25.789
that, and it elicited fear from those fools
at the SCP Foundation, but the mask noticed

00:53:25.789 --> 00:53:32.069
its brainwaves were extraordinarily muted.
Humans, to the mask’s vast and malicious

00:53:32.069 --> 00:53:37.090
consciousness, weren’t the sharpest knives
in the drawer, but compared to this thing’s

00:53:37.090 --> 00:53:42.780
mind, they were a pile of tempered katanas.
It barely thought at all. The mask would have

00:53:42.780 --> 00:53:50.470
to dig deeper to find anything it could use…
Meanwhile, SCP-096 finally began to calm down.

00:53:50.470 --> 00:53:56.010
The one who had seen it had been annihilated.
Bubbling rage was slowly siphoned out and

00:53:56.010 --> 00:54:02.171
replaced by the standard low but constant
hum of anxiety and despair. It would simply

00:54:02.171 --> 00:54:06.491
wait until its head was bagged and it was
dragged back to the dark. Same old, same old,

00:54:06.491 --> 00:54:13.870
all the way to the end of time. That’s when
it felt something else. It started as a faint

00:54:13.870 --> 00:54:19.031
buzz, an unintelligible whisper. Then, it
was almost like a door opened in the back

00:54:19.031 --> 00:54:24.160
of its head, Something stepped in and took
a seat.

00:54:24.160 --> 00:54:33.890
Can you hear me, stranger? Knock knock. I
want to speak to you…

00:54:33.890 --> 00:54:41.390
Something about the voice frightened and comforted
SCP-096 at the same time. It spoke with a

00:54:41.390 --> 00:54:47.391
greater degree of sympathy than the creature
had heard in a long time, and yet, something

00:54:47.391 --> 00:54:51.270
about the way it spoke implied evil in its
intent.

00:54:51.270 --> 00:55:00.190
I know what you want. I know what you fear.
Wouldn’t it be nice if they could never

00:55:00.190 --> 00:55:15.140
look at you again? If you could cover that
face of yours? I can help you. It will be

00:55:15.140 --> 00:55:26.839
so simple. All you need to do is put me on…
Little by little, 096 felt more of these strange

00:55:26.839 --> 00:55:32.779
thoughts filling up the emptiness in its head,
replacing the few thoughts the creature itself

00:55:32.779 --> 00:55:39.869
had. It felt itself lifting its hands from
the ground, lifting them and reaching towards

00:55:39.869 --> 00:55:47.190
something - A glass box. The glass shattered
and those long, white fingers reached for

00:55:47.190 --> 00:55:53.000
something within.
A mask. Just like the voice had said. A mask

00:55:53.000 --> 00:56:04.200
to stop people from looking at its face…
Yes, yes, you’re doing so well. You’re

00:56:04.200 --> 00:56:13.291
so close. Just a little further…
SCP-096 lifted the mask to its face, feeling

00:56:13.291 --> 00:56:19.600
black liquid that burned its skin dripping
from the porcelain, and put it on. And in

00:56:19.600 --> 00:56:25.329
that moment, everything changed.
The Shy Guy’s body began to seize up, rattling

00:56:25.329 --> 00:56:30.599
as the mask unleashed a web of psychic tendrils
through its body, mapping out across every

00:56:30.599 --> 00:56:37.130
inch like a new nervous system, taking control.
The Possessive Mask had never experienced

00:56:37.130 --> 00:56:43.470
a host like this before - That incredible,
perfect mix of physical durability and a mind

00:56:43.470 --> 00:56:52.809
so insubstantial that it was easy to sublimate.
Oh, this was going to be fun…

00:56:52.809 --> 00:56:58.960
For the first time, the Shy Guy - now under
the full control of the Possessive Mask - stood

00:56:58.960 --> 00:57:05.010
at full height on its hind legs, its spine
and shoulders clicking into place for its

00:57:05.010 --> 00:57:11.390
new stance. The Mask cracked its neck, getting
used to the new dimensions of its physicality.

00:57:11.390 --> 00:57:18.630
Its indestructible bones. Its long, grasping
limbs. Its skin, burning and fizzling with

00:57:18.630 --> 00:57:24.650
the gooey black secretions, but growing back
just as quickly. The Foundation had every

00:57:24.650 --> 00:57:28.730
reason to fear it now.
A group of security personnel had gathered

00:57:28.730 --> 00:57:33.310
in the ragged hole where the chamber’s north
wall used to be. Some were wielding light

00:57:33.310 --> 00:57:36.960
firearms; the guard at the front was carrying
an opaque black bag.

00:57:36.960 --> 00:57:43.859
The Mask laughed with its new body and turned
to the crowd. The second they saw it on 096’s

00:57:43.859 --> 00:57:49.990
body, their faces fell. For a moment, their
bodies went slack with terror. This situation

00:57:49.990 --> 00:57:55.279
was unprecedented. What course of action could
they possibly take at a time like this?

00:57:55.279 --> 00:57:59.619
It looked at the bag held by the leader of
the security force and projected a single

00:57:59.619 --> 00:58:05.750
thought into his mind: You won’t be needing
that.

00:58:05.750 --> 00:58:11.500
Before any of the guards even had a chance
to open fire, the Mask lunged forwards, using

00:58:11.500 --> 00:58:17.050
the long, terrible arms of the Shy Guy to
tear through the guards. They were dead in

00:58:17.050 --> 00:58:23.170
seconds, their bodies strewn about the hallway.
The mask’s porcelain was twisted into a

00:58:23.170 --> 00:58:28.660
wide, sadistic grin. It could tell that it
was about to have some real fun around here

00:58:28.660 --> 00:58:34.560
- and once it’d slaughtered everyone here,
it could finally stretch its legs out in the

00:58:34.560 --> 00:58:42.200
open again. True freedom to spread misery,
fear, and pain everywhere it went.

00:58:42.200 --> 00:58:47.819
There were just a few hundred members of Foundation
personnel it needed to turn into corpses,

00:58:47.819 --> 00:58:50.810
first.
More containment breach alarms sounded around

00:58:50.810 --> 00:58:55.930
the site as the Mask began its rampage, running
through the hallways and tearing apart any

00:58:55.930 --> 00:59:03.190
unfortunate Foundation personnel it could
get 096’s hands on. Guards, researchers,

00:59:03.190 --> 00:59:10.359
administrative staff, and even one extremely
unlucky janitor in Hallway C6. It was having

00:59:10.359 --> 00:59:16.200
the time of its long and terrible life, and
much to its glee, it seemed that this new

00:59:16.200 --> 00:59:23.630
host’s body was still holding up. It was
perfect symbiosis - A twisted, brilliant mind

00:59:23.630 --> 00:59:29.460
and a body that could forever support it.
There would be no stopping it, a conclusion

00:59:29.460 --> 00:59:35.360
that the hapless guards posted around the
site soon realized on their own terms. 096

00:59:35.360 --> 00:59:41.279
was indestructible, but it was dumb as a brick
and had an easily exploitable weakness: Get

00:59:41.279 --> 00:59:47.309
the bag on its head and you’re home free.
This new composite creature was a different

00:59:47.309 --> 00:59:52.550
story. It could think tactically, avoiding
head-on confrontations in favor of sneak attacks,

00:59:52.550 --> 00:59:58.990
and the monster had as much psychic combat
potential as physical. Guards roving the building

00:59:58.990 --> 01:00:04.829
in teams, heavily armed, with anti-memetic
protective gear, still reported feeling immense

01:00:04.829 --> 01:00:10.880
feelings of psychological dread over comms.
That was the greatest sign that The Mask would

01:00:10.880 --> 01:00:15.750
come bursting through the wall moments later
and tear them to shreds.

01:00:15.750 --> 01:00:20.430
The Site Director put out an urgent call for
all nearby Mobile Task Forces to intercept

01:00:20.430 --> 01:00:26.710
and help them take care of the unfolding situation.
Thankfully, a detachment of MTF Eta-10, also

01:00:26.710 --> 01:00:32.440
known as "See No Evil", was operating on a
case in close proximity. Given their specialization

01:00:32.440 --> 01:00:37.950
in anomalies with a memetic visual property,
many on the team had dealt with 096 breaches

01:00:37.950 --> 01:00:42.809
before. That at least gave them experience
in half of this situation.

01:00:42.809 --> 01:00:49.309
And one operative among them, Sergeant Henrique
Ramirez, would be the one to bring this nightmare

01:00:49.309 --> 01:00:53.810
to an end. But it would cost him his life
in the process.

01:00:53.810 --> 01:00:58.700
The Mask was still using its new indestructible
body to wreak havoc on the containment site.

01:00:58.700 --> 01:01:03.640
Once it had taken out the primary contingents
of guards, it was free to have its fun with

01:01:03.640 --> 01:01:09.460
the rest: Stalking defenseless researchers
through the halls, making sure to induce maximum

01:01:09.460 --> 01:01:16.000
terror before finally striking the killing
blow. Every single one of them died with a

01:01:16.000 --> 01:01:21.809
head full of demonic whispers - it told them
of the Mask’s eternal dominion. Now, it

01:01:21.809 --> 01:01:28.020
had found the perfect host, nothing on earth
would stop it. Humans would be mere ants under

01:01:28.020 --> 01:01:31.370
its feet.
Eta-10 touched down and entered the building.

01:01:31.370 --> 01:01:36.010
Ramirez was point man, leading the others
into the bowels of the blood-drenched containment

01:01:36.010 --> 01:01:42.640
site. They’d been briefed on what they were
heading in for: 096 and 035 had reached symbiosis

01:01:42.640 --> 01:01:48.390
and were displaying unprecedented anomalous
effects. Enter with extreme caution - They’re

01:01:48.390 --> 01:01:52.119
beyond dangerous, even more so together than
alone.

01:01:52.119 --> 01:01:56.779
Ideally, Ramirez would have wanted to go in
with a comprehensive plan, but lives were

01:01:56.779 --> 01:02:00.980
on the line here. They needed to leap off
the cliff and build their wings on the way

01:02:00.980 --> 01:02:03.830
down.
It was only when they finally encountered

01:02:03.830 --> 01:02:09.950
the monster that they realized just how outmatched
they were. Despite their best efforts, the

01:02:09.950 --> 01:02:15.079
combined speed, intelligence, and ferocity
of the Mask’s new form allowed it to make

01:02:15.079 --> 01:02:22.099
short work of Eta-10. Only Ramirez was left,
heavily injured. Even if a miracle happened,

01:02:22.099 --> 01:02:27.710
he knew he wasn’t getting out of here alive.
The Mask grabbed him with one of 096’s claws

01:02:27.710 --> 01:02:33.809
and lifted him up. It would take its time
with this one. Make him suffer. Watch him

01:02:33.809 --> 01:02:38.690
squirm. Destroy his mind.
Ramirez felt the Mask’s psychic tendrils

01:02:38.690 --> 01:02:44.640
penetrate the membrane of his mind. Those
whispers, those terrible whispers, reciting

01:02:44.640 --> 01:02:50.559
all his worst fears with terrible glee. His
gun was out of ammo. His knife was broken.

01:02:50.559 --> 01:02:56.810
All he had left on him was a pocket mirror.
And that was his eureka moment. It was a long

01:02:56.810 --> 01:03:01.790
shot, but it was also his only shot.
He reached out and grabbed the bottom of the

01:03:01.790 --> 01:03:07.760
mask, pulling for dear life. His other hand
shot into his pocket and grabbed the mirror,

01:03:07.760 --> 01:03:12.090
opening its lid with a deft flick of his thumb.
It was all too fast for the Mask to even register

01:03:12.090 --> 01:03:15.910
what was going on.
Ramirez forced his eyes shut and lifted up

01:03:15.910 --> 01:03:21.819
the mirror. The Mask saw its own reflection
in the glass, as the bottom of its face came

01:03:21.819 --> 01:03:25.910
loose, revealing the reflection of the face
underneath.

01:03:25.910 --> 01:03:30.849
The Mask squeezed, killing Ramirez, but it
was already too late. It had finally seen

01:03:30.849 --> 01:03:35.319
the face of its host, and that would cost
it dearly.

01:03:35.319 --> 01:03:40.619
The Mask felt a sudden and tremendous pushback
to its psychic forces - A blind despair and

01:03:40.619 --> 01:03:48.349
then rage that choked out everything. SCP-096
began to sob and howl. Somehow, the Mask was

01:03:48.349 --> 01:03:53.460
no longer in control.
Despite its psychic protests, 096 reached

01:03:53.460 --> 01:03:59.000
up and tore the terrible mask from its face,
tossing it against the wall with such force

01:03:59.000 --> 01:04:03.040
that it was embedded in the brickwork. Its
last thoughts, as other Mobile Task Force

01:04:03.040 --> 01:04:08.170
operators descended on the area to bag 096
and return it to its containment chamber,

01:04:08.170 --> 01:04:13.599
were: What the hell just happened?
And the next thought that crossed the mind

01:04:13.599 --> 01:04:19.069
of the Site Director was, “Request site
transfer for 035 as soon as possible. Don’t

01:04:19.069 --> 01:04:22.260
want a repeat of this incident any time soon…”

01:04:22.260 --> 01:04:26.900
Imagine knowing that there’s a monster,
right under your nose. Imagine knowing that

01:04:26.900 --> 01:04:32.750
this monster is capable of killing any human
being on earth, and all because they simply

01:04:32.750 --> 01:04:39.890
saw a photograph of its face. When this monster
is on the warpath, literally nothing can stop

01:04:39.890 --> 01:04:46.500
it. No weapon that human beings have invented
is capable of even slowing it down.

01:04:46.500 --> 01:04:53.299
Imagine knowing, in the age of mass media,
of clicks and shares, of 24 hour news, smart

01:04:53.299 --> 01:04:59.079
phones, and limitless social media connecting
billions across the globe, that one stray

01:04:59.079 --> 01:05:04.400
picture of this monster leaked in the wrong
place could cause the end of the world as

01:05:04.400 --> 01:05:10.720
we know it. And this fateful, terrifying day
could come at any time.

01:05:10.720 --> 01:05:17.810
Congratulations, you now know how it feels
to be Dr. Dan, the hot-headed lead SCP Foundation

01:05:17.810 --> 01:05:24.690
researcher on the SCP-096 case. The anomaly
itself requires no real introduction - the

01:05:24.690 --> 01:05:30.400
infamous, mass-murdering Shy Guy has haunted
the nightmares of Foundation experts for years.

01:05:30.400 --> 01:05:35.920
But nobody is more haunted by this thing than
Dr. Dan himself.

01:05:35.920 --> 01:05:42.329
For over a decade now, he has firmly believed
that there’s one solution to the 096 problem:

01:05:42.329 --> 01:05:48.700
Termination, with extreme prejudice. In his
eyes, to think that 096 is ever truly contained

01:05:48.700 --> 01:05:53.940
is an illusion. They could never be sure of
how many pictures of it are out there, floating

01:05:53.940 --> 01:06:00.490
around just waiting to be seen, like a time
bomb waiting to go off. The resources spent

01:06:00.490 --> 01:06:05.589
containing it and cleaning up after its rampages,
he would often argue, would be better spent

01:06:05.589 --> 01:06:08.490
instead figuring out how to finally kill it.

01:06:08.490 --> 01:06:14.700
But seeing as we’re talking about the SCP
Foundation here, not the Global Occult Coalition,

01:06:14.700 --> 01:06:20.830
the O5 Council didn’t see it quite the same
way. They constantly overturned Dr. Dan’s

01:06:20.830 --> 01:06:26.100
termination requests, leaving the paranoid
researcher more furious with each rejection.

01:06:26.100 --> 01:06:29.529
He tried to warn them, again and again and
again.

01:06:29.529 --> 01:06:34.270
“Don’t you see? It’s like a stick of
dynamite, and the fuse is already lit. If

01:06:34.270 --> 01:06:38.859
you don’t let me put it out before it’s
too late, there won’t even be anyone left

01:06:38.859 --> 01:06:40.510
to pick up the pieces!”

01:06:40.510 --> 01:06:48.440
But his pleas fell on deaf ears. Until, of
course, Incident 096-1-a, a dark and bloody

01:06:48.440 --> 01:06:53.920
day still looked back on with shame and horror
by even the most hardened members of Foundation

01:06:53.920 --> 01:06:55.119
staff.

01:06:55.119 --> 01:07:01.760
It was the day that SCP-096 gave everyone
a taste of the mass carnage it was truly capable

01:07:01.760 --> 01:07:02.760
of.

01:07:02.760 --> 01:07:07.750
Dr. Dan wasn’t even there when containment
was first breached. He was off on an expedition

01:07:07.750 --> 01:07:13.369
in the snowy mountains where 096 was first
found, trying to discover clues to its true

01:07:13.369 --> 01:07:19.860
origin. Dr. Dan’s second in command, Dr.
Oleksei, was also lucky enough to be outside

01:07:19.860 --> 01:07:25.180
of the containment area when the Shy Guy escaped.
According to interviews he gave at debriefing

01:07:25.180 --> 01:07:30.150
tribunals, he was in the break room, treating
himself to a warm cup of joe.

01:07:30.150 --> 01:07:35.340
All the other personnel assigned to 096’s
containment and research were near its containment

01:07:35.340 --> 01:07:41.339
chamber, a giant cube made of reinforced metal,
observing a live feed of data being collected

01:07:41.339 --> 01:07:47.660
from the cube’s interior. As usual, sensors
indicated that 096 was pacing within. For

01:07:47.660 --> 01:07:52.740
obvious reasons, the Foundation doesn’t
maintain any kind of live visual surveillance

01:07:52.740 --> 01:07:55.960
on 096. It wouldn’t be worth the risk.

01:07:55.960 --> 01:08:01.599
But what seemed like an otherwise quiet day
suddenly took a turn for the insane. The monitors

01:08:01.599 --> 01:08:06.730
began displaying terrifying data, indicating
an imminent containment breach, as the wails

01:08:06.730 --> 01:08:11.029
of the creature within the cube echoed out
through its metal walls.

01:08:11.029 --> 01:08:16.300
Some tried to run. Others did all they could
to engage emergency procedures, while guards

01:08:16.300 --> 01:08:21.270
armed with heavy weaponry rushed into the
room. They all knew in their hearts that it

01:08:21.270 --> 01:08:24.040
wouldn’t save them, but what else could
they do?

01:08:24.040 --> 01:08:29.319
A huge dent bulged out from the side of the
cube, then another, before the metal was torn

01:08:29.319 --> 01:08:33.799
open like tissue paper as the furious hands
of the Shy Guy began clawing their way through

01:08:33.799 --> 01:08:39.920
it. Secondary layers of protection were deployed,
but they didn’t help, 096 just kept ripping

01:08:39.920 --> 01:08:46.929
through all the barriers. As everyone in attendance
knew: Once it was in its rage state, nothing

01:08:46.929 --> 01:08:50.330
could stop it. Literally nothing.`

01:08:50.330 --> 01:08:55.100
The guards attempted to fire on the anomaly,
but their bullets did no good. As the creature

01:08:55.100 --> 01:09:00.080
burst free, most of the people in the room
had at least momentary visual contact with

01:09:00.080 --> 01:09:05.101
its face. In response, Code Lima was declared,
and the entire chamber was flooded with a

01:09:05.101 --> 01:09:09.109
fast-acting nerve gas, killing everyone.

01:09:09.109 --> 01:09:12.650
Everyone except for 096 itself of course.

01:09:12.650 --> 01:09:18.210
Within two minutes, 096 had broken free of
the entire containment site, and began running

01:09:18.210 --> 01:09:23.100
through the desert at breakneck speeds towards
its distant target. The Foundation needed

01:09:23.100 --> 01:09:27.449
to act fast and find a way to stop it before
it hit a population center where the death

01:09:27.449 --> 01:09:29.500
toll would skyrocket.

01:09:29.500 --> 01:09:34.029
Team Echo Romeo were dispatched to pursue
the creature in a heavily-armed attack helicopter

01:09:34.029 --> 01:09:41.080
while MTF Tau-1, a task force specializing
in SCP-096 containment and recovery, forged

01:09:41.080 --> 01:09:47.870
ahead to identify the anomaly’s target,
designated 096-1, and to move any other civilians

01:09:47.870 --> 01:09:53.460
out of harm’s way. Dr. Dan was alerted of
the disastrous situation and offered guidance

01:09:53.460 --> 01:09:55.840
to both teams from afar.

01:09:55.840 --> 01:10:01.750
Echo Romeo’s chopper pursued 096, barely
able to keep up. Dr. Dan recommended attempting

01:10:01.750 --> 01:10:07.070
to at least slow the creature down with heavy
arms fire, even though he knew actually stopping

01:10:07.070 --> 01:10:13.219
it would be impossible. A member of the team
used a modified XM500 anti-materiel rifle

01:10:13.219 --> 01:10:17.650
to fire several rounds at the creature, but
even the shot that passed directly through

01:10:17.650 --> 01:10:20.590
its head appeared to have no effect on it.

01:10:20.590 --> 01:10:26.650
But at least they had a lock on its direction
now. 096 always travels in a straight line,

01:10:26.650 --> 01:10:32.690
directly towards its target, smashing through
any obstacles in its way. This means its intended

01:10:32.690 --> 01:10:37.929
direction is relatively easy to track, and
people in the path of its trajectory are even

01:10:37.929 --> 01:10:43.449
easier to save - but it’s a high-pressure
job reserved for MTF Tau-1.

01:10:43.449 --> 01:10:50.130
Tau-1 passed over Echo Romeo and 096 in a
series of eight v-22 Ospreys, hoping beyond

01:10:50.130 --> 01:10:54.290
hope that they could outrun the creature;
since there were several different towns in

01:10:54.290 --> 01:10:55.530
its direct path.

01:10:55.530 --> 01:11:01.350
But, thanks once again to the one and only
Dr. Dan, Echo Romeo and Tau-1 had a secret

01:11:01.350 --> 01:11:06.800
weapon that neither team had ever possessed
before: SCRAMBLE. SCRAMBLE was an advanced

01:11:06.800 --> 01:11:11.870
computer program, created by Dr. Dan with
the help of Dr. Oleksei, which was coded into

01:11:11.870 --> 01:11:17.270
microprocessors for modification into camera
feeds and viewing goggles. The program had

01:11:17.270 --> 01:11:23.940
been fed intricate data concerning every single
detail of 096’s face, and as the name suggests,

01:11:23.940 --> 01:11:28.590
when encountering that visual data in real
life it scrambles it beyond recognition.

01:11:28.590 --> 01:11:35.280
In theory, it would make it far safer for
operatives to be around SCP-096 without activating

01:11:35.280 --> 01:11:36.750
its rage state.

01:11:36.750 --> 01:11:37.750
In theory.

01:11:37.750 --> 01:11:43.550
It wasn’t long before disaster struck. 096
had made its way onto I-40, and had begun

01:11:43.550 --> 01:11:49.030
attacking vehicles it encountered, killing
the people inside. By this point, it had already

01:11:49.030 --> 01:11:54.450
outrun the Echo Romeo chopper, rendering them
essentially useless. Tau-1 just had to forge

01:11:54.450 --> 01:11:59.310
ahead along 096’s trajectory, and try to
get as many people out of the danger zone

01:11:59.310 --> 01:12:05.010
as possible until 096-1 was located and destroyed
by the anomaly.

01:12:05.010 --> 01:12:09.989
Years of training for an awful day like this
had paid off, as the first three towns along

01:12:09.989 --> 01:12:15.860
096’s path were successfully evacuated.
The Shy Guy just ran right through all of

01:12:15.860 --> 01:12:22.440
them, and no lives were lost. The same, however,
could not be said for town number four.

01:12:22.440 --> 01:12:27.790
As was standard procedure, Tau-1 had everyone
in town gathered up and blindfolded. The entire

01:12:27.790 --> 01:12:33.090
team in attendance had modified night vision
goggles with SCRAMBLE microprocessors, so

01:12:33.090 --> 01:12:38.340
in theory, they were better off than most
from 096 if things went south.

01:12:38.340 --> 01:12:43.170
The Tau-1 members tried in vain to keep everybody
in line. They were threatening to shoot anyone

01:12:43.170 --> 01:12:49.170
who dared to remove their blindfold when 096
finally appeared. It tripped during its approach,

01:12:49.170 --> 01:12:54.770
rolling and shattering through several houses.
Chaos erupted. Some people instinctively removed

01:12:54.770 --> 01:12:59.679
their blindfolds to see what was going on,
and panicked members of Tau-1 began firing

01:12:59.679 --> 01:13:00.940
into the crowd.

01:13:00.940 --> 01:13:05.670
The screams of the civilians and the bursts
of gunfire were all drowned out by the horrific

01:13:05.670 --> 01:13:12.380
wailing of 096 itself as it tore through everyone
and everything to kill the unfortunate souls

01:13:12.380 --> 01:13:17.690
who had seen its face. And given how many
had removed their blindfolds during the initial

01:13:17.690 --> 01:13:24.170
pandemonium, SCP-096 had a huge number of
the townspeople on its list.

01:13:24.170 --> 01:13:30.190
096 located and grabbed the original 096-1,
the person that’d first glimpsed its face

01:13:30.190 --> 01:13:35.710
and caused all this, but in the grand scheme
of things, he was just one more body on the

01:13:35.710 --> 01:13:42.460
pile that day. Though of course, we mean this
in a purely metaphorical sense - 096 never

01:13:42.460 --> 01:13:44.710
leaves entire bodies.

01:13:44.710 --> 01:13:48.360
The Foundation operatives in attendance were
able to figure out what had caused all this

01:13:48.360 --> 01:13:55.010
madness and death. 096-1 had been an amateur
mountaineer, and spent his vacations hiking

01:13:55.010 --> 01:14:00.730
around any mountain ranges he could gain access
to - including the mountain range that 096

01:14:00.730 --> 01:14:06.280
had been inhabiting prior to its initial capture
and containment. But he hadn’t actually

01:14:06.280 --> 01:14:11.550
seen 096 on that fateful trip, which he had
taken over a decade ago.

01:14:11.550 --> 01:14:17.949
But in a photograph taken that day of himself,
standing on a picturesque mountainside, 096

01:14:17.949 --> 01:14:24.909
made a cameo. It took up all of four pixels,
a tiny off-white dot against the snow on the

01:14:24.909 --> 01:14:31.270
back-right side of the photograph. This photograph,
with all the deadly potency of an atom bomb,

01:14:31.270 --> 01:14:36.440
had been sitting there for years. Waiting.
When the mountaineer was looking through old

01:14:36.440 --> 01:14:41.610
photos that morning and his eyes passed over
those few blurry pixels, he didn’t have

01:14:41.610 --> 01:14:46.830
any idea that he had just laid his eyes on
one of the most dangerous anomalies the world

01:14:46.830 --> 01:14:48.260
has ever seen.

01:14:48.260 --> 01:14:52.920
But you don’t have to know. Only 096 does.

01:14:52.920 --> 01:14:58.550
As Tau-1, led by their commander, Major Jack
Wilford, tried to retake control of the situation,

01:14:58.550 --> 01:15:03.760
things got even worse. It turned out, Dr.
Dan’s SCRAMBLE technology - the one thing

01:15:03.760 --> 01:15:09.280
that was supposed to have given them an edge
in this situation - didn’t actually work.

01:15:09.280 --> 01:15:14.449
As soon as goggle wearing members of the team
laid eyes on 096, it entered its rage state

01:15:14.449 --> 01:15:16.630
and started massacring them.

01:15:16.630 --> 01:15:21.510
It tore many of the Mobile Task Force members
to shreds, including a number of the operatives

01:15:21.510 --> 01:15:27.480
required for piloting the eight Ospreys. It
was a disaster of truly epic proportions,

01:15:27.480 --> 01:15:32.400
and the catastrophic failure of Dr. Dan’s
SCRAMBLE technology was a key factor.

01:15:32.400 --> 01:15:37.091
The root of the technology’s failure was
easy enough to figure out. Dr. Dan’s SCRAMBLE

01:15:37.091 --> 01:15:43.150
microprocessors worked fast, but not fast
enough. Fractions-of-a-second glimpses of

01:15:43.150 --> 01:15:49.840
096’s face managed to get through, and that
was more than enough to set 096 off.

01:15:49.840 --> 01:15:54.410
Echo Romeo and MTF Epsilon arrived shortly
afterwards to clean up the mess. It’d been

01:15:54.410 --> 01:15:59.060
a disaster of such magnitude that even a CNN
reporter arrived on the scene and started

01:15:59.060 --> 01:16:04.110
filming before being promptly shut down by
Epsilon. It was a member of Echo Romeo that

01:16:04.110 --> 01:16:10.390
finally slipped the bag over a docile 096’s
head, preparing it for containment once again,

01:16:10.390 --> 01:16:15.659
the creature now content that it had eliminated
all of the unfortunate souls who had seen

01:16:15.659 --> 01:16:16.659
its face.

01:16:16.659 --> 01:16:22.320
He’d found 096 sitting next to a minivan
that had its roof ripped off, the young family

01:16:22.320 --> 01:16:26.199
inside being the last to have seen its face.

01:16:26.199 --> 01:16:30.440
Things were so awful in the aftermath of the
containment breach that the O5 Council changed

01:16:30.440 --> 01:16:37.980
their tune, finally granting Dr. Dan permission
to terminate 096, by any means necessary.

01:16:37.980 --> 01:16:42.630
It’s terrifying to think that all this can
unfold because of a couple of poorly-timed

01:16:42.630 --> 01:16:48.210
accidents. But it’s probably even more terrifying
to consider that it wasn’t an accident at

01:16:48.210 --> 01:16:53.830
all. It had seemed awfully convenient that
both Dr. Dan and Dr. Oleksei were far from

01:16:53.830 --> 01:16:59.250
danger when all hell broke loose. It was also
convenient that, despite the failure of his

01:16:59.250 --> 01:17:05.170
technology being a major reason why the breach
was as deadly as it was, Dr. Dan had still

01:17:05.170 --> 01:17:08.890
come out of the situation with everything
he wanted.

01:17:08.890 --> 01:17:13.550
The Foundation also found this strange, and
the more they looked into it, the more they

01:17:13.550 --> 01:17:19.989
found details that didn’t add up. Alibis
began to fall apart. Coincidences didn’t

01:17:19.989 --> 01:17:27.040
seem so coincidental, and eventually a weak
link in the chain was identified - Dr. Oleksei.

01:17:27.040 --> 01:17:32.170
Under pressure from the Foundation, Oleksei
spilled everything. Dr. Dan had essentially

01:17:32.170 --> 01:17:37.400
orchestrated the entire containment breach,
and on top of that, he did everything he could

01:17:37.400 --> 01:17:42.330
to make it as deadly as possible. All with
the purpose of getting the O5 Council to see

01:17:42.330 --> 01:17:50.170
things his way, and finally give him the permission
he needed to devote all of the SCP-096 resources

01:17:50.170 --> 01:17:52.210
to its termination.

01:17:52.210 --> 01:17:57.929
But it came at a terrible price to Dr. Dan
personally. For his crimes against the Foundation

01:17:57.929 --> 01:18:03.130
and humanity, he was permitted to try and
figure out a way to terminate 096 once and

01:18:03.130 --> 01:18:08.650
for all, but as payment for his crimes, he
himself would also be terminated as soon as

01:18:08.650 --> 01:18:10.350
his task was complete.

01:18:10.350 --> 01:18:15.480
Were the lives of all of those innocent people,
and Dr. Dan’s himself, worth the price of

01:18:15.480 --> 01:18:22.770
finally being authorized to terminate SCP-096?
When asked why he did all this, Dr. Dan simply

01:18:22.770 --> 01:18:23.770
said…

01:18:23.770 --> 01:18:27.860
“It worked. There was only a matter of time
until that happened in a major population

01:18:27.860 --> 01:18:34.550
center, and its face spread over the world
news. I can kill 096, but I’ve killed myself

01:18:34.550 --> 01:18:35.960
in the process.”

01:18:35.960 --> 01:18:40.850
While we don’t agree with his methods, there’s
something almost admirable about a person

01:18:40.850 --> 01:18:44.840
so single mindedly devoted to their cause.

01:18:44.840 --> 01:18:50.740
As you walk down the halls of the SCP Foundation
Site-19, peeking in the various windows at

01:18:50.740 --> 01:18:57.190
the anomalies contained there, you might catch
a glimpse of a dark figure bent over a table,

01:18:57.190 --> 01:19:03.130
tinkering away like an artisan in his workshop.
A vintage black apothecary bag sits next to

01:19:03.130 --> 01:19:07.440
him, open, and if you stop and watch for a
while, you’ll see him pull all manner of

01:19:07.440 --> 01:19:14.389
tools out of it. Impossibly large tools, things
that shouldn’t fit in such a small bag:

01:19:14.389 --> 01:19:19.920
a bone saw, an IV stand, jars of fluorescent
liquids and needles the length of your forearm.

01:19:19.920 --> 01:19:26.800
You shouldn’t be surprised, this is a place
for impossible things, after all. Still, it’s

01:19:26.800 --> 01:19:32.460
a curious sight, the shadowy man working so
diligently, so quietly, focused singularly

01:19:32.460 --> 01:19:37.100
on his craft. Whatever that may be.
Only one thing could distract him from his

01:19:37.100 --> 01:19:43.810
efforts: you. He feels your gaze on him, and
he looks up, dark eyes glittering from behind

01:19:43.810 --> 01:19:49.270
a beaked ceramic mask. He reminds you of an
illustration you once saw in a book about

01:19:49.270 --> 01:19:55.050
the Black Death, the gear the plague doctors
wore while treating patients on their deathbeds.

01:19:55.050 --> 01:20:00.440
“Hello!” He greets you in a friendly,
heavily accented voice. His eyes crinkle beneath

01:20:00.440 --> 01:20:04.639
the mask, and if you could see his mouth you
know he’d be smiling. “How are you today,

01:20:04.639 --> 01:20:09.810
dear fellow? Are you feeling quite well?”
He takes a step toward the window, stretching

01:20:09.810 --> 01:20:14.900
out one gloved hand, and you suddenly realize
that you can’t see where the mask ends and

01:20:14.900 --> 01:20:22.140
his skin begins…it’s not a mask, but a
part of his face. This is no ordinary man.

01:20:22.140 --> 01:20:28.660
“Do you require help? I can examine you…”
He offers, palm pressed flat against the glass.

01:20:28.660 --> 01:20:33.860
A chill runs up your spine, and you realize
that you should definitely not take him up

01:20:33.860 --> 01:20:39.590
on his offer. No matter how friendly he seems,
how good his intentions may be, you wouldn’t

01:20:39.590 --> 01:20:44.570
want to let the Plague Doctor treat you.
He sat in his containment cell, fidgeting

01:20:44.570 --> 01:20:50.139
with his favorite scalpel. He dragged it over
the surface of his work table, back and forth,

01:20:50.139 --> 01:20:55.810
listening to the sound it made. They had tried
to confiscate his table, his tools, but the

01:20:55.810 --> 01:21:00.510
guards had quickly learned that he had more
of them in his bag. They tried to take his

01:21:00.510 --> 01:21:06.139
bag from him, but, well, that didn’t go
over too well for anyone involved. So, he

01:21:06.139 --> 01:21:11.350
was allowed to keep it, to fashion himself
a makeshift laboratory in his lonely little

01:21:11.350 --> 01:21:15.780
cell. There was a time when they had given
him test subjects, fresh corpses from the

01:21:15.780 --> 01:21:20.380
morgue for him to dissect and research. There
was a time when the doctors here would come

01:21:20.380 --> 01:21:25.389
to speak to him, talking of cryptobiology
and the pestilence he had dedicated himself

01:21:25.389 --> 01:21:29.179
to fighting.
Those days were long gone. He had hidden away

01:21:29.179 --> 01:21:34.730
pieces of the corpses, tissue samples in jars
of formaldehyde he could pull out when the

01:21:34.730 --> 01:21:39.949
monotony became too much, but the days of
fresh materials, of enlightened discourse

01:21:39.949 --> 01:21:45.330
with other men and women of science, were
over. How he missed those days, the chance

01:21:45.330 --> 01:21:50.910
to work with others as he once had.
What had he done wrong? All he did was treat

01:21:50.910 --> 01:21:55.710
the sick. Sure, they didn’t always understand
their illness, didn’t want to receive their

01:21:55.710 --> 01:22:00.780
medicine, but that wasn’t a choice for the
patient to make. That should have been up

01:22:00.780 --> 01:22:05.830
to the physician. Perhaps they didn’t trust
his expertise, didn’t see how his work served

01:22:05.830 --> 01:22:11.550
the greater good. Like those who watched Jonas
Salk invent the polio vaccine, or Louis Pasteur

01:22:11.550 --> 01:22:16.880
rid milk of bacteria, they were confused by
the advanced scientific practices and feared

01:22:16.880 --> 01:22:21.730
that which they did not understand.
He could forgive them for their ignorance,

01:22:21.730 --> 01:22:26.900
he was magnanimous that way. If only they
would let him out of this infernal room, he

01:22:26.900 --> 01:22:32.660
could prove his work’s worth to them. He
could cure them all, begin a new era of wellness

01:22:32.660 --> 01:22:38.270
and peace worldwide. He didn’t exactly sleep,
but when he rested on his little cot in the

01:22:38.270 --> 01:22:45.440
corner of the room, he dreamed of that future.
Of a world healed by his touch. A knock at

01:22:45.440 --> 01:22:50.469
the door stirred him from his reverie. Someone
was at the door of his containment cell. He

01:22:50.469 --> 01:22:54.900
glanced at the little window, and saw a guard
there with a tray of food. He greeted the

01:22:54.900 --> 01:22:59.820
man with an enthusiastic wave. Sustenance!
He didn’t require the food for survival,

01:22:59.820 --> 01:23:04.840
of course, but it helped his mind work more
efficiently. It reminded him of a time before

01:23:04.840 --> 01:23:11.000
these fluorescent lights and these same four
walls, of crusty bread with fresh butter by

01:23:11.000 --> 01:23:14.449
the banks of the Seine.
A little slot opened in the door, and the

01:23:14.449 --> 01:23:18.739
tray was shoved through. There was bread,
just as he’d hoped. A small dish of butter,

01:23:18.739 --> 01:23:24.330
a pot of jam, and a cup of tea, still steaming.
He picked up the cup first, taking a deep

01:23:24.330 --> 01:23:30.580
breath. Ah, an herbal blend, with fresh lavender.
Lovely. He couldn’t see the guard through

01:23:30.580 --> 01:23:35.989
the window anymore, but he called out to him
just the same, “Thank you for the libations!”

01:23:35.989 --> 01:23:40.699
He still had his manners after all, even in
confinement. He wished he could have gotten

01:23:40.699 --> 01:23:45.810
a better look at the man, seen the pallor
of his complexion, a tremor in his hand. He

01:23:45.810 --> 01:23:50.260
thought he had spotted sweat, beading on his
forehead. Could he be ill? The case required

01:23:50.260 --> 01:23:55.050
further examination, to be certain. He sighed,
clutching the cup of tea tighter in frustration.

01:23:55.050 --> 01:24:00.540
Why wouldn’t they just let him work? Why
must they scream at the sight of his efforts,

01:24:00.540 --> 01:24:04.520
flee from his instruments? It didn’t seem
fair.

01:24:04.520 --> 01:24:10.340
Still, the pursuit of science rarely was a
glamorous one. He had learned as much over

01:24:10.340 --> 01:24:15.489
the centuries. One day, though, history would
look back on him kindly. Of this, he could

01:24:15.489 --> 01:24:18.300
be certain.
He was just settling in and beginning to spread

01:24:18.300 --> 01:24:23.310
butter across the admittedly stale bread,
when a horrible sound shook him to attention.

01:24:23.310 --> 01:24:29.480
He had heard the noise before, though he had
never seen its source. It was an ear splitting

01:24:29.480 --> 01:24:36.940
scream, a wail of pure agony like the sound
of a wounded wild animal. He had heard many,

01:24:36.940 --> 01:24:42.320
many screams during his life, from patients
and those who stood in the way of his work,

01:24:42.320 --> 01:24:47.210
but until he had been brought into the custody
of the Foundation, he had never heard a scream

01:24:47.210 --> 01:24:55.510
quite like this one. It was pure rage, devastation,
and suffering mixed together, wet with tears

01:24:55.510 --> 01:24:58.930
and loud enough to rip through human vocal
cords.

01:24:58.930 --> 01:25:04.719
Whatever was crying out, it was no mere man.
More screams answered it, and these were very

01:25:04.719 --> 01:25:11.320
much human. These sounds were more familiar
to him, shrieks of pain, of fear, of desperate

01:25:11.320 --> 01:25:16.739
(but futile) attempts to escape. Then, the
meaty thud of bodies falling to the floor,

01:25:16.739 --> 01:25:22.500
of torn off limbs hitting walls and windows.
A loud crash. And the sound of something large

01:25:22.500 --> 01:25:27.630
moving quite quickly through the halls.
Scientific curiosity got the better of the

01:25:27.630 --> 01:25:31.920
Doctor, and he found himself moving back to
his little window, face pressed to the glass

01:25:31.920 --> 01:25:36.730
so hard his beak nearly cracked it. He couldn’t
see much of anything, just guards running

01:25:36.730 --> 01:25:41.960
down the hall, weapons drawn. He saw one of
them fire, heard the gunshot ring out. What

01:25:41.960 --> 01:25:46.679
was he firing at?
Then, he saw it: a pale blur darting past

01:25:46.679 --> 01:25:50.620
the door. Whatever it was, it didn’t so
much as flinch when the bullet ricocheted

01:25:50.620 --> 01:25:56.280
off its skin. A long, thin arm crashed against
the door, knocking the Doctor backwards into

01:25:56.280 --> 01:26:01.320
his work table. He steadied himself and climbed
back to his feet, taking in the damage done

01:26:01.320 --> 01:26:06.550
to the door. It was crumpled in on itself,
nearly ripped off the hinges, and whatever

01:26:06.550 --> 01:26:12.160
had ploughed into it was already gone. From
the sounds of chaos in the distance, it had

01:26:12.160 --> 01:26:15.320
disappeared around the corner, with the guards
following it.

01:26:15.320 --> 01:26:20.500
He inspected the ruins of the door to his
containment cell. It was useless now, hanging

01:26:20.500 --> 01:26:25.920
loose and open. Well, that was an invitation
he was hardly about to decline. He grabbed

01:26:25.920 --> 01:26:30.930
his trusty bag, tossed his scalpel back inside,
and set off to see what all the commotion

01:26:30.930 --> 01:26:35.710
was. It was easy enough to follow the trail
of blood, stark and vivid red against the

01:26:35.710 --> 01:26:42.080
white tile floor, and the sound of gunfire,
human screams, and that loud, long, painful

01:26:42.080 --> 01:26:46.659
wail he had heard before.
He walked at a leisurely pace, taking his

01:26:46.659 --> 01:26:52.980
time, until the sound suddenly stopped. He
rounded the corner, and found a mound of bodies,

01:26:52.980 --> 01:26:59.320
guards and scientists, beaten and bloodied
almost beyond recognition. It was quiet here,

01:26:59.320 --> 01:27:05.280
save for one sound: the sound of weeping.
There, in the corner, huddled over with its

01:27:05.280 --> 01:27:10.949
face to the wall, was a pale, thin figure,
its shoulders heaving with the force of its

01:27:10.949 --> 01:27:17.480
sobs. This poor soul was clearly in great
distress. It was a peculiar sight, hairless

01:27:17.480 --> 01:27:21.040
and white, distended arms wrapped around itself
as it cried.

01:27:21.040 --> 01:27:26.260
“Excuse me!” The Doctor called out to
the pitiful creature. “Are you alright?

01:27:26.260 --> 01:27:31.050
Do you need assistance?”
It didn’t answer, it just continued to cry.

01:27:31.050 --> 01:27:36.710
Had something so despondent been responsible
for this destruction? The dozens of corpses,

01:27:36.710 --> 01:27:42.909
the smashed-in walls, the crumbled doors and
shattered windows. It seemed impossible. Sure,

01:27:42.909 --> 01:27:48.300
it was large, and looked strong, but he had
never seen a monster cry before. This couldn’t

01:27:48.300 --> 01:27:53.900
be a dangerous creature, not when it was so
sad. He would help it. But first, he would

01:27:53.900 --> 01:27:58.660
attend to some of these bodies. He sat his
bag on the ground, and pulled out several

01:27:58.660 --> 01:28:04.469
vials of liquid, a set of syringes, and a
variety of other surgical tools he might need.

01:28:04.469 --> 01:28:10.060
Now, after such a long hiatus, he could resume
his work in a meaningful way.

01:28:10.060 --> 01:28:14.390
He couldn’t be certain how long he worked,
reviving these poor souls and reconstructing

01:28:14.390 --> 01:28:19.380
their bodies, as the pale creature wept in
the corner. The sobbing faded into the background

01:28:19.380 --> 01:28:24.449
for a while, becoming a kind of white noise
as he removed a liver here, placed it in a

01:28:24.449 --> 01:28:30.600
chest cavity there, poked and prodded, injected
and extracted, testing out new methods alongside

01:28:30.600 --> 01:28:36.369
tried and true cures. One by one, the milky
eyes fluttered open, rigor mortis-stiff joints

01:28:36.369 --> 01:28:42.040
creaked into motion, sallow faces looking
at his with the vacant gratitude he saw in

01:28:42.040 --> 01:28:46.619
so many patients over the years.
He didn’t need them to thank him with their

01:28:46.619 --> 01:28:52.150
words, the work was its own reward. He kept
expecting more guards to arrive, to attempt

01:28:52.150 --> 01:28:57.910
to contain the situation, but none came even
as the alarm blared overhead.

01:28:57.910 --> 01:29:02.600
As for the morose creature, it didn’t seem
to notice his presence at all, not even when

01:29:02.600 --> 01:29:07.690
he had brought all of the intact corpses back
to life. The patients shuffled about the room

01:29:07.690 --> 01:29:13.100
aimlessly, waiting for orders of some kind.
The doctor tapped one on the shoulder, and

01:29:13.100 --> 01:29:15.949
handed the reborn man a vial of thick black
medicine.

01:29:15.949 --> 01:29:21.670
“Give this to the poor fellow in the corner,
please.” It wasn’t much, but it should

01:29:21.670 --> 01:29:27.139
calm him, provide some relief from his suffering.
The corpse nodded, mouth hanging loose and

01:29:27.139 --> 01:29:32.550
open, an eyeball dangling, unseeing, from
the socket. He shuffled over to the strange

01:29:32.550 --> 01:29:37.639
creature, and held out the vial to it. It
turned, lifting its head, and as it locked

01:29:37.639 --> 01:29:44.320
eyes with the cured patient, something shifted
in its face. Its mouth opened wide, impossibly

01:29:44.320 --> 01:29:49.500
wide, and it shrieked, that same terrible
sound as before.

01:29:49.500 --> 01:29:54.909
Tears streamed from its colorless eyes, its
arms shaking with unbridled rage, as its jaws

01:29:54.909 --> 01:30:00.330
locked around the patient’s head. Like a
boa constrictor, in one fluid motion it swallowed

01:30:00.330 --> 01:30:04.219
the revived man whole, while the Doctor watched
in shock.

01:30:04.219 --> 01:30:09.980
He had been wrong, this was not an innocent
caught in the wrong place at the wrong time.

01:30:09.980 --> 01:30:16.180
This creature, whatever it was, was deeply
sick. He had never seen such an advanced,

01:30:16.180 --> 01:30:21.010
aggressive case of the Pestilence. He’d
heard rumors, of course, but never encountered

01:30:21.010 --> 01:30:26.420
it firsthand. As a Doctor, he had sworn to
do no harm, but in a drastic situation, drastic

01:30:26.420 --> 01:30:31.719
measures have to be taken. It was well known,
by himself and the doctors at this Foundation,

01:30:31.719 --> 01:30:36.820
that he could cause any and all biological
functions in an organism to cease with a single

01:30:36.820 --> 01:30:41.900
touch. And so, he approached the creature,
arm outstretched, ready to administer that

01:30:41.900 --> 01:30:45.460
necessary touch to protect the rest of his
patients.

01:30:45.460 --> 01:30:50.020
As he approached, the creature turned to him,
its eyes wide and blank, an endless stream

01:30:50.020 --> 01:30:55.000
of tears pouring from them, spilling onto
the floor. It shrieked again, mouth stretching

01:30:55.000 --> 01:30:59.660
wide enough to engulf his entire head, and
ran toward him at a breakneck speed.

01:30:59.660 --> 01:31:05.800
“I’m so sorry, you’re not well…”
The Doctor said simply, as his hand pressed

01:31:05.800 --> 01:31:10.239
to the creature’s chest. As soon as the
tough hide of the Doctor’s hand (which the

01:31:10.239 --> 01:31:15.869
uninformed might mistake for gloves) made
contact with the unpigmented skin of the beast,

01:31:15.869 --> 01:31:22.310
its eyes closed, its muscles went slack, and
it collapsed to the ground with a mighty thud.

01:31:22.310 --> 01:31:27.330
The Doctor paced around the fallen creature,
taking in the sight. Then, something strange

01:31:27.330 --> 01:31:34.389
caught his eye. The creature’s chest still
rose, and fell. Was it his imagination? He

01:31:34.389 --> 01:31:40.790
checked its pulse, and though it was slow
and faint, it was very much present. The creature

01:31:40.790 --> 01:31:47.210
was still alive. It had merely been rendered
unconscious by his touch, rather than completely

01:31:47.210 --> 01:31:53.489
deceased.
“Curious…very curious indeed,” he muttered.

01:31:53.489 --> 01:31:58.330
Perhaps there were comorbidities present,
other infections aside from the Pestilence

01:31:58.330 --> 01:32:03.940
which rendered the creature unnaturally strong,
resilient to the usual courses of treatment.

01:32:03.940 --> 01:32:09.510
What would cause these abilities, this intense
aggression? It seemed to be brought on whenever

01:32:09.510 --> 01:32:14.780
someone looked at the entity’s face.
If only he’d studied psychology more, the

01:32:14.780 --> 01:32:19.730
science of the mind and its inner workings.
Since he had no experience with therapy, nor

01:32:19.730 --> 01:32:23.770
was he certain this creature could communicate
using language at all, there was only one

01:32:23.770 --> 01:32:29.330
way to find out more about how this creature’s
brain worked: he would have to take it apart

01:32:29.330 --> 01:32:33.330
and see for himself.
It was slow work, getting the massive creature

01:32:33.330 --> 01:32:38.270
back to the Doctor’s containment cell. He
required the help of his cured patients, who

01:32:38.270 --> 01:32:42.910
grasped it by its massive limbs and dragged
the limp body through the halls. Once back

01:32:42.910 --> 01:32:47.240
in a familiar environment, his work table
ready and waiting, the Doctor instructed his

01:32:47.240 --> 01:32:52.340
assistants to place the new patient on the
table. It was a bit small, unable to accommodate

01:32:52.340 --> 01:32:57.620
the creature’s distended limbs, but if he
attempted to use an official Foundation laboratory,

01:32:57.620 --> 01:33:02.580
he risked discovery and subsequent interruption.
So, it would have to suffice.

01:33:02.580 --> 01:33:07.580
First, he set up an IV stand, filled with
a vivid green liquid. It was easy to find

01:33:07.580 --> 01:33:12.449
a vein, the creature’s skin was nearly translucent.
Now that he could be certain the creature

01:33:12.449 --> 01:33:16.139
would not wake during surgery, he could make
the first incision.

01:33:16.139 --> 01:33:20.730
“Scalpel.” He held out a hand, and his
favorite surgical blade was placed in it by

01:33:20.730 --> 01:33:25.500
one of the helpful patients. “Thank you.”
He slid the scalpel along the hairline of

01:33:25.500 --> 01:33:30.060
the creature, or where the hairline would
be if it weren’t completely bald. Once the

01:33:30.060 --> 01:33:36.219
scalp was removed, he set it aside for later,
when it could be reattached. “Bone saw,

01:33:36.219 --> 01:33:41.360
please.” He held out his hand again, and
again his assistant gave him the proper tool.

01:33:41.360 --> 01:33:46.560
This, however, was when things got strange.
The Doctor had always been a deft hand at

01:33:46.560 --> 01:33:51.030
cutting - he’d once even received tutelage
from the great Robert Liston - but no matter

01:33:51.030 --> 01:33:56.460
how hard he tried to saw, it never left a
scratch on the creature’s skeleton. Naturally,

01:33:56.460 --> 01:34:00.949
this was somewhat frustrating - He wanted
to study the creature’s brain tissue, to

01:34:00.949 --> 01:34:04.989
get a sense for what was going on up there,
neuroligically. And he couldn’t do that

01:34:04.989 --> 01:34:09.969
if it was impossible to saw off the creature’s
cranial cap. He blunted two of his favorite

01:34:09.969 --> 01:34:13.179
saws while trying!
Thankfully, there was still a solution to

01:34:13.179 --> 01:34:17.780
accessing the beast’s grey matter: A little
trick he’d learned while studying the funerary

01:34:17.780 --> 01:34:23.610
practices of the ancient Egyptians. He produced
a long, curved hook from his bag and inserted

01:34:23.610 --> 01:34:28.600
it up the creature’s nasal passage. With
some fine manoeuvring, he eventually managed

01:34:28.600 --> 01:34:33.100
to remove the brain. It was such a terrible
shame that he needed to do it piece by piece

01:34:33.100 --> 01:34:39.020
via the nasal passage, but one makes do. All
that was left was to sew the skin of the creature’s

01:34:39.020 --> 01:34:42.929
head back into place.
He was mid-stitch when a voice interrupted

01:34:42.929 --> 01:34:46.070
his careful work, nearly making him drop the
needle.

01:34:46.070 --> 01:34:49.980
“Hey! What are you doing?”
He looked up to find a guard, aiming a gun

01:34:49.980 --> 01:34:53.969
at his face.
“Excuse me, I am in surgery at the moment.

01:34:53.969 --> 01:34:58.739
Please do not interrupt!” He admonished
the guard, but the man did not listen or lower

01:34:58.739 --> 01:35:02.530
his weapon.
In fact, he shouted something into a radio,

01:35:02.530 --> 01:35:06.760
code words the Doctor didn’t recognize.
Then, he fired a bullet into the skull of

01:35:06.760 --> 01:35:12.679
the patient standing at his side.
“How dare you!” the Doctor cried, readying

01:35:12.679 --> 01:35:17.180
himself to confront the guard. But it was
too late, dozens of other guards were swarming

01:35:17.180 --> 01:35:22.540
the room and neutralizing his assistants,
some in hazmat suits grabbed his arms and

01:35:22.540 --> 01:35:27.010
pulled him away from the creature on the operating
table, no matter how hard he fought or how

01:35:27.010 --> 01:35:32.140
loudly he protested.
Then, something incredible happened. Something

01:35:32.140 --> 01:35:35.950
wonderful.
The creature opened its eyes, and sat up.

01:35:35.950 --> 01:35:41.530
It looked directly at the guard closest to
it, and the two saw one another’s faces.

01:35:41.530 --> 01:35:47.170
The guard tensed, preparing for the worst…but
nothing came. The creature simply stared,

01:35:47.170 --> 01:35:52.810
placid and quiet. No screaming, no tearing
at flesh, no mouth opening wider and wider

01:35:52.810 --> 01:35:57.281
to swallow the man whole.
“I did it!” The Doctor shouted, overcome

01:35:57.281 --> 01:36:02.550
with elation. “I’ve cured you! Now begins
the rest of your happy life!”

01:36:02.550 --> 01:36:06.949
He watched as the guards led the shockingly
calm creature away, back to its containment

01:36:06.949 --> 01:36:11.940
cell. The Doctor’s door was repaired, and
he was returned to his state of captivity.

01:36:11.940 --> 01:36:16.630
But he never forgot the patient he helped
that day, and how marvelous it felt to do

01:36:16.630 --> 01:36:21.130
such a good deed.
Meanwhile, SCP-096’s brain regrew within

01:36:21.130 --> 01:36:26.790
the hour, and caused another massive containment
breach, murdering a variety of researchers

01:36:26.790 --> 01:36:32.929
and guards. But the staff agreed not to tell
SCP-049 about any of this. Better to just

01:36:32.929 --> 01:36:37.110
let him have this one, he really seemed like
he needed a win.

01:36:37.110 --> 01:36:42.150
The SCP Foundation’s hallowed halls have
been the site of many great and puzzling mysteries.

01:36:42.150 --> 01:36:48.230
Among them being, who was the true creator
of SCP-173, the neck-snapping Sculpture? What

01:36:48.230 --> 01:36:53.460
are the secrets that Dr. Alto Clef is keeping
about his past? Who are the true seven children

01:36:53.460 --> 01:37:00.860
of the Scarlet King? Who was SCP-106, the
Old Man, before he got all nasty? And of course,

01:37:00.860 --> 01:37:08.940
what is the true nature and origin of SCP-096
- The violent, terrifying, and incredibly

01:37:08.940 --> 01:37:10.540
withdrawn Shy Guy?

01:37:10.540 --> 01:37:15.270
It’s a question so great that we knew even
our experts here at SCP Explained couldn’t

01:37:15.270 --> 01:37:20.199
take on the mission alone. That’s why we
pooled the resources of you, the loyal viewers

01:37:20.199 --> 01:37:25.510
of SCP Explained, to find the answer. We made
a community post asking for your personal

01:37:25.510 --> 01:37:31.000
theories on how the Shy Guy came to be, and
you didn’t disappoint, sending in a cavalcade

01:37:31.000 --> 01:37:35.030
of curious conjecture that we just can’t
wait to explore.

01:37:35.030 --> 01:37:38.590
So let’s hop right in and see what all of
you had to say!

01:37:38.590 --> 01:37:40.130
Here is PuffyShade’s theory:

01:37:40.130 --> 01:37:45.650
“He was a lost British D-class named Martin
McGregor. On a risky experiment in Columbia

01:37:45.650 --> 01:37:51.350
regarding SCP-1290, a D-class prisoner was
teleported to an unknown mountainous location

01:37:51.350 --> 01:37:56.820
after a malfunction in the device. He stayed
there for 37 hours before dying of his bullet

01:37:56.820 --> 01:38:01.650
wounds from being shot by foreign soldiers.
Wherever the D-class was teleported to was

01:38:01.650 --> 01:38:04.599
a foreign country not welcome to outsiders.

01:38:04.599 --> 01:38:08.820
As we all know, there have been some SCPs
that have escaped the grasp of the foundation.

01:38:08.820 --> 01:38:13.750
The foundation has located them, but never
been able to see or capture them.

01:38:13.750 --> 01:38:18.150
Continuing with the story, it is speculated
that one of these entities that is still undiscovered

01:38:18.150 --> 01:38:23.540
had the power of necromancy. They brought
the D-class back to the mortal world, but

01:38:23.540 --> 01:38:29.080
in an immortal form. To terrorize humanity
forever. The entity brings humans back to

01:38:29.080 --> 01:38:34.590
life as supernatural beings, their powers
being determined by their former personality.

01:38:34.590 --> 01:38:39.929
The resurrected D-class was always shy, self
conscious of his unattractive face.

01:38:39.929 --> 01:38:44.380
As you can probably guess, this resulted in
the birth of our good old friend,

01:38:44.380 --> 01:38:45.659
Shy Guy.”

01:38:45.659 --> 01:38:50.449
Certainly a fascinating direction to go, with
a basis in recorded anomalous activity. And

01:38:50.449 --> 01:38:55.850
much like a few other anomalies originating
from D-Class personnel, such as SCP-2419,

01:38:55.850 --> 01:39:02.020
the Laughing Men, and SCP-4973, the Dead Men
Walking, it would feel oddly satisfying to

01:39:02.020 --> 01:39:07.460
see another one of the Foundation’s greatest
mistakes become one of the gravest threats.

01:39:07.460 --> 01:39:09.390
Here is DiamondStar Lemon’s theory:

01:39:09.390 --> 01:39:13.960
“The Shy Guy was Andrew Irvine, one of 2
mountaineers that went missing who were part

01:39:13.960 --> 01:39:18.219
of the British expedition that was sent to
reach the top of Mount Everest in 1924.

01:39:18.219 --> 01:39:23.580
While Andrew and his friend George Mallory
were heading for camp 5 , they encountered

01:39:23.580 --> 01:39:30.270
SCP-1529 at different intervals. Since Mallory's
body was eventually found in 1999 this tells

01:39:30.270 --> 01:39:37.210
us that Irvine also encountered 1529. Irvine
constantly rejected 1529's offers and eventually

01:39:37.210 --> 01:39:44.040
developed a defense mechanism where he gets
aggressive and attacks SCP-1529, in return

01:39:44.040 --> 01:39:50.320
1529 prolonged his life by years to make him
suffer every single minute. His skin turned

01:39:50.320 --> 01:39:55.170
white due to constant exposure to the sun
and he took off all of his clothes due to

01:39:55.170 --> 01:39:56.489
hypothermia.

01:39:56.489 --> 01:40:00.630
This is confirmed because Mallory's body was
just as white as Irvine and a letter he sent

01:40:00.630 --> 01:40:06.710
to his family says that Andrew became very
shy and barely speaks and he eventually disappears.

01:40:06.710 --> 01:40:11.849
Mallory then encounters 1529 and punches him
in the face and then Mallory falls unconscious

01:40:11.849 --> 01:40:17.340
leading to his death. Meanwhile Irvine loses
his sanity and has his body disfigured and

01:40:17.340 --> 01:40:20.090
his hands developed claws for better climbing.”

01:40:20.090 --> 01:40:25.710
This is another popular theory which has its
roots in pre-existing anomalous knowledge.

01:40:25.710 --> 01:40:31.020
The horrific tortures by SCP-1529, the Mountain
King, against Andrew Irvine is believed by

01:40:31.020 --> 01:40:36.080
many to be the root of the Shy Guy’s creation.
Of course, we can’t know for sure, but some

01:40:36.080 --> 01:40:40.630
compelling evidence undeniably points in this
intriguing direction.

01:40:40.630 --> 01:40:42.620
Here is #1 Try Hard’s Theory:

01:40:42.620 --> 01:40:46.600
“I think he was one of the subjects in the
Russian Sleep Experiment that escaped and

01:40:46.600 --> 01:40:51.760
ran into the Siberian mountains, became aware
of the monstrosity he had become, and became

01:40:51.760 --> 01:40:53.960
violently self conscious.”

01:40:53.960 --> 01:40:59.770
This is a strange and creative theory, wrapping
096 in with the lore of the legendary Russian

01:40:59.770 --> 01:41:05.349
Sleep Experiment. Russia does have its fair
share of snowy mountain ranges, and the experiment

01:41:05.349 --> 01:41:10.630
did turn its unfortunate subjects into horrifying
monsters. But the nature of these monsters

01:41:10.630 --> 01:41:15.580
did differ significantly from the Shy Guy
- showing higher intelligence, but far less

01:41:15.580 --> 01:41:18.210
durability against physical trauma.

01:41:18.210 --> 01:41:19.900
Here is Avery Spencer’s theory:

01:41:19.900 --> 01:41:24.730
“I have wondered if perhaps he was part
of an almost Dyatlov Pass incident and was

01:41:24.730 --> 01:41:29.650
the sole survivor. Being in such a position
he was forced to eat his comrades. He then

01:41:29.650 --> 01:41:34.830
took on almost wendigo aspects, but instead
of the blood thirsty feelings most got he

01:41:34.830 --> 01:41:40.080
became so ashamed of what he did he can no
longer tolerate someone looking at him.”

01:41:40.080 --> 01:41:45.360
This is another incredibly interesting theory
that has its basis in both Russian legend

01:41:45.360 --> 01:41:50.080
and real life history. There have been a number
of events in the snowy mountains of Russia,

01:41:50.080 --> 01:41:54.139
much like the Dyatlov Pass Incident, which
have left high-altitude tragedies in their

01:41:54.139 --> 01:41:58.590
wake. One of these, traumatizing its victim
to the point of turning them into an anomalous

01:41:58.590 --> 01:42:03.570
creature like SCP-096 definitely doesn’t
seem out of the question.

01:42:03.570 --> 01:42:05.239
Here is Kyreikal’s theory:

01:42:05.239 --> 01:42:11.949
“Shy Guy has no face. His face does not
exist. Not in the sense that its just smooth

01:42:11.949 --> 01:42:14.550
skin, the face is actually nothing.

01:42:14.550 --> 01:42:19.860
He is a body and mind directly attached to
a pattern screamer. Humans expect humanoid

01:42:19.860 --> 01:42:24.989
shaped creatures to have a face. Thus when
someone sees his "face" the screamer takes

01:42:24.989 --> 01:42:30.230
over and destroys that person so it can stop
existing. His body has changed over the years

01:42:30.230 --> 01:42:35.810
to facilitate eliminating people who see the
"face" more effectively. This is why he wants

01:42:35.810 --> 01:42:42.400
to dissappear in the desire camera, finish
being nothing so people stop filling in his

01:42:42.400 --> 01:42:43.400
face.

01:42:43.400 --> 01:42:48.230
A way it could have happened is an incomplete
encounter with 3930. The one who became the

01:42:48.230 --> 01:42:53.820
shy guy leaned in and just his face ceased
to exist. Though he probably would have already

01:42:53.820 --> 01:42:57.080
needed some anomalous traits to survive that.”

01:42:57.080 --> 01:43:02.989
This is another extremely original and interesting
theory based in pre-existing anomalies. Of

01:43:02.989 --> 01:43:08.190
course, there’s no real way to prove that
SCP-096’s face isn’t a pattern screamer,

01:43:08.190 --> 01:43:13.369
given that to look upon it is to court death,
so we may just need to take your word on this

01:43:13.369 --> 01:43:14.369
one…

01:43:14.369 --> 01:43:15.710
Here is Lacintag’s theory:

01:43:15.710 --> 01:43:21.030
“Perhaps 096 is an alien from a planet or
dimension that's completely dark, where being

01:43:21.030 --> 01:43:26.540
seen is a death sentence because of the local
anomalous fauna. He somehow wound up in our

01:43:26.540 --> 01:43:32.010
universe. Perhaps 096 is cursed that whoever
sees him will eventually transform into something

01:43:32.010 --> 01:43:38.280
much worse, or become a portal into a dark
dimension. And he does us all a favor by eliminating

01:43:38.280 --> 01:43:40.679
these people before that can happen.”

01:43:40.679 --> 01:43:46.190
Another two extremely interesting theories,
both of which could potentially hold water.

01:43:46.190 --> 01:43:51.580
They seem consistent with the fact that SCP-096
takes no pleasure in the killing of its victims,

01:43:51.580 --> 01:43:56.560
it only seems to be doing it out of the sense
that it feels compelled into action.

01:43:56.560 --> 01:43:58.270
Here is Chill Me’s theory:

01:43:58.270 --> 01:44:02.869
“He could be the physical embodiment of
the feeling of body dysmorphia.”

01:44:02.869 --> 01:44:09.000
Another very valid theory, given SCP-096’s
general temperament and caustic reactions

01:44:09.000 --> 01:44:11.389
to being perceived in any way.

01:44:11.389 --> 01:44:13.140
Here is Renan Roman’s theory:

01:44:13.140 --> 01:44:18.820
“What would happen if Dr Bright looked at
scp 096's face? I mean, his host would certainly

01:44:18.820 --> 01:44:24.670
be killed, but what would happen as soon as
Dr Bright picks another host? Would scp 096

01:44:24.670 --> 01:44:28.320
instantaneously charge at the new host and
kill it as well?”

01:44:28.320 --> 01:44:32.679
An interesting theory, and one that we’ve
explored ourselves on videos concerning Dr.

01:44:32.679 --> 01:44:36.920
Bright before. It’s another good reason
that this particular scenario should be placed

01:44:36.920 --> 01:44:41.580
on the “Dr. Bright is Not Allowed” list.
Though even though Dr. Bright does occasionally

01:44:41.580 --> 01:44:47.590
beg for death, this is one horrifying recursive
fate that even Jack himself probably wouldn’t

01:44:47.590 --> 01:44:48.590
enjoy.

01:44:48.590 --> 01:44:50.150
Here is Hon Guy’s theory:

01:44:50.150 --> 01:44:54.369
“From what I heard there are two theories,
one was the he was a mountaineer that survived

01:44:54.369 --> 01:44:58.310
an encounter with the king of the mountain
and had to adapt to the mountain to survive

01:44:58.310 --> 01:45:02.969
both from him and the mountain itself or he’s
actually one of the children of the Scarlet

01:45:02.969 --> 01:45:03.969
King.”

01:45:03.969 --> 01:45:08.630
While we’ve covered the Mountain King theory,
this Scarlet King theory is all new. Could

01:45:08.630 --> 01:45:15.170
SCP-096 be a child of the terrifying Scarlet
King? He’s certainly destructive, but perhaps

01:45:15.170 --> 01:45:21.540
he lacks the true knowing malice - the sadism
- that’d make that big, red, armored lunatic

01:45:21.540 --> 01:45:25.810
proud. He certainly doesn’t have the abject
hatred of a 682.

01:45:25.810 --> 01:45:27.110
Here is Clawed Cat’s theory:

01:45:27.110 --> 01:45:31.920
“I wouldnt be surprised if he was a species
and came to be that way as it is perfectly

01:45:31.920 --> 01:45:37.199
hidden. They blend in with the snow, and if
anyone saw its face to know it was there,

01:45:37.199 --> 01:45:40.100
they couldn’t document it for obvious reasons.”

01:45:40.100 --> 01:45:46.429
This is an interesting and terrifying concept.
A whole species of Shy Guys. This is another

01:45:46.429 --> 01:45:51.099
good reason to ardently fight against climate
change - If all the snow melts, there’ll

01:45:51.099 --> 01:45:53.610
be nowhere for them to hide anymore…

01:45:53.610 --> 01:45:55.900
Here is Dan Wolf’s theory:

01:45:55.900 --> 01:46:00.719
“The idea of "if a tree falls and no one
is around to hear it..." is his origin. He

01:46:00.719 --> 01:46:07.070
is the physical manifestation of the energy
of all the things unseen, the sound of a falling

01:46:07.070 --> 01:46:12.760
tree, the transparent skin of fish in the
abyssal zone, development on Halflife 3. All

01:46:12.760 --> 01:46:16.630
these things exist but they do so UNSEEN.

01:46:16.630 --> 01:46:22.880
SHY is physical manifestation of that metaphysical
energy, he didn't exist until he was seen

01:46:22.880 --> 01:46:29.270
thus he came to be. Existing is painful to
him so he kills anyone who sees his face,

01:46:29.270 --> 01:46:34.130
the thing we as humans use to identify one
another, in a hope to be unrecognized and

01:46:34.130 --> 01:46:37.480
unknown to eventually no longer exists.”

01:46:37.480 --> 01:46:43.550
This is a fascinating theory. Our one question
is how he could ever be seen if, by definition,

01:46:43.550 --> 01:46:50.080
he is an avatar of the unseen? What would
cause these lapses in non-existence? It raises

01:46:50.080 --> 01:46:53.770
an interesting metaphysical quandary in and
of itself…

01:46:53.770 --> 01:46:55.520
Here is Jonathan Collier’s theory:

01:46:55.520 --> 01:47:00.349
“The Shy Guy is a creation of the Plague
Doctor, who engineered him in the French Alps.

01:47:00.349 --> 01:47:05.820
He began as a human, but the doctor's process
mutated him into his current form. He designed

01:47:05.820 --> 01:47:10.561
him to destroy the pestilence, but he was
infected by it somewhere in the process. This

01:47:10.561 --> 01:47:15.080
means that whenever someone sees his face,
they catch the pestilence.

01:47:15.080 --> 01:47:20.280
His programming can't allow this, so he is
forced to destroy whoever sees him. The doctor's

01:47:20.280 --> 01:47:25.429
programming was incomplete when he was corrupted,
so he only hunts those that he has infected

01:47:25.429 --> 01:47:30.500
with the pestilence, not all that the Plague
Doctor deems infected. His indestructible

01:47:30.500 --> 01:47:35.360
form is a combination of the Plague Doctor's
tinkering and treatments as well as the doctor

01:47:35.360 --> 01:47:41.660
potentially incorporating DNA from other anomalous
creatures, such as 682, which did visit France

01:47:41.660 --> 01:47:43.680
in Lord Blackwood's tales.

01:47:43.680 --> 01:47:48.820
The doctor likely kept the DNA in reserve
for his ultimate device to destroy the pestilence,

01:47:48.820 --> 01:47:53.020
however it failed and led to him releasing
the Shy Guy to roam the mountains, traveling

01:47:53.020 --> 01:47:57.570
eventually from the Alps to the Himalayas
where he was discovered. The creature cries

01:47:57.570 --> 01:48:02.780
as it kills it's victims because of it's human
beginnings, and it's apparent anxiety is likely

01:48:02.780 --> 01:48:07.510
the lingering emotion it felt before it was
transformed, leading to it being repeated

01:48:07.510 --> 01:48:09.630
for the rest of it's existence.”

01:48:09.630 --> 01:48:14.090
We love the Plague Doctor here at SCP Explained,
so we’re always happy to consider fun potential

01:48:14.090 --> 01:48:18.790
crossovers featuring him with some of our
other favorite anomalies. The one thing working

01:48:18.790 --> 01:48:25.230
against this theory is that 096 is an uncharacteristically
effective creation for the Plague Doctor’s

01:48:25.230 --> 01:48:30.449
standards, most of which are janky, disfigured
zombies. Sorry, 049.

01:48:30.449 --> 01:48:32.090
Here is Devon Linder’s theory:

01:48:32.090 --> 01:48:37.659
“I always felt like 096 was possessed by
an evil spirit that lives in the frost, essentially

01:48:37.659 --> 01:48:43.160
the original native story of a wendigo. It
just all makes sense, deformities and strength,

01:48:43.160 --> 01:48:46.570
lives in the cold, hunts and kills people.
I don’t know if there’s anything about

01:48:46.570 --> 01:48:49.510
looking at a wendigo in the original stories
though.”

01:48:49.510 --> 01:48:55.599
The Algonquin myth of the Wendigo certainly
does have some similarities to SCP-096, however,

01:48:55.599 --> 01:49:02.349
it has a little more in common with another
anomaly - SCP-323, the Wendigo Skull, which

01:49:02.349 --> 01:49:08.360
forces its wearers into spontaneous acts of
violence and cannibalism. Though if ever 096

01:49:08.360 --> 01:49:13.170
found itself wearing the Wendigo Skull, we’d
all be in terrible trouble…

01:49:13.170 --> 01:49:15.300
Here is EpicKitty 101’s theory:

01:49:15.300 --> 01:49:20.760
“Not really a theory, it's just what I want
it to be. I believe that 096’s actions are

01:49:20.760 --> 01:49:25.760
derived from trauma. It could very well be
a species and might be a different version,

01:49:25.760 --> 01:49:30.420
like albino so whenever someone looks at its
face it feels as though that person was so

01:49:30.420 --> 01:49:36.210
inconvenienced by its looks it wants to kill
them because he thinks they hate him now.

01:49:36.210 --> 01:49:39.500
He believes he isn't good enough for anyone's
standards.”

01:49:39.500 --> 01:49:43.389
We can all relate to that feeling now and
then. And the hysteric crying and wailing

01:49:43.389 --> 01:49:49.110
of SCP-096 undeniably suggests a level of
psychological trauma. One thing we know for

01:49:49.110 --> 01:49:53.179
sure: It definitely inflicts trauma on the
rest of us.

01:49:53.179 --> 01:49:55.500
Here is Lemon Demon Danganronpa’s theory:

01:49:55.500 --> 01:50:01.570
“Hear me out: When Day Breaks was actually
a primitive event, and the Shy Guy was the

01:50:01.570 --> 01:50:05.770
result. Without any people to go around for
the next several millennia, The Shy Guy grew

01:50:05.770 --> 01:50:12.469
fond of the lack of company, ultimately turning
him into the person-or monster-he is today.”

01:50:12.469 --> 01:50:17.480
This is a fascinating one, however, there
is some pre-existing evidence - such as the

01:50:17.480 --> 01:50:22.920
tale Unexpected Light, which we’ve covered
in a video on this channel - that suggest

01:50:22.920 --> 01:50:28.620
SCP-096 survived into the reign of SCP-001
When Day Breaks. Though sadly, according to

01:50:28.620 --> 01:50:31.570
that tale, he didn’t survive beyond it.

01:50:31.570 --> 01:50:33.179
Here is Andrew Colon’s theory:

01:50:33.179 --> 01:50:37.550
“I have a theory that he was born with some
deformities and super strength and was mocked

01:50:37.550 --> 01:50:42.560
for it. As he grew older the deformities grew
more noticeable. So maybe the years of trauma

01:50:42.560 --> 01:50:48.200
and abuse or what not have caused him to self
isolate and attack violently against anyone

01:50:48.200 --> 01:50:49.490
who sees him.”

01:50:49.490 --> 01:50:55.679
This definitely reflects the internality that
many people believe SCP-096 has: A frightened,

01:50:55.679 --> 01:51:00.679
insecure creature, spiting its own creation
and anyone unfortunate enough to look upon

01:51:00.679 --> 01:51:04.829
it. Though we’ll never really know how exactly
it got there…

01:51:04.829 --> 01:51:06.989
Here is Stripeysox 101’s theory:

01:51:06.989 --> 01:51:12.190
“He could have been one of the SCP-1000
instances but born hairless and unable to

01:51:12.190 --> 01:51:14.980
think, so was abandoned by its parents.”

01:51:14.980 --> 01:51:20.440
Another good cross-anomaly connection, however,
096 is significantly more durable than any

01:51:20.440 --> 01:51:25.949
member of SCP-1000, as well as having far
more freakish proportions, so it’s unlikely

01:51:25.949 --> 01:51:29.930
to have any real relation to this advanced
species of apes.

01:51:29.930 --> 01:51:31.320
Here is Blue Iblis’ theory:

01:51:31.320 --> 01:51:37.040
“Well based on his height, his reaction
to his face being seen, and how quick he is...

01:51:37.040 --> 01:51:42.080
SCP-096 is literally just an IRL Enderman.
They're both tall, they're both able to move

01:51:42.080 --> 01:51:46.800
quickly, and become instantly pissed if you
look at their face/eyes. This probably means

01:51:46.800 --> 01:51:51.280
096 is maaaybe from another dimension, but
its just a theory.”

01:51:51.280 --> 01:51:56.199
We might dismiss a potential Minecraft influence
out of hand, were it not for the existence

01:51:56.199 --> 01:52:03.430
of SCP-4335, also known as A Welt In The Crucible,
an evil Minecraft monster. However, this monster

01:52:03.430 --> 01:52:08.219
was actually the one responsible for the Ender
Men in Minecraft due to a Foundation-inspired

01:52:08.219 --> 01:52:11.889
patch, sadly invalidating this theory.

01:52:11.889 --> 01:52:13.650
Here is James Bevan’s theory:

01:52:13.650 --> 01:52:18.840
“My theory is that the Shy Guy is a demon
summoned by a cult that dwelled in the mountains.

01:52:18.840 --> 01:52:22.770
They hoped to control him, but were unaware
that the entity reacts violently whenever

01:52:22.770 --> 01:52:25.909
anyone sees its face and all met a grisly
death.”

01:52:25.909 --> 01:52:32.980
SCP-096 is undeniably rather… Demonic, so
we couldn’t argue with this theory on that

01:52:32.980 --> 01:52:37.219
front. It’s also very likely that any cult
who unwittingly summoned him, without the

01:52:37.219 --> 01:52:42.270
knowledge of his primary anomalous effect,
would meet a truly gruesome death at his claws

01:52:42.270 --> 01:52:46.190
and teeth, so you may be on to something here,
James.

01:52:46.190 --> 01:52:48.030
Here is Harrison O’Leary’s theory:

01:52:48.030 --> 01:52:52.300
“Well, when he left the mushroom kingdom,
he obviously lost his mask and clothes.”

01:52:52.300 --> 01:52:59.020
The idea that the Shy Guy is literally, well,
a Shy Guy, is certainly an intriguing one

01:52:59.020 --> 01:53:04.480
- though how 096’s long, gangly body could
fit into those tiny, compact outfits is an

01:53:04.480 --> 01:53:09.210
open question. If only Researcher Mario and
Researcher Luigi would take some time off

01:53:09.210 --> 01:53:18.329
of their extremely busy schedules to help
us out with this one…

01:53:18.329 --> 01:53:19.900
Here is Roninski’s theory:

01:53:19.900 --> 01:53:25.100
“He was a very handsome man who was experimented
on, and now goes into violent fits of rage

01:53:25.100 --> 01:53:28.200
when people look at his now disfigured face.”

01:53:28.200 --> 01:53:35.320
Hmm. Is it just us, or is the idea of a strangely
handsome SCP-096 somehow even more disturbing

01:53:35.320 --> 01:53:39.060
than the one we already have? Just feels so
unnatural…

01:53:39.060 --> 01:53:46.710
He’s waiting for you in the dark. He sits,
cross-legged, still as a placid lake and patient

01:53:46.710 --> 01:53:52.900
as a praying monk six feet deep in meditation.
He’s been like this for so long. His body

01:53:52.900 --> 01:54:00.680
oil-slicked with deadly black grease, skin
ancient and haggard, eyes black and flat with

01:54:00.680 --> 01:54:06.840
the kind of calm you only find in crawling
insects and Great White Sharks. His lipless

01:54:06.840 --> 01:54:13.900
mouth is stretched across his jawbone in an
ugly, black rictus. He may be an Old Man,

01:54:13.900 --> 01:54:18.440
but trust us, he’s got plenty of new tricks
he’d love to show you.

01:54:18.440 --> 01:54:25.280
He sat in the loneliest place in the world:
The deep, dark bowels of an SCP Foundation

01:54:25.280 --> 01:54:30.270
containment site, surrounded by a complex
confluence of containment measures: Different

01:54:30.270 --> 01:54:36.080
shapes, running liquids, bright lights, the
kind of steel so thick a nuclear weapon would

01:54:36.080 --> 01:54:42.380
only leave it scuffed. All the things that
had a tendency to... Confuse him. Because

01:54:42.380 --> 01:54:44.099
that’s all they ever did.

01:54:44.099 --> 01:54:49.360
The Old Man laughed to himself, very quietly.
All the people out there are so scared of

01:54:49.360 --> 01:54:56.020
him because of one immutable fact: He isn’t
trapped here, he’s just resting. By virtue

01:54:56.020 --> 01:55:00.599
of his unique anomalous abilities - to pass
through solids at will and resist any form

01:55:00.599 --> 01:55:06.460
of physical damage they can dole out - there
is no way to really contain him like all the

01:55:06.460 --> 01:55:11.739
others. All the SCP Foundation could hope
to do was make leaving inconvenient enough

01:55:11.739 --> 01:55:17.170
for him to make his little adventures on the
outside less appealing than just sitting around,

01:55:17.170 --> 01:55:18.659
doing nothing.

01:55:18.659 --> 01:55:24.079
After all, he had all the time in the world,
and when you always have a “Later”, there’s

01:55:24.079 --> 01:55:26.510
a lot less pressure to do anything now.

01:55:26.510 --> 01:55:33.441
But like all thinking creatures - And please,
be aware that SCP-106, the wretched, terrifying

01:55:33.441 --> 01:55:39.409
Old Man, is very much a thinking creature
- he was subject to occasional boredom. And

01:55:39.409 --> 01:55:43.950
when most people are bored, they might play
a video game, watch a few YouTube videos,

01:55:43.950 --> 01:55:49.270
or rewatch It’s Always Sunny In Philadelphia
for the fifth time. But for the Old Man, killing

01:55:49.270 --> 01:55:55.421
boredom involved... Well, killing people.
But not just killing people, of course. Truth

01:55:55.421 --> 01:56:00.699
be told, for him, killing was really the least
entertaining part of the whole affair.

01:56:00.699 --> 01:56:08.610
The screams? The begging? The desperate pleas?
Warm tears on shaking cheeks? Convulsions

01:56:08.610 --> 01:56:15.980
of pain? Cold sweats? Fear so intense it gives
way to gibbering madness? That dull sheen

01:56:15.980 --> 01:56:20.790
of dead hope in their eyes when they then
realize that their doom is inevitable, and

01:56:20.790 --> 01:56:28.030
that he, their captor, their torturer, would
decide the exact moment they slipped into

01:56:28.030 --> 01:56:29.030
the void?

01:56:29.030 --> 01:56:35.250
Oh, that was what he lived for - If you could
call what the Old Man did Living.

01:56:35.250 --> 01:56:40.489
He cracked his neck and licked his teeth.
Was he salivating? All those thoughts about

01:56:40.489 --> 01:56:45.869
pain seemed to have got him worked up. He
could feel the distant thumping of his heart.

01:56:45.869 --> 01:56:51.659
Old, black blood pumping through the dusty
corridors of his vascular system. Synapses

01:56:51.659 --> 01:56:57.350
booming and sparking with excitement at the
thought of plumbing new depths of human misery,

01:56:57.350 --> 01:57:04.430
like a scholar of suffering, a pioneer of
pain. All those silly Foundation drones, with

01:57:04.430 --> 01:57:09.079
the audacity to think they’re in control.
He’d teach them to scream again...

01:57:09.079 --> 01:57:13.670
Meanwhile, in the control room for the Old
Man’s containment chamber, all hell was

01:57:13.670 --> 01:57:19.000
breaking loose. Monitors on the monster’s
vitals were all spiking: Heart and brain activity

01:57:19.000 --> 01:57:24.079
peaked, just as they always did before the
Old Man decided to get active. Cameras in

01:57:24.079 --> 01:57:29.510
his containment chamber showed him rising
steadily to his feet, cracking his joints,

01:57:29.510 --> 01:57:35.000
limbering up. His body started to produce
that terrible corrosive mucus at an accelerated

01:57:35.000 --> 01:57:40.270
rate, pooling around his feet and unleashing
squalls of noxious fumes as it melted into

01:57:40.270 --> 01:57:41.329
the floor.

01:57:41.329 --> 01:57:46.290
Years of Foundation testing, and they still
couldn’t find any material that could withstand

01:57:46.290 --> 01:57:48.460
that vile black slop...

01:57:48.460 --> 01:57:52.179
As the Old Man began strolling towards the
wall, a containment specialist in the control

01:57:52.179 --> 01:57:57.099
room grabbed a nearby phone and sounded the
alarm for the containment breach. They needed

01:57:57.099 --> 01:58:02.820
a Mobile Task Force and every guard on site
mobilized to tackle 106 before he got into

01:58:02.820 --> 01:58:07.949
the facility proper and started causing havoc.
They couldn’t risk it escaping, either - The

01:58:07.949 --> 01:58:12.389
infamous incident when the Old Man escaped
for a Halloween massacre still haunted the

01:58:12.389 --> 01:58:17.579
nightmares of many a staff member. He had
to be stopped - Now!

01:58:17.579 --> 01:58:22.770
A small fighting force was quickly armed and
assembled, along with armor and several high-intensity

01:58:22.770 --> 01:58:27.750
portable lights to hopefully slow the monster
down in time for them to grab a D-Class and

01:58:27.750 --> 01:58:32.070
set up the femur breaker. By the time they
all reached the hallway they intended to use

01:58:32.070 --> 01:58:36.449
as a choke point for the escape, the Old Man
was already half way through the outer layer

01:58:36.449 --> 01:58:42.650
of defence, phasing through the wall with
that same terrible, manic grin he always had

01:58:42.650 --> 01:58:46.330
when he was about to inflict terrible violence
for the sheer fun of it.

01:58:46.330 --> 01:58:51.119
The guards and task force members trained
their lights and weapons on him. All of them

01:58:51.119 --> 01:58:55.820
had seen terrible things on the job - the
kind of things that would saddle almost anyone

01:58:55.820 --> 01:59:00.949
with a lifetime of trauma - but almost nothing
compared to the horror of direct engagement

01:59:00.949 --> 01:59:08.030
during a 106 containment breach. That terrible
old coffin dodger was Hell on two legs.

01:59:08.030 --> 01:59:12.000
When they discovered, to their horror, that
the lights didn’t seem to be slowing him

01:59:12.000 --> 01:59:18.639
down as much as anticipated, they fell back
on Plan B: That B standing for, “Blast the

01:59:18.639 --> 01:59:24.320
living hell out of him with everything they
had.” The personnel opened fire, pumping

01:59:24.320 --> 01:59:29.361
round after round into his dark form. While
each gave a loud, meaty splatter, the Old

01:59:29.361 --> 01:59:34.610
Man didn’t even flinch. When he was close
enough, all those months of lethargy gave

01:59:34.610 --> 01:59:37.130
way to sudden, deadly speed.

01:59:37.130 --> 01:59:43.190
In many ways, the Old Man was like a crocodile.
A fierce and intelligent ambush predator who

01:59:43.190 --> 01:59:48.290
conserved his energy wisely. He could seem
so deceptively languid that you could easily

01:59:48.290 --> 01:59:53.470
mistake him for something slow and harmless.
But when you got within arm’s reach, the

01:59:53.470 --> 01:59:58.510
sudden quickness and ferocity would take you
off guard. And by then, it’s already too

01:59:58.510 --> 01:59:59.670
late.

01:59:59.670 --> 02:00:04.110
Within thirty seconds, the Old Man was the
only thing left alive in that hallway. All

02:00:04.110 --> 02:00:09.020
the guards and Task Force operatives sent
to slow him down lay dead, dripping with his

02:00:09.020 --> 02:00:15.080
black mucus. There was silence, save for the
quiet fizzing of the mucus eating into their

02:00:15.080 --> 02:00:22.030
clothes and skin. The Old Man sighed. How
boring. He’d killed so many people now,

02:00:22.030 --> 02:00:27.300
it all started to seem a little... Samey.
How many screams could you hear before they

02:00:27.300 --> 02:00:32.690
all just blended into a single shrill whine?
You can get sick of even your favorite meal

02:00:32.690 --> 02:00:34.340
if you eat it too often.

02:00:34.340 --> 02:00:40.080
But that didn’t matter. The Old Man had
a different order of business today: He happened

02:00:40.080 --> 02:00:45.179
to be inside an SCP Foundation containment
facility, filled with so many fascinating

02:00:45.179 --> 02:00:52.170
specimens of non-human entities that the one
they called SCP-106 simply couldn’t wait

02:00:52.170 --> 02:00:57.511
to torture. He’d only met a small portion
of the ones they had contained here, but given

02:00:57.511 --> 02:01:02.739
what a fascinating experience his past attack
on his fellow anomalies was, he couldn’t

02:01:02.739 --> 02:01:04.550
wait to meet some more.

02:01:04.550 --> 02:01:08.710
Meanwhile, the site’s top brass did all
they could to try to mobilize a second response

02:01:08.710 --> 02:01:13.860
to the rogue senior citizen from hell currently
strolling through their high-security containment

02:01:13.860 --> 02:01:18.150
facility. Could they get in contact with other
Mobile Task Forces deployed in the area? Maybe

02:01:18.150 --> 02:01:22.829
they could get Hammer Down or Samsara in on
this mess before anything got really out of

02:01:22.829 --> 02:01:28.620
hand? The Old Man seemed more intent on something
this time, they suspected even the femur breaker

02:01:28.620 --> 02:01:34.050
might not work in luring him back until he
claimed whatever he deemed to be his prize.

02:01:34.050 --> 02:01:36.969
Maybe they should contact the O5 Council...

02:01:36.969 --> 02:01:41.360
Security guards were tracking 106’s progress
through the facility via their vast networks

02:01:41.360 --> 02:01:46.429
of security cameras. They warned any research,
administration, and janitorial staff to clear

02:01:46.429 --> 02:01:51.040
out of his path, unless they felt like being
tortured to death. He hadn’t dragged anyone

02:01:51.040 --> 02:01:56.290
into his pocket dimension yet, so whoever
that privilege was reserved for would probably

02:01:56.290 --> 02:02:02.520
be getting a particular sticky experience.
They could only speculate as to the silent

02:02:02.520 --> 02:02:04.110
beast’s intentions.

02:02:04.110 --> 02:02:09.750
One guard noticed something. When cross-referencing
106’s movements with a map of the facility,

02:02:09.750 --> 02:02:14.770
they were able to triangulate where he was
heading: Straight for the containment chamber

02:02:14.770 --> 02:02:21.250
of SCP-096. The guard’s lips curled into
a slight smile, thinking, “If one of you

02:02:21.250 --> 02:02:26.410
freaks can actually kill the other, maybe
something positive will come out of this disaster...”

02:02:26.410 --> 02:02:31.360
The Old Man was having a stroll down memory
lane. The time prior, when he broke out of

02:02:31.360 --> 02:02:35.829
his cell and decided to hassle his fellow
intimates was certainly eventful - He wound

02:02:35.829 --> 02:02:41.360
up in the company of some cantankerous reptile
and a Korean fox woman, an unlikely pair if

02:02:41.360 --> 02:02:45.660
ever there was one. After finding himself
in a room with them, he decided that they

02:02:45.660 --> 02:02:50.650
should have a little more privacy, and dragged
them back into his pocket dimension for further

02:02:50.650 --> 02:02:51.719
fun.

02:02:51.719 --> 02:02:56.239
Of course, with two at once, his attention
had been a little split. They spent as much

02:02:56.239 --> 02:03:01.560
time tussling with each other as resisting
his attempts to torment them. They’d definitely

02:03:01.560 --> 02:03:07.110
put him through his paces that day, and he
gave far better than he got: After so many

02:03:07.110 --> 02:03:12.070
hours of brutal mutilation, there was barely
any of the lizard left, despite its frustrating

02:03:12.070 --> 02:03:18.599
insistence on quickly healing from so many
of its wounds. Still, it was good exercise,

02:03:18.599 --> 02:03:24.630
even if the results had been less than satisfying.
If he could get another anomaly alone, though,

02:03:24.630 --> 02:03:30.000
he’d be able to take his time, and really
savor the pain he caused.

02:03:30.000 --> 02:03:32.730
Oh, that sounded heavenly...

02:03:32.730 --> 02:03:38.690
Soon, he came upon an incredibly strange containment
chamber that quickly caught his eye: It looked

02:03:38.690 --> 02:03:45.380
like a huge, metal cube, with no way in or
out. Not even a seam. Whatever was trapped

02:03:45.380 --> 02:03:50.159
in there, the Old Man thought, it must be
something that even the fools at the SCP Foundation

02:03:50.159 --> 02:03:56.159
really, truly wanted nothing to do with. But
even more so than the strange sight, it was

02:03:56.159 --> 02:04:01.119
the sound that drew the Old Man in, tempting
him like a siren song.

02:04:01.119 --> 02:04:07.570
It was weeping. A soft, sobbing keen that
betrayed a bottomless well of misery. Whatever

02:04:07.570 --> 02:04:13.329
was making that crying sound had known true
despair. Or, whatever passes for true despair

02:04:13.329 --> 02:04:17.511
before you’ve had a close encounter with
the Old Man in his pocket dimension. The Old

02:04:17.511 --> 02:04:23.710
Man grinned, and thought, “Oh, sonny, I’ll
give you something to really cry about...”

02:04:23.710 --> 02:04:29.290
He stepped forward and phased his way into
the metal cube. The creature was in the corner.

02:04:29.290 --> 02:04:35.560
In contrast to that stupid lizard, this monster
was pleasingly humanoid, with, presumably,

02:04:35.560 --> 02:04:41.130
all the familiar pain points. Splendid. The
only real difference was color and proportion:

02:04:41.130 --> 02:04:46.640
It was far longer and ganglier than an average
human, with greyish skin and no hair anywhere

02:04:46.640 --> 02:04:51.429
on its body. Still, it was something the Old
Man could work with. The creature didn’t

02:04:51.429 --> 02:04:56.739
even seem to register his presence, it just
sat crumpled in the corner, weeping.

02:04:56.739 --> 02:05:01.369
Still grinning his lunatic grin, the Old Man
approached and rested a moist hand on the

02:05:01.369 --> 02:05:05.190
creature’s shoulder. Its pale skin began
to fizzle from the intruder’s corrosive

02:05:05.190 --> 02:05:10.380
touch. A pool of vile sludge began spreading
out beneath his feet until the other creature

02:05:10.380 --> 02:05:15.690
was encompassed too. Little by little, they
sank into the Old Man’s favorite place out

02:05:15.690 --> 02:05:22.390
of the world: The Pocket Dimension, his own
personal, eternal, infinite dungeon, where

02:05:22.390 --> 02:05:27.820
the only laws are pain and terror for all
but the Old Man himself.

02:05:27.820 --> 02:05:33.369
Little did he know, this time, the scores
may be a little more even on that front.

02:05:33.369 --> 02:05:38.120
There they both stood, in that terrible labyrinth
of hallways and chambers where the Old Man

02:05:38.120 --> 02:05:43.170
had made countless lives infinitely worse
than any terrible afterlife that could follow

02:05:43.170 --> 02:05:48.440
them, and still, this creature didn’t seem
to change its reaction. He was almost irritated

02:05:48.440 --> 02:05:53.409
by it. And what’s more, this whole time,
the creature hadn’t even turned to look

02:05:53.409 --> 02:05:56.309
at him. He’d have to fix that, too.

02:05:56.309 --> 02:06:01.409
The Old Man, with his shocking strength, gripped
the monster’s shoulder and whipped him around,

02:06:01.409 --> 02:06:07.800
staring into his face, bearing his black eyes
and manic smile. But he couldn’t help find

02:06:07.800 --> 02:06:11.320
what looked back at him... Disconcerting.

02:06:11.320 --> 02:06:17.369
Its eyes were blank and white. Its face, stretched
and warped into a permanent grimace, like

02:06:17.369 --> 02:06:22.610
Munch’s old painting, The Scream. And the
second the Old Man laid eyes on it, that’s

02:06:22.610 --> 02:06:27.500
exactly what it did. Its gaping mouth let
out the most terrible wail the Old Man had

02:06:27.500 --> 02:06:32.849
ever heard in all his years of torturing.
It was a scream of unknowable sadness and

02:06:32.849 --> 02:06:38.739
fear. But somehow, the Old Man sensed that
this creature didn’t fear him. It feared

02:06:38.739 --> 02:06:44.989
itself. Feared what it was about to do. What
the hell had he dragged down here with him?

02:06:44.989 --> 02:06:49.420
Without warning, the terrible wails soon gave
way to sudden and ferocious aggression - Just

02:06:49.420 --> 02:06:53.381
like the Old Man had dealt out to a group
of Foundation guards and Mobile Task Force

02:06:53.381 --> 02:06:59.270
agents earlier that same night. It was only
out of a mix of pure luck and his own finely-tuned

02:06:59.270 --> 02:07:04.800
animal instinct that the Old Man was able
to dodge the first strike from the creature’s

02:07:04.800 --> 02:07:06.730
long, lithe forelimb.

02:07:06.730 --> 02:07:11.630
He couldn’t believe it. They were in his
special place, and this victim of his had

02:07:11.630 --> 02:07:16.380
the audacity to fight back? Well, he’d soon
put this monster in its place...

02:07:16.380 --> 02:07:20.619
As the monster continued roaring and swiping
for him, the Old Man simply walked backwards

02:07:20.619 --> 02:07:26.650
into a nearby wall, disappearing into a black
stain. Of course, the monster - better known

02:07:26.650 --> 02:07:32.460
to the SCP Foundation As SCP-096, or The Shy
Guy, simply crashed through the wall to give

02:07:32.460 --> 02:07:38.150
chase. But on the other side... Just another
wall. He’d disappeared. How?

02:07:38.150 --> 02:07:42.179
Typically, when something sees the face of
the Shy Guy, he immediately has a kind of

02:07:42.179 --> 02:07:47.800
intuitive connection to them. He can sense
their presence, it’s burned into his mind,

02:07:47.800 --> 02:07:53.639
a homing beacon, an unstoppable imperative.
He knows exactly where they are and how to

02:07:53.639 --> 02:07:58.550
get to them. But nothing about this situation
was typical. The Shy Guy felt all the murderous

02:07:58.550 --> 02:08:04.650
rage he normally did, but the rest was just
static. Where was the life he needed to abolish

02:08:04.650 --> 02:08:05.650
hiding?

02:08:05.650 --> 02:08:10.630
Suddenly, the Old Man emerged behind him,
and forced his hand into the Shy Guy’s back.

02:08:10.630 --> 02:08:14.809
His fist melted straight through flesh and
skin, causing the creature to let out another

02:08:14.809 --> 02:08:19.460
terrible wail, but as could be expected, they
didn’t leave a scratch on the Shy Guy’s

02:08:19.460 --> 02:08:21.489
indestructible skeleton.

02:08:21.489 --> 02:08:25.389
The Shy Guy pulled a one-eighty and swung
for the Old Man again, but he was already

02:08:25.389 --> 02:08:30.559
gone. Here in the Pocket Dimension, the Old
Man had the ultimate home-turf advantage.

02:08:30.559 --> 02:08:35.660
Here, space and time were his playthings.
In the infinite twisting, labyrinthine walls

02:08:35.660 --> 02:08:41.389
of the Pocket Dimension, the Old Man wasn’t
just the Devil, he was God, too.

02:08:41.389 --> 02:08:44.960
But it would take far more than some cheap
tricks and bodily harm to dissuade the Shy

02:08:44.960 --> 02:08:49.679
Guy from his sole purpose in life. It began
running through the Old Man’s personal maze

02:08:49.679 --> 02:08:54.369
like a crazed lab rat, tearing through wall
after wall, forever searching for the monster

02:08:54.369 --> 02:08:59.790
that trapped it here. It would see the smiling
face of that rotten old man every so often,

02:08:59.790 --> 02:09:04.530
the flash of black eyes and dirty teeth in
shadows, but every time it tried to strike

02:09:04.530 --> 02:09:07.270
the shadows, there would be nothing there.

02:09:07.270 --> 02:09:11.170
And every time he got a chance, the Old Man
would emerge from the shadows behind the Shy

02:09:11.170 --> 02:09:16.270
Guy to strike back, clawing into his flesh
and vanishing again before the trapped beast

02:09:16.270 --> 02:09:23.090
could retaliate. The nasty old sadist was
now starting to have some fun: It was a reflex

02:09:23.090 --> 02:09:27.590
test, a classic game of high-stakes Whack
A Mole. And every time the creature failed

02:09:27.590 --> 02:09:33.219
to strike him, its distress only seemed to
grow. This was perfectly fine for the Old

02:09:33.219 --> 02:09:34.340
Man.

02:09:34.340 --> 02:09:38.489
After all, he enjoyed a little mental torment
as much as he loved physical torture, and

02:09:38.489 --> 02:09:43.980
a human would have died or given in to despair
long before now. This monster’s wonderful

02:09:43.980 --> 02:09:47.690
mix of resilience and pain made it a great
chew toy.

02:09:47.690 --> 02:09:52.530
The Old Man kept up this routine, baiting
and striking, until something unanticipated

02:09:52.530 --> 02:09:58.159
happened. He appeared, waited for the swing,
disappeared as it approached, then reappeared

02:09:58.159 --> 02:10:03.030
behind the Shy Guy to deliver another painful
strike. This time, however, it seemed his

02:10:03.030 --> 02:10:08.230
victim had anticipated his pattern. Before
he could hit the creature, the Shy Guy turned,

02:10:08.230 --> 02:10:12.250
quicker than anything the Old Man had ever
seen, and hit him with such force he flew

02:10:12.250 --> 02:10:16.070
through several walls in his own maze, shattering
them as he went.

02:10:16.070 --> 02:10:21.800
He hit the ground, stunned by the fact the
Shy Guy had actually landed a hit, but he

02:10:21.800 --> 02:10:26.909
wasn’t done. The shrieking monster came
bounding after him. Before the Old Man could

02:10:26.909 --> 02:10:33.059
stand, it struck him again, and again, and
again, pounding his vile body into the ground,

02:10:33.059 --> 02:10:38.190
which cracked and crumbled. The Old Man sneered
and sank into a puddle of his mucus on the

02:10:38.190 --> 02:10:39.190
ground.

02:10:39.190 --> 02:10:45.690
The Old Man was gone again. SCP-096 screamed
in a mix of rage, despair, and frustration,

02:10:45.690 --> 02:10:49.960
so consumed with its hunt that it didn’t
notice the pool of corrosive mucus was slowly

02:10:49.960 --> 02:10:57.290
growing. It began to rise, bubbling and hissing
at SCP-096’s feet. Getting deeper and deeper

02:10:57.290 --> 02:11:02.930
and deeper, as the walls rose taller and taller
around it. The creature wailed, until a tide

02:11:02.930 --> 02:11:09.330
of the awful stuff reached its face and cascaded
down its throat, melting all the organs within.

02:11:09.330 --> 02:11:15.320
As the creature was fully submerged, SCP-106
manifested atop the wall, and looked down,

02:11:15.320 --> 02:11:16.679
smiling spitefully.

02:11:16.679 --> 02:11:27.820
He thought, oh, you may think you had me there,
boy-o, but in the end, I always win. Nothing

02:11:27.820 --> 02:11:28.820
could survive that!

02:11:28.820 --> 02:11:32.219
And yet, bubbles still rose from the slop
below. The Old Man gritted his rotten teeth

02:11:32.219 --> 02:11:35.210
and balled his fists in quiet anger.

02:11:35.210 --> 02:11:40.469
Moments later, back in our dimension, a portal
opened in the ground of SCP-096’s containment

02:11:40.469 --> 02:11:48.780
chamber. It vomited out SCP-096’s skeleton,
smothered in bubbling black goo, and SCP-106.

02:11:48.780 --> 02:11:54.159
096 would probably be fully regenerated within
a couple of hours - and if 682’s encounter

02:11:54.159 --> 02:11:59.890
with him was anything to go by, probably extremely
unwilling to interface with the Old Man. And

02:11:59.890 --> 02:12:04.739
the Old Man himself, who couldn’t help but
feel a little humiliated by the whole experience,

02:12:04.739 --> 02:12:07.219
was glad the two of them would probably never
meet again.

02:12:07.219 --> 02:12:12.230
He left the regenerating 096 and commenced
the walk of shame back to his containment

02:12:12.230 --> 02:12:16.869
chamber. They didn’t even need the femur
breaker, he just felt like having a sit-down

02:12:16.869 --> 02:12:21.579
and processing it all. Nobody else would ever
know what happened in the pocket dimension

02:12:21.579 --> 02:12:26.740
that day, and for the Old Man’s pride, that
was probably for the best. But he’d know.

02:12:26.740 --> 02:12:28.159
He’d always know.

02:12:28.159 --> 02:12:32.970
By the night’s end, he was sitting in his
containment chamber again, forlornly wondering,

02:12:32.970 --> 02:12:36.730
“Goddamn it, old-timer, have you lost your
edge?”

02:12:36.730 --> 02:12:43.869
What do you do, when the sun turns wrong?
When light, which once represented life, joy,

02:12:43.869 --> 02:12:51.070
and warmth becomes a symbol of death and destruction?
Do you hide inside, shutter the windows, keep

02:12:51.070 --> 02:12:56.400
out the light by any means necessary? Do you
retreat underground, where the sun’s rays

02:12:56.400 --> 02:13:01.660
cannot penetrate, burrowing into the dark
and the cold? Do you simply give yourself

02:13:01.660 --> 02:13:09.440
to it, accept that there is no escaping something
so vast, so far-reaching as sunlight itself,

02:13:09.440 --> 02:13:13.110
and allow yourself to be lost in the end of
all things?

02:13:13.110 --> 02:13:20.739
We’ve covered this possibility before, SCP-001,
“When Day Breaks”, or S.D. Locke’s Proposal.

02:13:20.739 --> 02:13:26.130
It’s difficult to know what any given person
might do in such harrowing circumstances.

02:13:26.130 --> 02:13:30.500
After all, we have always learned to love
the light and fear the darkness. It turns

02:13:30.500 --> 02:13:36.820
our very understanding of reality upside down
to have that paradigm shifted, the dark becoming

02:13:36.820 --> 02:13:44.880
safety and the light becoming death. The source
of all life on earth, of all warmth, transforming

02:13:44.880 --> 02:13:51.329
in an instant from the sun as we know it to
an Apollyon Class SCP that melts all living

02:13:51.329 --> 02:13:56.389
organisms that cross into its gaze.
But what about a being that has always feared

02:13:56.389 --> 02:14:02.780
the light? Something that prioritizes solitude,
and never ever wants to be seen? How might

02:14:02.780 --> 02:14:06.930
it live out those darkened days?
Today, we’re taking a look at what happens

02:14:06.930 --> 02:14:15.150
when Daybreak meets a familiar face, even
if a constantly hidden one: SCP-096.

02:14:15.150 --> 02:14:21.380
SCP-096, or “The Shy Guy,” is something
of a celebrity around here. It is a humanoid

02:14:21.380 --> 02:14:27.130
creature with long, distended arms, pale skin,
and a jaw that can open four times wider than

02:14:27.130 --> 02:14:34.239
an ordinary human’s. There is only one thing,
above all else, that this entity desires:

02:14:34.239 --> 02:14:40.730
for no one to see its face. When someone views
its face, it flies into an uncontrollable

02:14:40.730 --> 02:14:45.590
rage, and seems to have no other option but
to destroy the person who has seen it.

02:14:45.590 --> 02:14:50.980
So, it is unlikely that a creature so intent
on solitude would be in much danger from the

02:14:50.980 --> 02:14:57.480
transformed sun. In fact, it might even be
a positive thing, and for some time it was.

02:14:57.480 --> 02:15:02.830
With so many human beings gone, reduced to
gelatinous masses with no desire but to slither

02:15:02.830 --> 02:15:07.639
around in the ghoulish sunlight and merge
with one another, there were fewer people

02:15:07.639 --> 02:15:14.340
to observe his face than ever. He could stay
inside and know, with almost complete certainty,

02:15:14.340 --> 02:15:19.770
that no one would come to bother him. No one
was coming to look at him, and there were

02:15:19.770 --> 02:15:25.739
no more visitors, researchers poking and prodding,
that might sneak a look at his face.

02:15:25.739 --> 02:15:31.070
And so, he stayed there, in the room that
had served as his containment unit before

02:15:31.070 --> 02:15:36.830
the world turned upside down. The masses of
flesh that once were humans did not concern

02:15:36.830 --> 02:15:42.420
him, as they never looked at his face. All
he did was stay in his room, and never go

02:15:42.420 --> 02:15:47.170
outside. The outside frightened him terribly,
though he was not concerned with the deadly

02:15:47.170 --> 02:15:52.240
sunlight or the possibility of it melting
him as it had so many others. He was much

02:15:52.240 --> 02:15:58.510
more afraid that someone, some unlikely survivor
clinging to their original life, might look

02:15:58.510 --> 02:16:05.119
at him. That they might see his face. That
was simply too distressing to risk. He would

02:16:05.119 --> 02:16:10.570
much rather stay alone, in his old cell, weeping
softly in the shadows.

02:16:10.570 --> 02:16:15.210
One day, though we couldn’t say how many
days after the end of things it had been,

02:16:15.210 --> 02:16:20.770
he was weeping alone in his former cell. The
days blurred together, spent the same way,

02:16:20.770 --> 02:16:26.430
and time had lost much of its meaning. Through
the echoing sound of his sobs, and the sloshing

02:16:26.430 --> 02:16:32.200
sounds of the creatures outside, a woman’s
voice cut through. It was faint, but he could

02:16:32.200 --> 02:16:37.440
make out what she was saying. She was calling
out for help, asking if anyone had any food

02:16:37.440 --> 02:16:43.200
or fresh water. The sound was getting louder,
closer, accompanied by the disconcerting thump

02:16:43.200 --> 02:16:48.400
of her footsteps. She was coming down the
hall, as much as it could be called a hall

02:16:48.400 --> 02:16:53.700
anymore. She was coming toward his room, to
disrupt his miserable peace.

02:16:53.700 --> 02:16:58.650
He heard her steps cross the threshold of
his room, and her voice broke into a terrified

02:16:58.650 --> 02:17:03.170
scream, no doubt at the unexpected sight of
a pale, naked creature weeping in the corner

02:17:03.170 --> 02:17:08.280
of what she expected to be an empty room.
Startled by the first loud sound he’d heard

02:17:08.280 --> 02:17:13.120
in a long time, in a world that had mostly
been silent since the initial outpouring of

02:17:13.120 --> 02:17:18.580
agonized screams had faded into the soft slap
of flesh and slime on the ground, he turned

02:17:18.580 --> 02:17:22.030
around.
He shouldn’t, ordinarily he wouldn’t,

02:17:22.030 --> 02:17:28.081
but he couldn’t stop himself. There, he
saw a woman, wide eyes filled with tears,

02:17:28.081 --> 02:17:33.350
face streaked with dirt, a small child next
to her holding her hand and clinging to her

02:17:33.350 --> 02:17:40.430
leg. Just as he saw the woman, she saw him.
She saw…his face. Anger boiled over inside

02:17:40.430 --> 02:17:49.180
of him, and he let out a scream, wild, primal,
and filled with inhuman rage. She could not

02:17:49.180 --> 02:17:54.381
be looking at him, seeing his face. He couldn’t
let her.

02:17:54.381 --> 02:17:58.940
The woman ran with the child in tow, panicked
by the wild roar and the sight of him standing

02:17:58.940 --> 02:18:04.530
upright before her. The anger faded as she
left his site, making her way down the hall

02:18:04.530 --> 02:18:10.320
and to the stairs. But then, he remembered:
she had seen his face. He could not let her

02:18:10.320 --> 02:18:16.410
leave. It was time to do something he never
thought he’d have to do again: hunt. His

02:18:16.410 --> 02:18:20.860
heightened senses picked up the sound of muffled
footsteps, coming up through the floor below

02:18:20.860 --> 02:18:25.530
him. She was downstairs, just beneath the
floor where he was standing.

02:18:25.530 --> 02:18:30.310
His instincts kicked in, and his body moved
almost of its own accord. He lifted his long,

02:18:30.310 --> 02:18:36.730
long arms above his head, and drove his hands
down into the floor. It was weakened by the

02:18:36.730 --> 02:18:42.940
ravages of the elements and time, and crumbled
under his unnatural strength. He dug his claws

02:18:42.940 --> 02:18:49.260
into the plaster, tearing it apart and cracking
it into pieces. He pushed his claws in, deeper

02:18:49.260 --> 02:18:54.450
and deeper, until he could feel them breaking
through to the other side. He pulled the floor

02:18:54.450 --> 02:18:59.810
apart until the hole was wide enough to fit
through, then dropped down to the room below.

02:18:59.810 --> 02:19:04.000
He spotted the woman’s back, fleeing through
the door, just as he landed.

02:19:04.000 --> 02:19:07.950
She was getting away!
He tore after her, ripping through concrete

02:19:07.950 --> 02:19:13.301
and steel, destroying everything in his path
in a mad dash for the object of his rage.

02:19:13.301 --> 02:19:19.250
He had to catch her, she could not be allowed
to leave. She looked over her shoulder at

02:19:19.250 --> 02:19:25.290
him, her face a mask of pure terror. She knew,
just as he knew, that there was no escape

02:19:25.290 --> 02:19:30.930
in sight. In his single-minded obsession,
and her desperation to escape from him, neither

02:19:30.930 --> 02:19:35.800
of them noticed that she had run so far and
so carelessly that she was now outside of

02:19:35.800 --> 02:19:41.070
the building. She had crossed from the shadows,
into the harsh light of the sun.

02:19:41.070 --> 02:19:47.240
The woman’s eyes widened in grim understanding
of her fate. He could only watch, helplessly,

02:19:47.240 --> 02:19:53.400
as her body began to succumb to the vicious
rays. Almost instantly, she began to melt.

02:19:53.400 --> 02:19:58.120
As it was the closest to the sun, her head
was the first to go. Her nose drooped like

02:19:58.120 --> 02:20:03.840
hot wax, dripping as it went until it slumped
off her face and landed on the ground with

02:20:03.840 --> 02:20:10.300
a sickening splash. Her eyes were next, popping
out of their sockets and hanging, loose and

02:20:10.300 --> 02:20:14.380
limp, on her cheeks and dangling from sinewy
strands.

02:20:14.380 --> 02:20:19.190
She tried, in vain, to stuff them back into
place, but they squished in her hands like

02:20:19.190 --> 02:20:24.360
overripe fruit. She attempted to run into
the comparative safety of the ruined building,

02:20:24.360 --> 02:20:29.120
but her feet were already beginning to spread
out onto the street, melting into a sticky

02:20:29.120 --> 02:20:35.431
red smear on the pavement. Her legs crumbled
beneath her, and she collapsed to the ground,

02:20:35.431 --> 02:20:39.240
her face leaving a trail of gelatinous flesh
as it landed.

02:20:39.240 --> 02:20:44.650
She tried, uselessly, to drag herself along
the ground back into the darkness, but her

02:20:44.650 --> 02:20:50.441
arms were melting into the dirt, mixing with
it into a horrible red mud. She let out a

02:20:50.441 --> 02:20:56.480
last scream of agony, but the sound turned
to a thick gurgle as her lips fused together

02:20:56.480 --> 02:21:02.910
and her mouth melted into nothingness. He
watched it all, sobbing and roaring in frustration.

02:21:02.910 --> 02:21:09.381
She was gone, and he would never get the satisfaction
of ending her. The infernal sun had stolen

02:21:09.381 --> 02:21:16.200
her from him. Now she was nothing but a wet
shadow, like the rest of the humans had become

02:21:16.200 --> 02:21:20.660
long ago.
He heard the sound of a soft sob behind him,

02:21:20.660 --> 02:21:27.221
a tiny human voice. He turned, looking for
the source. There, was the little girl. He

02:21:27.221 --> 02:21:31.740
had almost forgotten about her. She was calling
out for her mother and crying, and she was

02:21:31.740 --> 02:21:37.900
looking right at him. Right at his face. He
waited for the anger to overtake him, but

02:21:37.900 --> 02:21:42.761
instead…there was nothing. Why wasn’t
he angry? She was looking at his face, so

02:21:42.761 --> 02:21:49.950
why? Then he realized: her eyes were on him,
but she did not see him. She was blind.

02:21:49.950 --> 02:21:55.261
She approached the creature, her arms outstretched,
following the sound of his crying. She touched

02:21:55.261 --> 02:22:01.080
his hand with her own tiny fingers, so small
and fragile, and asked, “Mommy?” Then,

02:22:01.080 --> 02:22:08.271
he felt something he had never felt before.
Tenderness, care, a desire to protect this

02:22:08.271 --> 02:22:13.971
tiny innocent being. It was the first time
he had ever felt warm, ever felt anything

02:22:13.971 --> 02:22:20.370
but sadness, anger, or fear. Overcome with
this softness, he wrapped his arms around

02:22:20.370 --> 02:22:26.090
the child and hugged her close. She hugged
him back, tightly, and the room went quiet

02:22:26.090 --> 02:22:30.900
as they both stopped crying.
From that day on, they became a family of

02:22:30.900 --> 02:22:35.490
sorts. They lived together, and he took care
of her. She didn’t know what he looked like,

02:22:35.490 --> 02:22:42.450
and had no idea he might be something to fear.
To her, he was safety. He was home. For the

02:22:42.450 --> 02:22:48.771
first time in his long, awful life, he felt
like a human instead of a monster. He would

02:22:48.771 --> 02:22:54.291
scavenge food for her, bring her fresh water,
lay down stolen blankets and pillows so she

02:22:54.291 --> 02:23:00.181
would have a soft place to rest. He even found
her a ratty, worn teddy bear amongst the rubble

02:23:00.181 --> 02:23:04.641
and gave it to her as a gift.
She clutched it to her chest and thanked him,

02:23:04.641 --> 02:23:10.320
and for once his tears were those of joy.
In this cursed world, they had found something

02:23:10.320 --> 02:23:15.780
like happiness in one another. He knew he
would protect her no matter what.

02:23:15.780 --> 02:23:21.721
Then, one day, everything changed. The creature
and the little girl were playing in the abandoned

02:23:21.721 --> 02:23:26.351
site, making up little games together, when
he took a break to look out the window and

02:23:26.351 --> 02:23:32.370
monitor their surroundings. Outside, the evil
red sun waited. When he turned back, the little

02:23:32.370 --> 02:23:38.721
girl was gone. One of the shadowy creatures,
once human, was imitating his voice to lure

02:23:38.721 --> 02:23:43.450
her away. He could hear it in the distance,
and her tiny footsteps following it.

02:23:43.450 --> 02:23:49.301
He chased after her, the only thing he had
ever loved, and saw her just as she stepped

02:23:49.301 --> 02:23:55.101
out into the light. She screamed as one of
her arms began to melt in the light, and he

02:23:55.101 --> 02:24:00.230
knew what he had to do. He grabbed her and
pulled her back inside, using his body to

02:24:00.230 --> 02:24:05.320
shield her from the horrible rays. As he wept
over her, his tears stopped her body from

02:24:05.320 --> 02:24:11.150
melting any further, but his body continued
to fall apart. It was too late for him.

02:24:11.150 --> 02:24:16.971
He ran, as far away as he could. Once he transformed,
he did not want to end up hurting the girl.

02:24:16.971 --> 02:24:23.431
He ran until his legs melted away and he could
not run anymore. He had reached the shoreline,

02:24:23.431 --> 02:24:27.440
and stared out at the ocean, unable to move
any more.

02:24:27.440 --> 02:24:33.641
He screamed, a final time, in agony, in heartbreak,
in mourning for the happiness he had finally

02:24:33.641 --> 02:24:41.330
found and now would lose. But at least he
knew, as his eyes melted away and he was nearly

02:24:41.330 --> 02:24:48.580
gone, that the girl would be safe. And then,
he was gone, and the world was quiet.

02:24:48.580 --> 02:24:54.320
A very strange anomaly sits in a humanoid
containment cell in the minimum security wing

02:24:54.320 --> 02:25:00.900
of Site 17. He walks, talks, and looks like
a man, but everything else is convoluted in

02:25:00.900 --> 02:25:08.370
a question mark. This is SCP-343 - Who, like
his namesake, God, is likely to cause arguments

02:25:08.370 --> 02:25:14.080
whenever he’s brought up. Is he the creator
of all that exists? The basis for the Abrahamic

02:25:14.080 --> 02:25:19.910
Faiths? Or is he a pretender - A reality warper
with immense power and a predilection towards

02:25:19.910 --> 02:25:26.660
delusion? A courtesan of the House of Malidraug?
Matthew? Methuselah? Yahweh?

02:25:26.660 --> 02:25:32.761
Who knows? Really, it depends on who you ask,
and which stories you choose to believe. And

02:25:32.761 --> 02:25:38.880
few people enjoy a good story more than SCP-343
himself. If, one day, you take the time to

02:25:38.880 --> 02:25:44.280
visit him in his room, and ask him to reveal
a page in the long book of his personal history,

02:25:44.280 --> 02:25:50.931
he might be kind enough to tell you a story.
A story like this - Of an encounter with something

02:25:50.931 --> 02:25:55.710
monstrous, that few others could hope to survive
meeting face to face…

02:25:55.710 --> 02:26:01.540
Rewind a few thousand years. Nobody knows
how many, exactly. God - as he chooses to

02:26:01.540 --> 02:26:07.711
dub himself - walked across the cracked ground
on worn sandals. It’d been some time since

02:26:07.711 --> 02:26:12.601
he’d seen an animal around here, and even
longer since he’d seen a human being. Not

02:26:12.601 --> 02:26:16.791
that this bothered him. He’d never been
bothered by his own company on a long walk

02:26:16.791 --> 02:26:17.971
like this.

02:26:17.971 --> 02:26:23.550
Of course, he could have sped up time or teleported,
but where was the fun in that? He was a tourist

02:26:23.550 --> 02:26:31.291
in the world of sensation. Of experience.
Of flesh, bone, dirt, blood, and sand. After

02:26:31.291 --> 02:26:35.200
all, where’s the fun in creating a whole
universe if you can’t drop in now and then

02:26:35.200 --> 02:26:40.250
to visit and do as the Romans do? Not that
the Romans would be around for another few

02:26:40.250 --> 02:26:45.050
thousand years. Even Atlas must occasionally
shift the weight of the globe from his shoulders

02:26:45.050 --> 02:26:50.380
for a jaunt around the cosmic neighborhood,
and whatever passes for “fresh air” in

02:26:50.380 --> 02:26:51.500
the vacuum.

02:26:51.500 --> 02:26:56.811
God whistled a tune to himself. It was a craggy,
mountainous region he’d found himself in.

02:26:56.811 --> 02:27:03.011
The distant peaks had frosted caps. A breathtaking
place, where many truly had their breaths

02:27:03.011 --> 02:27:08.061
taken away. How humans will so happily risk
their lives to do something extraordinary.

02:27:08.061 --> 02:27:10.391
It never ceased to amaze him.

02:27:10.391 --> 02:27:15.340
His stomach rumbled. Oh, how he enjoyed that
sensation. One of the funny little quirks

02:27:15.340 --> 02:27:20.370
of this human form that he’d weaved for
himself. It was no reason to be concerned

02:27:20.370 --> 02:27:25.081
- If memory served, from his last trip through
the area a few decades before, there was a

02:27:25.081 --> 02:27:30.330
friendly village not far from here. They had
always accepted him as a genial stranger,

02:27:30.330 --> 02:27:35.150
having no knowledge of his true power. God
had always believed that a person’s goodness

02:27:35.150 --> 02:27:39.621
is defined by how they treat those from whom
they had nothing to gain.

02:27:39.621 --> 02:27:44.330
So it caused him great concern as he approached
the village, and saw great plumes of smoke

02:27:44.330 --> 02:27:49.340
rising into the sky. He was so shocked by
this that he decided to break his rule about

02:27:49.340 --> 02:27:54.500
walking as a man, in case there was still
some way he could help. With a snap of his

02:27:54.500 --> 02:27:59.050
fingers, he disappeared and reappeared in
the center of the village’s town square.

02:27:59.050 --> 02:28:05.740
Total devastation. Huts and houses had been
torn asunder. Broken weapons lay on the ground.

02:28:05.740 --> 02:28:11.120
Some places were on fire, others smeared with
streaks of blood, like some terrible battle

02:28:11.120 --> 02:28:19.960
had occurred here. But something was wrong.
No bodies. Not one, from defender or assailant.

02:28:19.960 --> 02:28:25.551
How could a thriving village be so thoroughly
destroyed, and not leave a single corpse?

02:28:25.551 --> 02:28:31.110
It was an act so bizarre and depraved that
it even left God puzzling.

02:28:31.110 --> 02:28:35.650
That was another downside of his human form:
Here, on earth, he didn’t have access to

02:28:35.650 --> 02:28:41.691
true omniscience. How could a mere human mind,
bound by the constraints of linear time, ever

02:28:41.691 --> 02:28:46.700
truly comprehend the totality of existence?
Even attempting to do that here would melt

02:28:46.700 --> 02:28:51.541
the brain of his human body in its skull,
and leave it dribbling out of his nose and

02:28:51.541 --> 02:28:52.541
ears.

02:28:52.541 --> 02:28:57.210
Instead, he chose to walk around the ruins
of the village and investigate firsthand.

02:28:57.210 --> 02:29:02.500
Arrows and broken spears and swords littered
the ground. Some buildings were demolished,

02:29:02.500 --> 02:29:07.790
but there were no tracks or stray projectiles
that could suggest the presence of siege weapons.

02:29:07.790 --> 02:29:14.170
No, these buildings looked like they were
ripped apart. Some even still had claw marks…

02:29:14.170 --> 02:29:18.240
What terrible beast could have set upon this
town and done this?

02:29:18.240 --> 02:29:24.910
Then, he heard a voice, quiet and pleading
beneath some nearby rubble. A survivor! He

02:29:24.910 --> 02:29:30.870
rushed over to the pile and evaporated it
with a thought, Underneath, a feeble old man,

02:29:30.870 --> 02:29:35.710
covered in stone dust, was quivering. God
helped him up and guided him into one of the

02:29:35.710 --> 02:29:42.051
few remaining huts still standing in the village.
They both took seats - God held up two hands,

02:29:42.051 --> 02:29:48.000
cradling empty space. Two cups suddenly occupied
that space, both filled with warm, healing

02:29:48.000 --> 02:29:52.271
tea. He passed the old man one of the cups
while sipping from his own.

02:29:52.271 --> 02:29:57.410
He asked the old man if he’d seen what had
happened. The old man told him, no, he hadn’t

02:29:57.410 --> 02:30:02.870
seen anything in decades. He’d been rendered
blind in his youth. Little did either of them

02:30:02.870 --> 02:30:08.841
know, that very blindness was the only reason
he was the sole survivor of the massacre.

02:30:08.841 --> 02:30:12.920
The blind man told God that one of the village’s
scouts had gone up into the mountain with

02:30:12.920 --> 02:30:18.261
a small hunting party. The group was gone
for days, until one of the members - the youngest

02:30:18.261 --> 02:30:24.410
among them - returned, weeping, frostbitten,
and covered in blood. He said that his friends

02:30:24.410 --> 02:30:29.250
had been killed by a beast in the mountains.
Something that looked almost like a man, but

02:30:29.250 --> 02:30:37.221
terribly wrong. And its face. Its awful, awful
face. He would never forget it. He was just

02:30:37.221 --> 02:30:40.450
lucky to escape with his life when the others
were torn apart.

02:30:40.450 --> 02:30:45.860
But when the young man returned, he’d brought
the shadow of death with him. It was a curse

02:30:45.860 --> 02:30:52.500
that doomed the entire village - men, women,
and children - to a terrible fate. And that

02:30:52.500 --> 02:30:57.210
fate was upon them a mere hour after the survivor
had returned.

02:30:57.210 --> 02:31:01.610
Of course, there were gaps in the blind man’s
understanding, given he was lacking one of

02:31:01.610 --> 02:31:07.030
his major senses. But the sounds he could
describe with perfect clarity. It was faint

02:31:07.030 --> 02:31:13.460
and distant at first. That awful wail, and
the galloping. Hands and feet thundering against

02:31:13.460 --> 02:31:18.971
the ground, faster than any horse could move.
Getting closer and closer and closer.

02:31:18.971 --> 02:31:24.940
Another villager saw it approaching and screamed,
then it was upon them. The villagers screamed

02:31:24.940 --> 02:31:30.910
but it screamed louder. Always wailing and
shrieking and sobbing like a monster crawled

02:31:30.910 --> 02:31:36.110
straight up from hell. People tried to fight
it, by the sounds of it. The blind man, with

02:31:36.110 --> 02:31:42.500
teary, blank eyes, recalled the sounds of
arrows nocking and swords clashing against…

02:31:42.500 --> 02:31:47.940
Something. But even their greatest warriors
had screamed and died. Those who saw it and

02:31:47.940 --> 02:31:53.550
tried to flee and hide were slaughtered all
the same. Soon enough, there were only two

02:31:53.550 --> 02:31:58.360
sounds left in the village. The monster and
the blind man, both weeping.

02:31:58.360 --> 02:32:04.391
He didn’t understand why it never took him.
It wasn’t fair. It took everything else.

02:32:04.391 --> 02:32:10.171
To leave him here, alive, when everyone and
everything he’d ever known was destroyed,

02:32:10.171 --> 02:32:15.400
was a greater punishment than even death.
After killing all of these innocents, the

02:32:15.400 --> 02:32:20.221
monster had simply wandered off to the mountains
again, the sound of its quiet sobs getting

02:32:20.221 --> 02:32:24.540
smaller and smaller until it was gone altogether.

02:32:24.540 --> 02:32:29.311
God comforted the blind man as he wept for
the loss of all his loved ones. He told the

02:32:29.311 --> 02:32:33.182
blind man that he would venture up to the
mountains himself and confront the creature

02:32:33.182 --> 02:32:39.820
on its own territory, and, at the very least,
find out why it had done this terrible thing.

02:32:39.820 --> 02:32:43.500
But first, he must relocate the blind man
to a safer place.

02:32:43.500 --> 02:32:48.290
He placed a hand on the blind man’s shoulder
and he vanished. He would appear in another

02:32:48.290 --> 02:32:54.650
friendly village, miles away. God sent a silent
message into the minds of every villager:

02:32:54.650 --> 02:33:02.340
Take good care of this man. He has undergone
horrors you can’t even imagine. Your kindness

02:33:02.340 --> 02:33:07.980
will be rewarded later, for that, you have
my promise.

02:33:07.980 --> 02:33:13.600
God sighed and turned his tired eyes to the
distant mountains. A monster lurked up there

02:33:13.600 --> 02:33:19.740
- Perhaps one of his own creations, or maybe
a corruption of one of his creations. Either

02:33:19.740 --> 02:33:25.660
way, whatever existed without his knowledge
existed without his consent, and he intended

02:33:25.660 --> 02:33:30.601
to know of the beast in the mountains. Though
given what he’d seen already, he didn’t

02:33:30.601 --> 02:33:34.450
expect to receive a warm welcome from this
murderous demon.

02:33:34.450 --> 02:33:38.631
Miles away, up in the mountains, the creature
licked the blood from its cracked lips. It

02:33:38.631 --> 02:33:43.730
looked like it might once have been a human
being or something that aspired to humanity,

02:33:43.730 --> 02:33:50.590
or mocked it with its very existence. It was
a huge, gangling beast, skin alabaster, eyes

02:33:50.590 --> 02:33:56.870
empty and soulless, dribbling rivulets of
burning tears down a hideous, gaunt face.

02:33:56.870 --> 02:34:02.900
It crawled into the frozen mouth of a cave
with great icicle fangs, wheezing and weeping.

02:34:02.900 --> 02:34:08.540
All it ever wanted was to be alone. Why did
they have to keep interfering? Didn’t they

02:34:08.540 --> 02:34:13.051
know what happened? All the terrible things
they’d made it do…

02:34:13.051 --> 02:34:17.620
The creature curled its long, gangly body
into the fetal position, scratching great

02:34:17.620 --> 02:34:23.711
ruts into the sides of its bald cranium with
long, sharp fingers. Terrible things. Terrible,

02:34:23.711 --> 02:34:26.341
terrible things…

02:34:26.341 --> 02:34:31.740
And then there was a brilliant flash, just
a few feet away. The monster was surprised,

02:34:31.740 --> 02:34:36.681
it turned to see a figure silhouetted in the
mouth of the cave. He wore sandals and thin

02:34:36.681 --> 02:34:41.961
robes. His eyes glowed with a kind of power
that the monster didn’t recognize. This

02:34:41.961 --> 02:34:46.391
stranger stared at the monster without an
ounce of fear in his heart: He stared right

02:34:46.391 --> 02:34:48.141
into its eyes, unwavering.

02:34:48.141 --> 02:34:55.841
No! No, no, no, no! It could feel it again.
The rage bubbling up, deep down. A cauldron

02:34:55.841 --> 02:35:01.141
of seething anger. It hated the feeling, like
being lowered into a vat of molten metal.

02:35:01.141 --> 02:35:05.980
Unspeakable fire and pain coursing through
every vessel. It began to weep and scream

02:35:05.980 --> 02:35:09.690
while the stranger in the cave mouth just
watched, not moving a muscle.

02:35:09.690 --> 02:35:17.460
“Do you know who I am?” the stranger asked
with a deep voice that betrayed almost infinite

02:35:17.460 --> 02:35:22.261
power, knowledge, and wisdom. But it wouldn’t
change the outcome here.

02:35:22.261 --> 02:35:26.330
The monster bounded at him at speeds that
wouldn’t be seen again until jet planes

02:35:26.330 --> 02:35:31.891
and bullet trains are invented millennia into
the future. Its jaws were hanging impossibly

02:35:31.891 --> 02:35:37.961
wide, fangs borne, its arms extended and deadly
claws ready to strike. But before those terrible

02:35:37.961 --> 02:35:42.431
hands could close around the stranger, he
vanished. The monster stumbled and rolled

02:35:42.431 --> 02:35:46.240
across the snow, confused. What trickery was
being used here?

02:35:46.240 --> 02:35:52.690
“I’ll take that as a no,” said the voice
from behind him. “You ought to show your

02:35:52.690 --> 02:35:57.860
father some respect. More respect than you
gave to those poor people down in the village,

02:35:57.860 --> 02:35:59.341
at least.”

02:35:59.341 --> 02:36:03.530
Seething, the creature turned and saw the
stranger standing back in the darkness of

02:36:03.530 --> 02:36:09.450
the cave, staring at him. But the beast didn’t
have the capacity for awe or holy terror.

02:36:09.450 --> 02:36:15.360
Only violence. Boundless, limitless, unstoppable
violence.

02:36:15.360 --> 02:36:19.900
It darted towards the stranger again, trying
to strike at him. Somehow, it was like fighting

02:36:19.900 --> 02:36:25.490
an empty robe. Not a single one of its deadly
strikes seemed to hit the stranger. The stranger

02:36:25.490 --> 02:36:30.880
leaped backwards, putting some space between
himself and the monster but still not breaking

02:36:30.880 --> 02:36:34.261
a sweat. He breathed in deeply, then exhaled.

02:36:34.261 --> 02:36:39.420
The breath came out like a mighty typhoon,
shocking even the monster with its sudden

02:36:39.420 --> 02:36:45.070
force. It was blown backwards, a leaf in the
wind, until its long claws dug into the ground

02:36:45.070 --> 02:36:49.440
and anchored it in place. The stranger gave
a wry smile at this, impressed.

02:36:49.440 --> 02:36:54.280
“My, my, you’re certainly a tenacious
one, aren’t you?” he said. “Perhaps

02:36:54.280 --> 02:37:02.641
we can talk for a little while instead of
fighting. I want to know why you killed all

02:37:02.641 --> 02:37:03.660
those people.”

02:37:03.660 --> 02:37:09.021
No answer. The beast roared, its mighty limbs
pounding into the ground as it closed the

02:37:09.021 --> 02:37:16.431
gap between itself and the stranger in fractions
of a second. It would kill him. Rend him.

02:37:16.431 --> 02:37:20.900
Destroy him just like all the others. He’d
left it no choice!

02:37:20.900 --> 02:37:26.280
Suddenly, the ground below it seemed to give
away. The creature was confused. It looked

02:37:26.280 --> 02:37:30.900
down to see that the ancient stone below had
somehow taken on the properties of a liquid,

02:37:30.900 --> 02:37:36.280
and it was sinking. The beast panicked and
began to thrash. It was a strong swimmer,

02:37:36.280 --> 02:37:41.820
but it didn’t expect to need to swim here.
The shock was too much, and soon, the ground

02:37:41.820 --> 02:37:46.380
submerged it entirely, muffling its terrible
roars and shrieks.

02:37:46.380 --> 02:37:51.250
And just like that, the ground was solid again,
trapping the beast inside.

02:37:51.250 --> 02:37:56.580
The stranger stepped forward and looked at
the ground. “A much-needed time out,”

02:37:56.580 --> 02:38:01.960
he said. “You do yourself no good struggling
like this.”

02:38:01.960 --> 02:38:07.190
Despite its terrible capacity for evil, God
couldn’t help but admire the beast, at least

02:38:07.190 --> 02:38:13.690
on the level of construction. It was so pared
down, so unburdened. A killer to the core,

02:38:13.690 --> 02:38:19.511
but seemingly unkillable. Had he made this
creature? Billions of species and the species

02:38:19.511 --> 02:38:24.830
those, in turn, had created through billions
of years of breeding and evolution, and somewhere

02:38:24.830 --> 02:38:30.080
along the line, this thing happened. It was
easy even for the universe’s creator to

02:38:30.080 --> 02:38:33.011
lose track of some of the tinier variables.

02:38:33.011 --> 02:38:38.681
And in the grand scheme of things, even this
monster was still a tiny variable. But right

02:38:38.681 --> 02:38:43.040
here, right now, it was still one hell of
a problem.

02:38:43.040 --> 02:38:49.820
The ground rumbled below God. Cracks formed.
The mountain peak shook. God raised an eyebrow,

02:38:49.820 --> 02:38:54.710
genuinely impressed, as the monster ripped
free of its stone prison and re-entered the

02:38:54.710 --> 02:39:01.240
fray. It roared and screamed still, its blank
eyes fixed on him. Its skeletal body throbbed

02:39:01.240 --> 02:39:06.660
and heaved with power. Unlike any other creature
in nature, it was almost like, the longer

02:39:06.660 --> 02:39:10.860
their conflict went on, the more energized
the beast became.

02:39:10.860 --> 02:39:16.340
God sighed. All those poor villagers. They
never stood a chance against this monster.

02:39:16.340 --> 02:39:21.681
It lunged for him, even faster and stronger
than before. He teleported out of the way

02:39:21.681 --> 02:39:27.160
in the nick of time and the beast’s claws
cleaved through a nearby cave wall, effortless.

02:39:27.160 --> 02:39:32.211
God materialized nearby, but he didn’t have
time to speak. The beast lunged again, and

02:39:32.211 --> 02:39:39.391
again, and again. Every time he reappeared,
the beast went for him with impossible speed.

02:39:39.391 --> 02:39:44.070
Deciding to widen the playing field, God teleported
to the top of the mountain. The creature,

02:39:44.070 --> 02:39:49.080
somehow sensing his presence, vaulted upwards
and tunneled through the roof of the cave,

02:39:49.080 --> 02:39:53.340
bursting out of the ground in front of God,
who was floating just slightly off the ground.

02:39:53.340 --> 02:39:59.340
“It would be wise of you to stop,” God
carefully intoned. “All this time, you know,

02:39:59.340 --> 02:40:05.681
I’ve been going easy on you. You don’t
want to find out what the wrath of God looks

02:40:05.681 --> 02:40:06.681
like.”

02:40:06.681 --> 02:40:12.120
Storm clouds were gathering above. Mighty
thunder roared across the sky. The beast was

02:40:12.120 --> 02:40:18.130
undeterred. It roared and galloped towards
God. And God, in turn, called down a response.

02:40:18.130 --> 02:40:23.511
A volley of lightning the likes of which the
world has never seen before or since struck

02:40:23.511 --> 02:40:29.150
down on the charging monster. The sudden white
flash could sting the eyes from miles away.

02:40:29.150 --> 02:40:33.780
The monster shrieked from the blast, feeling
its flesh lift off its bones and atomize in

02:40:33.780 --> 02:40:38.540
the sheer heat of the electricity around it.
It could smell itself cooking. The lightning

02:40:38.540 --> 02:40:43.771
blasts only lasted for a few seconds, but
for the beast, it felt like an eternity.

02:40:43.771 --> 02:40:48.700
When the onslaught stopped, the air was still
heavy with electrical potential. God stared

02:40:48.700 --> 02:40:52.990
down at the black scorch mark on the side
of the mountain where the creature had been

02:40:52.990 --> 02:40:57.580
standing. All the snow within the mile had
been evaporated by the blast. It was a raw

02:40:57.580 --> 02:41:03.150
display of the power of nature that would
make even Zeus tremble in his sandals.

02:41:03.150 --> 02:41:09.240
And yet… There was still movement. Something
started to get up from the burnt patch where

02:41:09.240 --> 02:41:15.250
nothing should be left alive. A blackened
skeleton, rising shakily from the ash but

02:41:15.250 --> 02:41:21.302
still very much alive. As it started to rise,
new flesh began growing over the bones, little

02:41:21.302 --> 02:41:26.010
by little. Even God was astonished by the
sight of it. He’d never seen a creature

02:41:26.010 --> 02:41:31.561
cling so ardently to life in spite of having
truly unsurmountable power amassed against

02:41:31.561 --> 02:41:32.561
it.

02:41:32.561 --> 02:41:36.130
It was up against God, and still, it fought.

02:41:36.130 --> 02:41:41.540
The monster tottered on its freakishly long
limbs, still disoriented, unusually staggered

02:41:41.540 --> 02:41:46.391
for a creature driven by such single-minded,
violent purpose. When enough of its face grew

02:41:46.391 --> 02:41:53.121
back to do so, it began to weep and sob again,
tears streaking down its terrible face. Looking

02:41:53.121 --> 02:41:58.660
at this creature, after all this, God couldn’t
help but feel a new emotion: Pity.

02:41:58.660 --> 02:42:03.960
He lowered himself to the ground and approached
the creature. Like none had ever done before,

02:42:03.960 --> 02:42:09.461
he gathered it up into his arms and held it,
feeling its heaving, wretched sobs against

02:42:09.461 --> 02:42:15.190
him. This beast was in so much pain. He could
feel it, radiating from within.

02:42:15.190 --> 02:42:20.250
“Speak, my son,” God said in a soft, fatherly
voice.

02:42:20.250 --> 02:42:29.190
And for the first and only time, the monster
spoke, “Can’t look. Can’t see. Make

02:42:29.190 --> 02:42:40.951
me hurt people. Don’t want hurt people.
Can’t look, can’t look. Please.”

02:42:40.951 --> 02:42:46.681
That was all it managed to choke out before
devolving back into unintelligible babble.

02:42:46.681 --> 02:42:52.670
But it was enough. Enough for God to understand
its pain. He did not know if it would be right

02:42:52.670 --> 02:42:58.181
to change the monster’s nature - is it ever
right to truly change anyone’s nature? - but

02:42:58.181 --> 02:43:03.311
it was within his almost limitless power to
grant it one reprieve from its pain.

02:43:03.311 --> 02:43:08.210
He settled the beast in the snow below him.
It was quiet and still.

02:43:08.210 --> 02:43:15.211
And God said unto the beast, “Rest now,
child. Rest for thousands of years, if you

02:43:15.211 --> 02:43:22.150
must. I hope only that when you eventually
awaken, you feel differently…”

02:43:22.150 --> 02:43:28.880
And so another story from the catalog of SCP-343.
Of course, it leaves us with certain questions,

02:43:28.880 --> 02:43:36.021
chief among them being: Is it true? Did 343
and 096 have this chance encounter long ago,

02:43:36.021 --> 02:43:41.230
or is this just another tall tale from an
anomaly who fancies himself a deity? We have

02:43:41.230 --> 02:43:43.710
our truth, and you have yours

02:43:43.710 --> 02:43:48.290
If you’ve been a close follower of this
channel for a while now, or even of the SCP

02:43:48.290 --> 02:43:52.410
Foundation Archive as a whole, then there’s
a pretty high chance that you might have heard

02:43:52.410 --> 02:43:58.690
of SCP-096. Even if this just so happens to
be the first of our SCP videos that you’ve

02:43:58.690 --> 02:44:03.110
ever clicked on, you still might have seen
the Shy Guy itself in various other corners

02:44:03.110 --> 02:44:09.540
of the internet. Since the earliest days of
the SCP Foundation, SCP-096 has gradually

02:44:09.540 --> 02:44:14.400
become one of the most iconic and recognizable
creatures in the Foundation’s entire menagerie

02:44:14.400 --> 02:44:18.870
of anomalous beings.
Despite standing taller than any ordinary

02:44:18.870 --> 02:44:24.190
person, you might be forgiven for mistaking
SCP-096 for a human being if you were to see

02:44:24.190 --> 02:44:29.710
it at a distance. But the creature is unusually
proportioned, to say the least, still appearing

02:44:29.710 --> 02:44:34.580
almost human in its shape, but possessing
long, dangling arms that almost reach the

02:44:34.580 --> 02:44:40.740
ground. SCP-096’s body is thin and hairless,
with opaque, clear white pupil-less eyes and

02:44:40.740 --> 02:44:45.870
a wide, gaping mouth - or so we’ve heard,
thanks to drawings provided by someone who

02:44:45.870 --> 02:44:51.290
sadly isn’t with us anymore...
But you might be surprised to learn that SCP-096

02:44:51.290 --> 02:44:56.561
doesn’t really do all that much. Mostly,
the tall, spindly humanoid spends its waking

02:44:56.561 --> 02:45:02.240
days mindlessly pacing around in its containment
cell. Sounds like most days in a college dorm

02:45:02.240 --> 02:45:07.630
during lockdown, right? That is, of course,
until someone dares to look at SCP-096’s

02:45:07.630 --> 02:45:12.561
face. It doesn’t have to be directly either,
it can be something as simple as glancing

02:45:12.561 --> 02:45:18.550
at a photo, or watching a video of the creature.
But the moment it is seen, the exact second

02:45:18.550 --> 02:45:24.570
it’s perceived, the wiry monster will know.
Having its face viewed in any way will cause

02:45:24.570 --> 02:45:29.570
the Shy Guy to really live up to its nickname;
the creature suffers some sort of psychological

02:45:29.570 --> 02:45:35.921
meltdown, covering its face with its huge,
claw-like hands and weeping. SCP-096 quickly

02:45:35.921 --> 02:45:41.070
becomes utterly inconsolable, suffering from
extreme emotional distress until it simply

02:45:41.070 --> 02:45:46.811
can’t take it anymore. Pulling itself up
to its full, looming height, SCP-096 will

02:45:46.811 --> 02:45:51.450
stand and travel at impossible speeds towards
the person that dared look at its face.

02:45:51.450 --> 02:45:56.641
There’s no way to hide from the Shy Guy,
nothing can stand in its path as it tracks

02:45:56.641 --> 02:46:02.660
down this unlucky person. And when it finds
them? Well, it’s certainly not pleasant.

02:46:02.660 --> 02:46:08.181
Somehow, without leaving any trace on their
body, SCP-096 will kill the person that looked

02:46:08.181 --> 02:46:14.101
at it, possibly even eating them afterwards.
Then, it calms itself down, returns to its

02:46:14.101 --> 02:46:18.920
more docile state, and is typically bagged
and brought back home by whatever Mobile Task

02:46:18.920 --> 02:46:23.240
Force is closest.
SCP-096 has killed many of the teams sent

02:46:23.240 --> 02:46:28.420
to contain it, proving itself to be resistant
to gunfire and other forms of conventional

02:46:28.420 --> 02:46:33.021
weaponry. As much as being only a little as
a tiny speck in a photograph is enough to

02:46:33.021 --> 02:46:38.521
trigger the creature’s relentless defense
mechanism. Nobody can get away from it either,

02:46:38.521 --> 02:46:45.031
there is literally nowhere in the entire world
that someone can run to escape SCP-096. Not

02:46:45.031 --> 02:46:50.021
even crossing the oceans or jumping aboard
a plane is enough to stop it. And if our community

02:46:50.021 --> 02:46:55.480
questions video on the matter is anything
to go by, you may not even be safe in outer

02:46:55.480 --> 02:46:58.381
space.
The SCP Foundation’s only real way to keep

02:46:58.381 --> 02:47:03.010
the creature safely under wraps and contained
is to lock it up and force all members of

02:47:03.010 --> 02:47:07.880
personnel to never, ever look at it, while
they hunt down any pieces of recorded media

02:47:07.880 --> 02:47:11.540
still out there, waiting like a ticking time
bomb.

02:47:11.540 --> 02:47:17.630
So, if no member of the Foundation can look
at SCP-096, how do they interact with it?

02:47:17.630 --> 02:47:25.120
Well, luckily, there’s a tale that answers
exactly that, known as “A Lesson in Power.”

02:47:25.120 --> 02:47:30.480
Doctor Danielle Kowalski, a member of the
Foundation’s research team assigned to SCP-096,

02:47:30.480 --> 02:47:34.440
was sent into the creature’s cell wearing
a sleep mask. You know, the kind you get on

02:47:34.440 --> 02:47:39.650
expensive flights? What she was really wishing
for though, was a gas mask to go with it;

02:47:39.650 --> 02:47:45.181
Nobody had warned her how bad SCP-096 smelled.
Then again though, few had been as close to

02:47:45.181 --> 02:47:48.280
the creature as she had and lived to tell
the tale.

02:47:48.280 --> 02:47:52.580
As a child, Danielle Kowalski had a fear of
stepping into the ocean. Her mother used to

02:47:52.580 --> 02:47:57.351
warn her about treading on a stingray and
dying as a result, giving her a phobia that

02:47:57.351 --> 02:48:03.010
stayed with her for many years. But Danielle’s
father had encouraged her to face her fear,

02:48:03.010 --> 02:48:07.710
to shuffle her feet on the ocean floor, kicking
up the sand so that the stingrays would know

02:48:07.710 --> 02:48:11.521
she was coming, and would simply just swim
away.

02:48:11.521 --> 02:48:14.760
Perhaps if he could have seen what kind of
horrors Danielle would be working with at

02:48:14.760 --> 02:48:19.181
the Foundation, her father wouldn’t have
tried to make her so fearless. Maybe there

02:48:19.181 --> 02:48:22.900
are some things that it’s logical to be
afraid of.

02:48:22.900 --> 02:48:27.921
Entering SCP-096’s cell, Doctor Kowalski
could hear the monster’s heaving breaths,

02:48:27.921 --> 02:48:32.551
the perfect mix between a frightened whimper
and a death rattle, the haunting sound of

02:48:32.551 --> 02:48:37.181
a person’s final gasp of air. In the back
of her mind, Danielle hoped she didn’t touch

02:48:37.181 --> 02:48:41.710
the creature by accident. The closer she got,
the tension and the heat of its warm breath

02:48:41.710 --> 02:48:46.950
against her skin was making the approach all
the more terrifying. She had been issued with

02:48:46.950 --> 02:48:52.561
an instant camera, and took a photo in the
dark. Click. The flash fired, but with her

02:48:52.561 --> 02:48:57.980
eyes covered she couldn’t tell.
That was good though, that was the point.

02:48:57.980 --> 02:49:02.360
If Danielle could see the flash going off,
she could see what else was in the room, and

02:49:02.360 --> 02:49:07.130
that would put her in even more danger. She
was locked in with a kind of stingray that

02:49:07.130 --> 02:49:11.000
wouldn’t swim away, even if it knew she
was there.

02:49:11.000 --> 02:49:16.280
Slowly, Doctor Kowalski began blindly photographing
the cell around her, stuffing each freshly

02:49:16.280 --> 02:49:21.550
printed photo into her pocket as the camera
spat them out. Over the span of forty-five

02:49:21.550 --> 02:49:26.150
minutes, she took a series of pictures of
the entire cell, until the film in the instant

02:49:26.150 --> 02:49:31.400
camera had run out. Danielle knew that she
would probably only need one picture, but

02:49:31.400 --> 02:49:36.391
better to have more, just in case. Luckily,
none of her Foundation colleagues were around

02:49:36.391 --> 02:49:42.030
to tell her how insane what she was doing
was. They were all preoccupied, preparing

02:49:42.030 --> 02:49:45.521
the site for its first official visit from
the FBI.

02:49:45.521 --> 02:49:50.891
This all took place after what was known as
the Broken Masquerade had taken place. Essentially,

02:49:50.891 --> 02:49:55.521
knowledge of the SCP Foundation and its research
into the endless stream of anomalies it had

02:49:55.521 --> 02:50:00.911
contained was all made public. People knew
the truth now, and the Foundation found itself

02:50:00.911 --> 02:50:05.200
in the one place it never wanted to be: the
public eye.

02:50:05.200 --> 02:50:10.200
The organization and its activities were under
the scrutiny of every journalist on the planet,

02:50:10.200 --> 02:50:14.630
and once more information about the SCPs and
the anomalous world of the Foundation became

02:50:14.630 --> 02:50:20.400
known, the world would surely change forever.
Danielle Kowalski knew all of this and told

02:50:20.400 --> 02:50:26.200
herself that everyone would need something
for protection now. She only hoped her photographs

02:50:26.200 --> 02:50:29.870
would be enough.
Fifteen months later, Doctor Kowalski was

02:50:29.870 --> 02:50:35.120
interrogated in her apartment, by one Agent
O’Brien. He and a group of fellow agents

02:50:35.120 --> 02:50:41.110
had barged into Danielle’s home, demanding
to know the location where a number of SCP-610

02:50:41.110 --> 02:50:46.591
specimens were being held. SCP-610 being,
of course, the infamous infection known as

02:50:46.591 --> 02:50:51.641
the Flesh that Hates, a disease capable of
turning human beings into horrible, deformed

02:50:51.641 --> 02:50:57.141
monstrosities of flesh. Agent O’Brien, and
presumably whoever he was working for, was

02:50:57.141 --> 02:51:01.980
intent on turning these creatures into weapons,
and the man had little patience for a former

02:51:01.980 --> 02:51:06.181
Foundation researcher who wasn’t giving
up the answers that he wanted.

02:51:06.181 --> 02:51:10.891
Danielle tried to argue with the Agent, citing
the Berlin Accords Against Weaponized Anomalies,

02:51:10.891 --> 02:51:16.170
a motion proposed to the United States Senate
that would forbid the use of anomalous objects,

02:51:16.170 --> 02:51:21.420
entities, and creatures as weapons for any
country. O’Brien wasn’t interested, he

02:51:21.420 --> 02:51:25.690
claimed that if the Kremlin in Russia had
no intention of ratifying such a law, then

02:51:25.690 --> 02:51:30.280
America wouldn’t either. The Agent had his
men grab Danielle by her arm, while he grabbed

02:51:30.280 --> 02:51:34.820
her right middle finger and began to push
a thin knife under her fingernail.

02:51:34.820 --> 02:51:39.040
The former member of the Foundation tried
to stifle her scream, but couldn’t stop

02:51:39.040 --> 02:51:43.190
herself as the pain struck.
As Agent O’Brien tortured Danielle, he told

02:51:43.190 --> 02:51:48.431
her that the Russians had all the information
they would need about SCP-610 in order to

02:51:48.431 --> 02:51:53.771
turn the Flesh into some kind of weapon. As
far as he was concerned, the United States

02:51:53.771 --> 02:51:58.710
government needed access to those SCP-610
specimens to know exactly what they would

02:51:58.710 --> 02:52:03.540
be up against in the likely event that Russia
used the Flesh that Hates against them. And

02:52:03.540 --> 02:52:09.510
only a researcher for the SCP Foundation,
the former senior researcher of the SCP-610

02:52:09.510 --> 02:52:14.670
project, would know where to find those specimens.
“You don't think it's reasonable for the

02:52:14.670 --> 02:52:19.070
United States government to want to understand
what could be used against us?” O’Brien

02:52:19.070 --> 02:52:23.420
asked as Danielle’s blood trickled out of
her finger into his leather gloves. “Hell,

02:52:23.420 --> 02:52:28.940
I don't care what you think we're going to
do with it. I want those specimens.”

02:52:28.940 --> 02:52:33.330
When the Agent finally let her go, Doctor
Kowalski tried to catch her breath, forcing

02:52:33.330 --> 02:52:39.181
herself not to scream in pain again. She pointed
with her other, unharmed hand, to her bedroom,

02:52:39.181 --> 02:52:43.530
telling O’Brien to check the bottom left
drawer. She had placed a manilla envelope

02:52:43.530 --> 02:52:49.000
in there, containing the only thing Danielle
had kept since departing the SCP Foundation.

02:52:49.000 --> 02:52:53.341
Under O’Brien’s order, his men ransacked
the nearby room and were quickly able to find

02:52:53.341 --> 02:52:57.280
what they were looking for.
The envelope was presented to Agent O’Brien,

02:52:57.280 --> 02:53:02.011
who was no doubt expecting to find a file
inside, something that detailed the exact

02:53:02.011 --> 02:53:07.590
location of those specimens he was looking
for. So, you can probably imagine the dissatisfied

02:53:07.590 --> 02:53:13.971
look on his face when he opened the envelope
to find… photographs. A collection of instant

02:53:13.971 --> 02:53:20.490
photos, all taken in a dark cell, with all
of them depicting a tall, thin humanoid creature.

02:53:20.490 --> 02:53:26.910
A being with long, dangling arms that almost
reached the ground, a thin, hairless body,

02:53:26.910 --> 02:53:31.591
opaque, pupil-less eyes, and a wide, gaping
mouth.

02:53:31.591 --> 02:53:35.811
Furious to once again be denied the information
he was looking for, O’Brien disregarded

02:53:35.811 --> 02:53:39.000
the pictures and burned Doctor Kowalski’s
neck with his cigarette.

02:53:39.000 --> 02:53:43.610
In his anger, he said “I think your Foundation
sure thought they were powerful hiding in

02:53:43.610 --> 02:53:49.150
the shadows. But I'd like to explain to you
why you're wrong.” The Agent then went on

02:53:49.150 --> 02:53:54.110
to describe his time as a member of the DEA
in the late 1980’s, and how he’d been

02:53:54.110 --> 02:53:57.871
involved in the hunting of notorious cartel
boss Pablo Escobar.

02:53:57.871 --> 02:54:03.610
“I had a narco in custody… every bit as
uncooperative as you are now.” The man had

02:54:03.610 --> 02:54:08.790
insisted that Escobar owned all of the Colombian
law enforcement, but a younger O’Brien had

02:54:08.790 --> 02:54:13.641
argued that, if that was the case and they
possessed all this power, then why did Pablo

02:54:13.641 --> 02:54:18.820
Escobar’s men hide their guns? Why smuggle
drugs in secret, and bury their money where

02:54:18.820 --> 02:54:23.130
no one would find it if they really had the
control they claimed?

02:54:23.130 --> 02:54:28.940
Much like Escobar’s cartel, the SCP Foundation
had always moved in the shadows, a power that

02:54:28.940 --> 02:54:34.290
O’Brien believed to have really belonged
to the government. “Your anomalies belong

02:54:34.290 --> 02:54:42.011
to us. Your research belongs to us. Your life
belongs to us,” he told Doctor Kowalski.

02:54:42.011 --> 02:54:46.221
Suddenly, one of the other agents noticed
movement outside the apartment. The second

02:54:46.221 --> 02:54:51.340
that Danielle saw a long arm phase through
her wall, with grey skin like that of a corpse,

02:54:51.340 --> 02:54:55.801
she snapped her eyes shut. O’Brien and his
men all drew their guns and opened fire on

02:54:55.801 --> 02:54:59.880
the monster that had walked through the wall
as if it wasn’t even there.

02:54:59.880 --> 02:55:05.601
All the while, Danielle Kowalski kept her
eyes shut. The sounds of screaming and snapping

02:55:05.601 --> 02:55:11.592
bones and a sickening slurping noise filled
the air, but she made sure not to look. Within

02:55:11.592 --> 02:55:18.030
a few quick moments of panic and agonizing
pain, it was over. The apartment was quiet,

02:55:18.030 --> 02:55:23.170
apart from strange breathing; like a mix of
a frightened whimper and a death rattle.

02:55:23.170 --> 02:55:28.051
“Run along now, 096,” Doctor Kowalski
told the Shy Guy. “There are worse monsters

02:55:28.051 --> 02:55:29.670
in this world than you.”

02:55:29.670 --> 02:55:35.370
Dr. Dan anxiously paced his cell. It was the
only way he’d been able to occupy himself

02:55:35.370 --> 02:55:40.990
for the past six months, save for poring through
files and, when he was lucky, being able to

02:55:40.990 --> 02:55:43.671
oversee another failed termination attempt.

02:55:43.671 --> 02:55:48.690
He only stopped pacing when, for a brief and
horrible moment, he realized that monster

02:55:48.690 --> 02:55:55.650
was probably doing the exact same thing in
its big, metal cube at this very moment. That

02:55:55.650 --> 02:56:01.391
horrible creature that he’d thrown away
his life for a futile shot at killing.

02:56:01.391 --> 02:56:05.900
Perhaps the whole thing had been a fool’s
errand. After all, he was formerly a researcher

02:56:05.900 --> 02:56:12.711
under the employ of the SCP Foundation. Secure,
Contain, Protect. They had truly sadistic

02:56:12.711 --> 02:56:21.450
beasts like SCP-106, the nightmarish Old Man,
and SCP-352, Baba Yaga, in containment cells,

02:56:21.450 --> 02:56:25.711
and there were no plans to terminate either
of them. They weren’t the Global Occult

02:56:25.711 --> 02:56:28.301
Coalition, for 343’s sake.

02:56:28.301 --> 02:56:33.101
Why did Dr. Dan even want to terminate SCP-096
in the first place?

02:56:33.101 --> 02:56:39.000
He’d tried to explain it to them again and
again, but his pleas had fallen on deaf ears.

02:56:39.000 --> 02:56:44.791
In his mind, the impetus for destroying the
Shy Guy was terrifyingly clear. It was a monster

02:56:44.791 --> 02:56:50.631
that killed anyone or anything that looked
at its face, even a photograph of it, even

02:56:50.631 --> 02:56:56.190
a tiny collection of pixels. When its rage
state was induced, it seemed that nothing

02:56:56.190 --> 02:57:01.511
would stop it. It would have an intuitive
psychic link to its victim, charge at them

02:57:01.511 --> 02:57:07.130
at breakneck speed, and only find its zen
again once all of them were dead.

02:57:07.130 --> 02:57:12.690
And if a picture of SCP-096 was leaked onto
the Internet, it would cause a chain reaction

02:57:12.690 --> 02:57:17.550
that triggered an XK-Class End of the World
Scenario. There were plenty of creatures and

02:57:17.550 --> 02:57:21.950
entities that the Foundation knew about that
could theoretically bring about the apocalypse,

02:57:21.950 --> 02:57:26.120
but to him, 096 was the most realistic.

02:57:26.120 --> 02:57:31.211
It preyed upon the inherent curiosity and
thirst for knowledge seeded in every human

02:57:31.211 --> 02:57:36.620
heart. The same drive to simply Know that
led to the creation of the SCP Foundation

02:57:36.620 --> 02:57:43.320
would be humanity’s doom in the face of
an SCP-096 outbreak, and if such a thing happened,

02:57:43.320 --> 02:57:49.290
the Foundation would need to break the Masquerade
of secrecy to have any hope of saving humanity.

02:57:49.290 --> 02:57:55.141
In his many feverish nightmares, Dr. Dan had
seen it all so clearly. The first photographs

02:57:55.141 --> 02:58:00.931
of SCP-096’s face would be leaked online.
Perhaps a few hundred people across the globe

02:58:00.931 --> 02:58:05.871
would look, in disparate enough locations
that it would be almost impossible for the

02:58:05.871 --> 02:58:13.150
Foundation to detect and save all of them
as SCP-096 went on its bloody rampage. Of

02:58:13.150 --> 02:58:19.221
course, as the image proliferated, as images
on the internet do, the purview of 096’s

02:58:19.221 --> 02:58:24.811
violence would only grow. People across the
nation would start to take notice.

02:58:24.811 --> 02:58:28.061
And soon after that, across the world.

02:58:28.061 --> 02:58:33.190
“Mysterious creature causing mass casualties”
would be the headline on every news desk,

02:58:33.190 --> 02:58:37.900
because why would anybody want to talk about
anything else? Perhaps some foolish reporters

02:58:37.900 --> 02:58:43.351
would take video footage of the creature.
Everyone has a phone these days with high-definition

02:58:43.351 --> 02:58:48.420
cameras, pictures would be taken and posted.
More and more and more of it would flood the

02:58:48.420 --> 02:58:53.820
internet. Too many simultaneous moles for
even the SCP Foundation to whack. The news

02:58:53.820 --> 02:58:59.300
would spread. Panic and hysteria would spread.
People wouldn’t even know what’s triggering

02:58:59.300 --> 02:59:03.820
all this horror if the Foundation didn’t
go public and tell them that it was the pictures

02:59:03.820 --> 02:59:05.980
that was killing everyone.

02:59:05.980 --> 02:59:09.250
And by then, wouldn’t it already be too
late?

02:59:09.250 --> 02:59:14.840
The antidote to fear is knowledge. It’s
why, when anything goes wrong, we’re refreshing

02:59:14.840 --> 02:59:20.190
timelines and feeds and news websites for
any kind of information. And if you were told

02:59:20.190 --> 02:59:24.860
an unstoppable monster was killing scores
of people and might be coming to a neighborhood

02:59:24.860 --> 02:59:29.860
near you sometime soon, wouldn’t you want
a good look at it? Wouldn’t you want to

02:59:29.860 --> 02:59:36.880
know your enemy, having no idea that the mere
act of knowing was what sealed your horrible

02:59:36.880 --> 02:59:37.880
fate?

02:59:37.880 --> 02:59:42.011
By Dr. Dan’s bleak estimations, if only
several million people died as a result of

02:59:42.011 --> 02:59:46.400
this kind of scenario, it could be considered
a positive outcome.

02:59:46.400 --> 02:59:52.561
He’d seen it all. But no matter how many
times he tried to articulate this to his superiors,

02:59:52.561 --> 02:59:59.490
he wasn’t listened to. They didn’t take
him or the threat that SCP-096 posed seriously.

02:59:59.490 --> 03:00:05.650
Every time he warned of the apocalyptic potential
of SCP-096, he was simply told to get back

03:00:05.650 --> 03:00:11.671
to working on those goggles. It was why he
needed to give those naysayers a demonstration.

03:00:11.671 --> 03:00:17.160
It was why a certain photograph needed to
show up in the home of a certain mountaineer.

03:00:17.160 --> 03:00:24.471
It’s why all those people had to die. There
was no other way. The deaths of the civilians

03:00:24.471 --> 03:00:29.750
and the researchers and the Mobile Task Force
members were, of course, regrettable, but

03:00:29.750 --> 03:00:35.971
it was the only way to prevent the deaths
of so many more. They gave him what he wanted:

03:00:35.971 --> 03:00:40.870
Permission to put this gangly time bomb in
the ground, even if the beast’s death was

03:00:40.870 --> 03:00:46.630
inextricably tied to his own execution at
the hands of the very employers who authorized

03:00:46.630 --> 03:00:47.630
him.

03:00:47.630 --> 03:00:52.200
The world was full of funny little ironies
like that. Another one is that, even though

03:00:52.200 --> 03:00:58.801
Dr. Dan now had the authorization and funding
to terminate SCP-096, he was discovering that

03:00:58.801 --> 03:01:02.550
he lacked another important factor: The capability.

03:01:02.550 --> 03:01:07.840
They’d tried incinerating it. Exposing it
to massive amounts of radiation. Exposing

03:01:07.840 --> 03:01:12.410
it to an insane degree of kinetic trauma - the
equivalent of being hit by an out-of-control

03:01:12.410 --> 03:01:17.960
bullet train. They’d tested every kind of
experimental, off-the-books weaponry they

03:01:17.960 --> 03:01:24.471
had access to through various research projects.
Proton blasts. Lasers. High-intensity energy

03:01:24.471 --> 03:01:31.000
beams. Nothing. Even when they fried off every
scrap of flesh on the monster’s body, its

03:01:31.000 --> 03:01:36.681
unbreakable skeleton still remained, and in
what seemed like no time at all, it’d be

03:01:36.681 --> 03:01:37.950
back in action.

03:01:37.950 --> 03:01:40.881
Its very existence seemed to mock him.

03:01:40.881 --> 03:01:46.740
A guard opened his cell door, staring disdainfully
at the disgraced researcher. That same guard

03:01:46.740 --> 03:01:51.480
had slammed his head against a wall a month
earlier. One of his friends had apparently

03:01:51.480 --> 03:01:56.820
been killed in the legendary 096 containment
breach that set this whole sordid thing in

03:01:56.820 --> 03:01:57.820
motion.

03:01:57.820 --> 03:02:02.860
“Up and at ‘em, scumbag,” he said in
a gruff, surly voice, training a handgun on

03:02:02.860 --> 03:02:07.650
the Doctor he knew wouldn’t fight back.
“They need you in test chamber six.”

03:02:07.650 --> 03:02:15.090
Dr. Dan allowed himself a small grin. Imperceptible
to the guard who clearly hated his guts. They’d

03:02:15.090 --> 03:02:23.681
approved his 096 Vs 682 cross test. Marvelous.
To all the others, it would seem like just

03:02:23.681 --> 03:02:29.760
one more entry in a long line of 682 cross
tests. He thought it darkly funny - In his

03:02:29.760 --> 03:02:36.710
eyes, the Foundation had no hope of ever killing
SCP-682. Its defining quality was being impossible

03:02:36.710 --> 03:02:43.131
to kill, and it didn’t present nearly the
threat to the entire world that 096 posed.

03:02:43.131 --> 03:02:48.130
Their futile attempts to murder the lizard
were little more than a money sink to justify

03:02:48.130 --> 03:02:55.730
the site’s exorbitant funding. But perhaps
he could turn this to his benefit. Maybe SCP-682

03:02:55.730 --> 03:02:59.230
would hold the secret to actually killing
off his nemesis...

03:02:59.230 --> 03:03:05.880
Within 30 hours, Dr. Dan had his disappointing
answer. Both anomalies had undeniably done

03:03:05.880 --> 03:03:11.641
a real number on each other, and Dr. Dan did
take a kind of sick amusement in the mental

03:03:11.641 --> 03:03:20.371
trauma that SCP-682 had induced in 096, but
ultimately it was all for nothing. 682 had

03:03:20.371 --> 03:03:26.280
skinned 096 alive and melted off its flesh
with acid, but once again, that indestructible

03:03:26.280 --> 03:03:28.740
skeleton stood firm.

03:03:28.740 --> 03:03:34.911
This had been the 27th termination attempt
since the incident. The 27th failed termination

03:03:34.911 --> 03:03:41.320
attempt, Dr. Dan mentally corrected himself.
096’s skeleton kept defeating them - Even

03:03:41.320 --> 03:03:45.800
their most ardent attempts to penetrate the
bones and destroy the creature within had

03:03:45.800 --> 03:03:50.570
failed. During a previous attempt, they’d
tried to access the brain by putting a diamond-hard

03:03:50.570 --> 03:03:56.510
drill into its eye sockets. Of course, it
was the drill that actually broke, much to

03:03:56.510 --> 03:03:59.771
Dr. Dan’s seemingly tireless frustration.

03:03:59.771 --> 03:04:04.250
Needless to say, in the debriefing interview
with Dr. Carver, a researcher who once would

03:04:04.250 --> 03:04:09.551
have considered him a colleague, he wasn’t
in high spirits. If he managed to kill this

03:04:09.551 --> 03:04:14.820
thing, then he would always be the hero who
made a terrible sacrifice in order to save

03:04:14.820 --> 03:04:21.070
many more lives. If he didn’t kill 096,
he was little more than the monster who murdered

03:04:21.070 --> 03:04:23.891
all those innocent people for nothing.

03:04:23.891 --> 03:04:26.530
Though you’d never hear him say it out loud.

03:04:26.530 --> 03:04:32.130
If it wasn’t for that damnable indestructible
skeleton, this monster would be long dead

03:04:32.130 --> 03:04:37.170
already. If only there was some way to break
the creature’s bones...

03:04:37.170 --> 03:04:45.181
That’s when it hit him: SCP-173. The Sculpture.
A Site-19 icon often overlooked because of

03:04:45.181 --> 03:04:49.900
its more “silent but deadly” style, but
undeniably one of the most frightening and

03:04:49.900 --> 03:04:54.690
dangerous anomalies out there. It had racked
up a truly shocking body count since it was

03:04:54.690 --> 03:05:01.661
first interred at Site-19 in 1993, one broken
neck at a time, and it’s one of the few

03:05:01.661 --> 03:05:06.490
creatures out there that even the Hard to
Destroy Reptile himself is utterly terrified

03:05:06.490 --> 03:05:07.601
of.

03:05:07.601 --> 03:05:12.681
Perhaps a monster that practically had a PhD
in breaking bones would be the ideal candidate

03:05:12.681 --> 03:05:15.750
to vandalize the Shy Guy’s vertebrae.

03:05:15.750 --> 03:05:20.480
Once his proposal was greenlit for initial
testing, Dr. Dan arranged for the Shy Guy

03:05:20.480 --> 03:05:25.360
- with a bag over his head, of course - to
be brought into a testing chamber with the

03:05:25.360 --> 03:05:32.200
ever-stoic SCP-173. When all of the researchers
and guards were safely outside, all it took

03:05:32.200 --> 03:05:37.450
was a single blink to make all of Dr. Dan’s
darkest dreams come true.

03:05:37.450 --> 03:05:42.181
In the nanoseconds his eyes were closed, he
heard the most beautiful sounds in the world:

03:05:42.181 --> 03:05:49.920
A loud, fleshy crunch, and a pained howl from
096. He opened his eyes to an equally wondrous

03:05:49.920 --> 03:05:56.920
sight: 096 doubled over at an unnatural angle,
its spine snapped between the fourth and fifth

03:05:56.920 --> 03:06:03.280
vertebrae, spinal fluid leaking down the creature’s
flank. That freaky little sculpture had done

03:06:03.280 --> 03:06:07.900
it. It had actually damaged the integrity
of the Shy Guy’s skeleton.

03:06:07.900 --> 03:06:12.280
It was at that moment that Dr. Dan knew he
could kill this thing.

03:06:12.280 --> 03:06:16.081
Admittedly, there was a containment breach
shortly after that when the bag slipped off

03:06:16.081 --> 03:06:21.101
of 096’s head and it charged through the
nearby steel wall, killing multiple people

03:06:21.101 --> 03:06:26.811
before being subdued and recontained. But
when it comes to projects involving Dr. Dan,

03:06:26.811 --> 03:06:32.780
a mere handful of innocent people being killed
sits comfortably within the acceptable margin

03:06:32.780 --> 03:06:33.780
of error.

03:06:33.780 --> 03:06:38.860
A day later, Dr. Dan was standing before a
crowded boardroom, with agents, researchers,

03:06:38.860 --> 03:06:45.110
and even a few members of the O5 Council in
attendance. He explained his findings to them,

03:06:45.110 --> 03:06:49.471
and the fact that, even more importantly,
they may have a way to bring the wretched

03:06:49.471 --> 03:06:53.250
existence of 096 to an absolute halt.

03:06:53.250 --> 03:06:58.740
His plans were approved. Not long after, he
was putting them into motion.

03:06:58.740 --> 03:07:04.811
Dr. Dan was surrounded by researchers and
heavily armed guards as 173 was escorted into

03:07:04.811 --> 03:07:10.301
the test chamber with a forklift. 096 was
there, still bagged. Off to the side was a

03:07:10.301 --> 03:07:17.170
giant tub of hydrofluoric acid connected to
a hose and injector attachment. If this didn’t

03:07:17.170 --> 03:07:19.040
work, nothing would.

03:07:19.040 --> 03:07:24.981
Everyone stood back in a safe zone as 173
was released to do its thing. Crunch. Just

03:07:24.981 --> 03:07:30.891
like the previous time, 096’s spine had
been successfully snapped. Spinal fluid dribbled

03:07:30.891 --> 03:07:36.771
down its skin. Everyone had rushed into the
chamber - One group kept 173 isolated with

03:07:36.771 --> 03:07:42.260
their stares. The other, headed by Dr. Dan,
prepared the acid injector and shoved it into

03:07:42.260 --> 03:07:45.271
the breach in 096’s spine.

03:07:45.271 --> 03:07:50.391
As the acid was pumped by the gallon into
096’s bones, the creature let out the most

03:07:50.391 --> 03:07:56.971
horrifying wail. It kicked and bucked as more
acid was pumped in, melting those indestructible

03:07:56.971 --> 03:08:02.660
bones from within. The creature even began
to vomit acid as it panicked, melting the

03:08:02.660 --> 03:08:08.801
bag off of its own head. Some panicked and
averted their gaze. Dr. Dan didn’t.

03:08:08.801 --> 03:08:14.240
He stared directly at the creature’s face
for the first time as it melted in front of

03:08:14.240 --> 03:08:20.130
him with nothing short of pure elation. Had
he won? Had he actually won?

03:08:20.130 --> 03:08:24.551
The creature let out a gurgling shriek and
the guards opened fire. Their bullets splattered

03:08:24.551 --> 03:08:29.021
into the monster’s melting flesh and bones
as it hollered and shrieked. They didn’t

03:08:29.021 --> 03:08:35.210
let up, firing up and down its body, spraying
it with white-hot lead. It began to melt and

03:08:35.210 --> 03:08:42.130
bubble away, expanding into a great, gruesome
puddle on the floor beneath them. Dr. Dan

03:08:42.130 --> 03:08:44.490
laughed like a madman.

03:08:44.490 --> 03:08:49.881
Even as the guards grabbed him and dragged
him away from the foul-smelling, greasy mess,

03:08:49.881 --> 03:08:55.380
bubbling with chemical life, he just kept
laughing. Even though he knew the death of

03:08:55.380 --> 03:09:01.550
096 meant an equally swift death of his own,
it didn’t matter. It had all been for something.

03:09:01.550 --> 03:09:03.660
The world was saved.

03:09:03.660 --> 03:09:07.340
It was over. He’d finally won.

03:09:07.340 --> 03:09:11.891
Close your eyes and pray for salvation: We’re
returning to the lanky, lethal, lad whose

03:09:11.891 --> 03:09:18.631
rampages often result in legendary losses
of life. It’s SCP-096, the Shy Guy. Long-time

03:09:18.631 --> 03:09:22.939
fans of the channel will no doubt know this
anomalous monster like the back of their hand

03:09:22.939 --> 03:09:26.660
- after all, we have several videos on the
subject that are definitely worth checking

03:09:26.660 --> 03:09:30.940
out sometime - but to get the full benefit
of today’s tale, we’ll do a quick refresh

03:09:30.940 --> 03:09:36.980
for any potential newcomers in the audience.
Let’s start with the basics: The first thing

03:09:36.980 --> 03:09:42.791
you’ll probably notice about 096 is its
daunting size and rather unusual bodily proportions.

03:09:42.791 --> 03:09:47.081
While mostly humanoid in shape, the Shy Guy
has long, dangling arms that almost reach

03:09:47.081 --> 03:09:52.250
the ground. Its body is thin and hairless,
with opaque, clear white pupil-less eyes and

03:09:52.250 --> 03:09:57.820
a wide, gaping mouth. It’s a truly chilling
sight to behold, an abomination that conjures

03:09:57.820 --> 03:10:02.740
up nightmares just to look at it… and we
mean that literally, you don’t want to look

03:10:02.740 --> 03:10:06.280
at this thing, and we’ll get to why in a
moment.

03:10:06.280 --> 03:10:12.351
SCP-096 doesn’t really do much during an
average day. Usually, this tall, spindly humanoid

03:10:12.351 --> 03:10:17.860
entity will spend its downtime just aimlessly
pacing around in its containment cell, and

03:10:17.860 --> 03:10:21.980
perhaps occasionally taking a nap. Pretty
relatable, right? Especially if you’ve been

03:10:21.980 --> 03:10:26.420
trapped in your college dorm room during the
lockdown. But, if some unlucky soul should

03:10:26.420 --> 03:10:32.860
dare to look at SCP-096 - specifically, its
horrible face - that’s when the monster

03:10:32.860 --> 03:10:37.421
starts to act very differently.
Now, someone doesn’t have to be directly

03:10:37.421 --> 03:10:43.351
looking at SCP-096 to make this happen, even
glancing at a blurry, distorted photo of it,

03:10:43.351 --> 03:10:47.880
or watching security footage of the creature
can have the same deadly result. You see,

03:10:47.880 --> 03:10:53.320
the moment the SCP-096’s face is seen, the
wiry monster will know.

03:10:53.320 --> 03:10:57.990
When this happens, the creature suffers what
appears to be a psychological meltdown, covering

03:10:57.990 --> 03:11:04.090
its face with its huge, claw-like hands and
weeping. Hence the nickname, Shy Guy. Utterly

03:11:04.090 --> 03:11:08.250
inconsolable and suffering from extreme emotional
distress, eventually the creature will be

03:11:08.250 --> 03:11:13.881
unable to take it anymore. Standing up, SCP-096
will then travel towards whoever dared to

03:11:13.881 --> 03:11:19.280
look at it, using anomalous speeds to hunt
down the person that saw its face.

03:11:19.280 --> 03:11:25.670
The Foundation has proven that it is unquestionably
impossible for anyone to hide from SCP-096.

03:11:25.670 --> 03:11:30.561
Literally nothing can stand in its path as
it tracks down its victim; not walls or locked

03:11:30.561 --> 03:11:35.260
doors, even being aboard a boat in the middle
of the ocean, or in a plane in mid-air can

03:11:35.260 --> 03:11:42.190
prevent it from finding you. And when it does
find you, it’s not a pleasant sight. SCP-096

03:11:42.190 --> 03:11:46.660
will kill the person that looked at it without
leaving any detectable trace on their body.

03:11:46.660 --> 03:11:51.561
The monster is impervious to gunfire and all
other forms of conventional weaponry. It can’t

03:11:51.561 --> 03:11:56.681
be killed or even so much as harmed. Even
if the Shy Guy is only seen as a tiny little

03:11:56.681 --> 03:12:01.051
speck in a photograph, this will be enough
to trigger the creature’s relentless defense

03:12:01.051 --> 03:12:06.240
mechanism. So, because of this, the SCP Foundation’s
only real way to keep the creature safely

03:12:06.240 --> 03:12:11.240
locked up is in an airtight steel cube, with
no security cameras or surveillance tools

03:12:11.240 --> 03:12:16.641
of any kind. All members of personnel are
reminded that they are to never, ever look

03:12:16.641 --> 03:12:20.431
at it.
But what if they could? What if, somehow,

03:12:20.431 --> 03:12:26.110
someone was able to neutralize SCP-096’s
anomalous abilities? Or was resistant to being

03:12:26.110 --> 03:12:29.470
killed when the creature inevitably tracked
them down?

03:12:29.470 --> 03:12:31.921
This is how the tale known as “Picture Perfect”
begins.

03:12:31.921 --> 03:12:37.101
“What do you think boys? Is she a beauty
or what?” Doctor Clef proclaimed to a group

03:12:37.101 --> 03:12:42.590
of his fellow high-ranking Foundation researchers.
The group looked in confusion and unease at

03:12:42.590 --> 03:12:46.971
what Clef had been referring to, namely a
photograph that he’d just hung up on the

03:12:46.971 --> 03:12:51.011
wall of his office.
A photo of what, though? Well, a four-foot

03:12:51.011 --> 03:12:57.420
by five-foot picture of none other than SCP-096,
of course. Naturally, the other researchers

03:12:57.420 --> 03:13:01.830
shared a mixture of shock and dismay at what
Clef had just done, as you would if one of

03:13:01.830 --> 03:13:06.030
your co-workers hung up a photo of a creature
that could kill you just for looking at that

03:13:06.030 --> 03:13:11.081
said photo. We’ve lost a number of staff
members from around the SCP Explained office

03:13:11.081 --> 03:13:18.120
that exact same way.
Doctor Clef had long been one of the more

03:13:18.120 --> 03:13:22.410
enigmatic and mysterious scientists working
at the Foundation, with the exception of the

03:13:22.410 --> 03:13:28.030
elusive and infamous O5 Council. Alto Clef
was formerly an operative for the Global Occult

03:13:28.030 --> 03:13:33.301
Coalition, as well as being known to be a
bit of a gun enthusiast and all-around crazy

03:13:33.301 --> 03:13:39.051
wildcard. Clef earned his own brand of infamy
for his rumored practice of brutally “decommissioning”

03:13:39.051 --> 03:13:44.990
dangerous SCPs, and you can probably guess
what we mean by that. He’s the Foundation’s

03:13:44.990 --> 03:13:49.660
go-to executioner and a specialist in dealing
with reality-bending anomalies. Would you

03:13:49.660 --> 03:13:53.870
expect any less from an ex-GOC?
Joining Clef in his office were the likes

03:13:53.870 --> 03:14:01.311
of Doctor Jack Bright, another infamous Foundation
researcher. Doctor Bright, also known as SCP-963-2,

03:14:01.311 --> 03:14:07.090
was assigned to conduct experiments on an
anomalous amulet, SCP-963, which resulted

03:14:07.090 --> 03:14:12.860
in his death at the hands of SCP-076, Able.
However, anomalous properties of the amulet

03:14:12.860 --> 03:14:17.980
gave Bright the ability to inhabit new bodies
after his death. Specifically, anyone who

03:14:17.980 --> 03:14:22.730
also touches the amulet, which kills the host
and allows Bright to take control of their

03:14:22.730 --> 03:14:28.001
body. While technically immortal, Doctor Bright
isn’t exactly stoked about that fact, often

03:14:28.001 --> 03:14:31.780
seeking out death and wanting to know exactly
what it feels like.

03:14:31.780 --> 03:14:37.290
Also present at the unveiling of Doctor Clef’s
photo of SCP-096 was his friendly rival, Doctor

03:14:37.290 --> 03:14:43.010
Kondraki, While usually gruff and anti-social,
Kondraki is an expert in perception-based

03:14:43.010 --> 03:14:47.540
anomalies, normally called upon by the Foundation
to photograph SCPs that would otherwise be

03:14:47.540 --> 03:14:52.940
invisible or imperceivable. Doctor Cimmerian,
a member of the Foundation’s Ethics Committee,

03:14:52.940 --> 03:14:58.030
was also in the room as Doctor Clef proudly
showed off his office’s latest decoration.

03:14:58.030 --> 03:15:02.811
Perhaps the most outwardly strange researcher
in attendance was Kain “Pathos” Crow,

03:15:02.811 --> 03:15:06.861
a scientist from the Foundation’s Research
Department who had been transformed into a

03:15:06.861 --> 03:15:12.380
Labrador Retriever after an experiment that
needless to say, went a bit awry. Crow must

03:15:12.380 --> 03:15:14.630
have been barking up the wrong tree on that
one.

03:15:14.630 --> 03:15:19.580
Each of them expressed their reactions differently.
Deeply exasperated, Doctor Cimmerian shook

03:15:19.580 --> 03:15:23.851
his head and began to question his recent
decision to stop drinking. Meanwhile, Doctor

03:15:23.851 --> 03:15:29.110
Bright looked up at the painting with a bemused
tilting of his head. He wondered if SCP-096

03:15:29.110 --> 03:15:33.490
would come to kill him, and if it did, would
it only do so once, or would the creature

03:15:33.490 --> 03:15:38.440
try again and again each time that Bright
inhabited a new body? Perhaps in an attempt

03:15:38.440 --> 03:15:42.960
to look away from the photo, Kain lowered
his head and covered his snout with a paw,

03:15:42.960 --> 03:15:46.870
while Doctor Kondraki hung his head in deep
disappointment.

03:15:46.870 --> 03:15:51.950
The only one among the group that seemed unaffected
by the sight of the SCP-096 photograph was

03:15:51.950 --> 03:15:57.021
Doctor Charles Gears, another high-ranking
SCP Foundation scientist.

03:15:57.021 --> 03:16:02.000
It was Gears who finally broke the uncomfortable
silence, asking Doctor Clef the one question

03:16:02.000 --> 03:16:07.380
that had been on everyone’s minds: why?
Clef excitedly exclaimed that the photo was

03:16:07.380 --> 03:16:12.530
meant to be in celebration of SCP-096 finally
having been neutralized, and according to

03:16:12.530 --> 03:16:16.370
the scientist, they were free to look at the
creature’s “ugly mug” all they wanted.

03:16:16.370 --> 03:16:21.041
The group stared in horror at the gaping mouth,
the screaming expression of the monster that

03:16:21.041 --> 03:16:26.280
had killed so many people. Not one of them
shared in Doctor Clef’s enthusiasm.

03:16:26.280 --> 03:16:30.940
Some, like Bright and Gears, simply argued
that the picture was creepy, while others

03:16:30.940 --> 03:16:37.181
voiced far greater concerns. Kain argued that
putting up a photo of SCP-096 was in poor

03:16:37.181 --> 03:16:41.181
taste, that it was massively disrespectful
to all the unwitting victims that the Shy

03:16:41.181 --> 03:16:46.051
Guy had killed over the years. It was also
reckless and dangerous, Doctor Cimmerian added.

03:16:46.051 --> 03:16:52.001
It had not yet been a full twenty four hours
since SCP-096’s apparent death, and if the

03:16:52.001 --> 03:16:56.061
creature was somehow able to come back to
life, it could easily kill every researcher

03:16:56.061 --> 03:16:59.700
in the office.
By showing them this so-called masterpiece,

03:16:59.700 --> 03:17:04.240
Clef had put all his co-workers’ lives at
risk. He shrugged off their concerns of the

03:17:04.240 --> 03:17:08.980
creature coming back to life, despite knowing
that was entirely possible. After all, it

03:17:08.980 --> 03:17:13.230
wasn’t just anomalies and SCPs that could
defy death, most of the Foundation’s higher

03:17:13.230 --> 03:17:17.660
ranked staff were also capable of the same.
Even some of the researchers in that very

03:17:17.660 --> 03:17:23.900
room had died at least once. Doctor Clef claimed
that, should the worst happen and SCP-096

03:17:23.900 --> 03:17:27.900
return to life, they could simply forget its
face with amnestics.

03:17:27.900 --> 03:17:33.561
Clef claimed that they were now free of SCP-096’s
anomalous powers, thanks to the creature’s

03:17:33.561 --> 03:17:38.330
termination. But the four men and one dog
in his office with him failed to share his

03:17:38.330 --> 03:17:42.851
enthusiasm. The group then began debating
why the Foundation even needed to neutralize

03:17:42.851 --> 03:17:48.480
SCP-096 in the first place, when its containment
purely hinged on nobody ever looking at the

03:17:48.480 --> 03:17:50.540
creature.
“He could be contained by putting a sack

03:17:50.540 --> 03:17:54.580
over his head,” Doctor Bright pointed out,
met with murmurs of agreement from the others.

03:17:54.580 --> 03:17:58.580
“That’s where you’re wrong Jack. There
could be photos of him from before he was

03:17:58.580 --> 03:18:01.910
contained, or someone could get a hold of
the photos we already have of him,” Doctor

03:18:01.910 --> 03:18:05.712
Clef argued back. “If they did something
like hack Markiplier’s Youtube account and

03:18:05.712 --> 03:18:09.710
put that photo on his homepage, it’d be
game over. The Shy Guy would breach containment

03:18:09.710 --> 03:18:14.141
with millions of dash ones to kill all over
the world, and that kind of rampage would

03:18:14.141 --> 03:18:19.370
guarantee he’d have cameras pointed on him
24/7. Exposure would grow exponentially until

03:18:19.370 --> 03:18:23.450
the bastard slaughtered most of civilization.
As far as I'm concerned, he should have been

03:18:23.450 --> 03:18:27.510
classified as Keter from the start.”
But Bright argued back, stating that the Foundation

03:18:27.510 --> 03:18:33.601
had no real cause to kill SCP-096. They already
had a clear understanding that the Shy Guy’s

03:18:33.601 --> 03:18:38.301
anomalous behaviors were only even triggered
by somebody observing it, and preventing that

03:18:38.301 --> 03:18:44.061
from happening was pretty easy. Killing the
creature just seemed excessive, cruel even.

03:18:44.061 --> 03:18:49.230
Cimmerian chimed in again, saying that killing
SCP-096 was something the Global Occult Coalition

03:18:49.230 --> 03:18:54.181
would do, and the Foundation still hadn’t
learned all they could about 096. Could someone

03:18:54.181 --> 03:18:58.320
survive looking at the creature if they were
in space, or another dimension? Now no one

03:18:58.320 --> 03:19:01.261
would ever know.
On top of that, Doctor Gears pointed out that

03:19:01.261 --> 03:19:07.210
the Foundation had no clue what SCP-096’s
exact origin was; where the creature had come

03:19:07.210 --> 03:19:12.080
from, if it was the only one of its kind or
whether it was part of a species of similar

03:19:12.080 --> 03:19:16.290
monsters. If more beings like the Shy Guy
could exist in the future, they could cause

03:19:16.290 --> 03:19:21.450
a global catastrophe. But despite all the
points raised by his colleagues, Doctor Clef

03:19:21.450 --> 03:19:23.440
didn’t want to hear any of what they had
to say.

03:19:23.440 --> 03:19:26.790
“Alright, I hear what you're all saying,
but shut up, you're wrong! The Shy Guy was

03:19:26.790 --> 03:19:31.671
a Sword of Damocles dangling over the whole
world, we killed him, and we're going to celebrate

03:19:31.671 --> 03:19:36.660
that by doing the one thing we never could
while he was alive; looking at his face, each

03:19:36.660 --> 03:19:41.920
and every day, for the rest of our lives,
so that we never forget what we've won and

03:19:41.920 --> 03:19:45.021
could have lost…”
The four researchers and the one who had been

03:19:45.021 --> 03:19:49.880
turned into a dog all took a moment to consider
Clef’s argument - more courtesy than he

03:19:49.880 --> 03:19:55.281
had extended to them. They all looked up at
the haunting, grotesque face of SCP-096 once

03:19:55.281 --> 03:19:59.761
more. As much as they protested the decision
to have the creature killed, each of them

03:19:59.761 --> 03:20:05.421
secretly hoped it stayed neutralized, especially
now that Clef was using SCP-096’s photo

03:20:05.421 --> 03:20:10.811
as an office decoration. If the creature ever
managed to come back, anyone that set foot

03:20:10.811 --> 03:20:14.711
in Clef’s office and looked at the picture
was as good as dead.

03:20:14.711 --> 03:20:19.550
Finally, Doctor Kondraki spoke up and threatened
to reassign Clef to one of the worst positions

03:20:19.550 --> 03:20:24.761
imaginable: Critiquing first drafts of SCP
reports by junior researchers. For a moment,

03:20:24.761 --> 03:20:29.710
Clef thought his rival was bluffing, but eventually
decided it was better to be safe than sorry.

03:20:29.710 --> 03:20:33.851
As the gung-ho Foundation scientist begrudgingly
pulled the photo from his office wall, he

03:20:33.851 --> 03:20:37.460
muttered to the group “None of you appreciate
post-modern art.”

03:20:37.460 --> 03:20:42.171
A group of well-armed Mobile Task Force members
entered the containment chamber, armed to

03:20:42.171 --> 03:20:47.351
the teeth. Some faced the ground, droplets
of sweat dripping onto their visors, some

03:20:47.351 --> 03:20:52.391
wore Dr. Dan’s infamous SCRAMBLE goggles,
and one particularly brave member of personnel

03:20:52.391 --> 03:20:58.010
wielded only a bag. The room they were currently
standing in was perhaps the most dangerous

03:20:58.010 --> 03:20:59.730
place in the world.

03:20:59.730 --> 03:21:00.730
Why?

03:21:00.730 --> 03:21:06.380
Because it contained two terrifying titans
of the anomalous world: SCP-682, the infinitely

03:21:06.380 --> 03:21:13.860
hateful Hard to Destroy Reptile, and SCP-096,
the Shy Guy, the ultimate mindless killing

03:21:13.860 --> 03:21:18.410
machine. And if the very idea of having them
in the same place doesn’t feel risky enough,

03:21:18.410 --> 03:21:23.230
this deadly duo had just spent 27 hours in
one of the most brutal brawls imaginable.

03:21:23.230 --> 03:21:28.660
And incidentally, fights like this are exactly
why we’re here today. We cover a lot of

03:21:28.660 --> 03:21:32.780
SCPs on this channel, and we’ve seen the
way some of the more dangerous anomalies out

03:21:32.780 --> 03:21:37.090
there can tear their way through civilians
and trained Foundation MTF operatives with

03:21:37.090 --> 03:21:43.360
ease. But how do they fare against monsters,
maniacs, and reality benders their own size?

03:21:43.360 --> 03:21:47.471
With the help of the SCP Explained audience,
we’re going to explore theoretical fights

03:21:47.471 --> 03:21:51.101
between some of the anomalous world’s biggest
badasses.

03:21:51.101 --> 03:21:55.570
And who better to get this show on the road
than two of the most iconic and deadly monsters

03:21:55.570 --> 03:22:00.540
that haunt the halls of a Foundation containment
site near you? The Hard to Destroy Reptile

03:22:00.540 --> 03:22:05.751
and the Shy Guy are household names for anyone
familiar with the SCP Foundation, and before

03:22:05.751 --> 03:22:10.023
we get to the gruesome results of their first
fight - and your theories about how a potential

03:22:10.023 --> 03:22:15.570
rematch might play out - let’s get to know
the combat prowess of this pair of nasty customers.

03:22:15.570 --> 03:22:18.931
What kind of damage are they capable of taking
and inflicting?

03:22:18.931 --> 03:22:21.660
Spoiler Alert: It’s a hell of a lot.

03:22:21.660 --> 03:22:27.630
In this corner, we have SCP-682: A monster
whose ability to survive seemingly anything

03:22:27.630 --> 03:22:32.480
is only equaled by its terrible attitude toward
pretty much everyone and everything around

03:22:32.480 --> 03:22:36.870
it. Though to be fair, you’d probably be
a little grumpy too if the SCP Foundation

03:22:36.870 --> 03:22:42.290
was constantly trying to murder you in different
ways rather than just keeping you contained.

03:22:42.290 --> 03:22:46.540
682’s basic offensive power set involves
impressive strength, speed, intelligence,

03:22:46.540 --> 03:22:53.410
and the aid of its deadly fangs, claws, and
tail. However, 682 is considerably stronger

03:22:53.410 --> 03:22:57.450
after it’s just survived and regenerated
from a termination attempt.

03:22:57.450 --> 03:23:03.480
That’s because the true combat asset of
682 is and always has been its adaptability.

03:23:03.480 --> 03:23:09.351
Specifically, if some kind of attack brings
682 into a state of near-death but doesn’t

03:23:09.351 --> 03:23:14.610
finish the job, 682 will generally either
develop a perfect defense or counter-offensive

03:23:14.610 --> 03:23:19.380
for that attack or somehow add a version of
the attack to its own arsenal.

03:23:19.380 --> 03:23:23.771
This has ranged from super-pressurized jets
of its own blood, dangerously radioactive

03:23:23.771 --> 03:23:29.301
banana beams fired out of its mouth, and even
an incapacitating laugher blast gained from

03:23:29.301 --> 03:23:35.330
an encounter with the adorable SCP-999. It’s
an absolute swiss army knife of dangerous

03:23:35.330 --> 03:23:40.061
anomalous powers and abilities - And if you
need any further proof of that, check out

03:23:40.061 --> 03:23:44.891
our multiple videos on all the ludicrous failed
attempts to kill this nightmare lizard.

03:23:44.891 --> 03:23:51.610
And in this corner, we’ve got SCP-096, the
Shy Guy. While 682 is freakishly adaptable,

03:23:51.610 --> 03:23:57.391
in most situations, SCP-096 is pretty much
unstoppable. This long, gangly creep will

03:23:57.391 --> 03:24:02.830
murder anyone who sees even the tiniest glimpse
of its face with such ferocity that not even

03:24:02.830 --> 03:24:08.690
a trace of the body will remain. It does this
by entering what many call its “Rage State”,

03:24:08.690 --> 03:24:13.690
activated by the sudden immense distress of
its face being seen. When it enters this state,

03:24:13.690 --> 03:24:18.851
the SCP Foundation has only one prerogative:
Get themselves and anyone else out of its

03:24:18.851 --> 03:24:22.980
way, because you will not stop this thing
until it’s killed its target.

03:24:22.980 --> 03:24:27.641
All the SCP Foundation has ever been able
to do is wait until after the target has been

03:24:27.641 --> 03:24:33.210
obliterated, and then come in to bag its head
when it enters its docile state. Almost nothing

03:24:33.210 --> 03:24:38.300
else has even successfully slowed it down.
While they have been capable of damaging its

03:24:38.300 --> 03:24:43.000
flesh, the monster’s skeleton seems to be
utterly indestructible, and its damaged flesh

03:24:43.000 --> 03:24:48.210
has proven consistently able to grow back.
The monster seems to be immune to everything

03:24:48.210 --> 03:24:53.050
from pain to cold, and moves at such a speed
that it’s pretty much impossible to escape

03:24:53.050 --> 03:24:54.050
for long.

03:24:54.050 --> 03:24:59.550
So when you factor in an indestructible body,
immense strength, immense speed, and an unstoppable

03:24:59.550 --> 03:25:04.230
urge to kill anyone who sees its face, you’re
looking at a formidable opponent.

03:25:04.230 --> 03:25:10.001
It’s only natural that the Foundation would
want to use 096 in their 682 cross tests,

03:25:10.001 --> 03:25:14.090
so what exactly happened when the Mobile Task
Force entered the room after their first epic

03:25:14.090 --> 03:25:18.550
battle? What did they find inside the chamber
in the grisly aftermath?

03:25:18.550 --> 03:25:25.101
SCP-096 lay crumpled in the corner, wheezing,
scarred, covered in its own blood. SCP-682

03:25:25.101 --> 03:25:30.610
was barely recognizable - SCP-096 had reduced
it to a quarter of its original size, the

03:25:30.610 --> 03:25:35.901
rest having been utterly torn to shreds. Both
had narrowly survived the encounter, but it

03:25:35.901 --> 03:25:41.400
was an epic battle neither of them would ever
forget - Though this was especially true for

03:25:41.400 --> 03:25:42.400
SCP-096.

03:25:42.400 --> 03:25:49.190
You see, as we alluded to earlier, SCP-682
is used to taking a kicking at this point.

03:25:49.190 --> 03:25:54.271
It hasn’t ever truly been killed, but it’s
been beaten, eaten, burned, zapped, and smashed

03:25:54.271 --> 03:25:59.070
within an inch of its life more times than
you can count. 096, on the other hand, was

03:25:59.070 --> 03:26:04.721
not used to not being able to utterly eliminate
its prey. While it’s impossible to speculate

03:26:04.721 --> 03:26:10.641
what’s going on inside SCP-096’s mind,
all evidence points towards getting a draw

03:26:10.641 --> 03:26:14.440
in this battle, seriously damaging SCP-096’s
confidence.

03:26:14.440 --> 03:26:20.670
Now, every time Foundation personnel tries
to bring 096 into 682’s containment chamber,

03:26:20.670 --> 03:26:27.931
he begins crying and scratching at his own
face. Why? Is he scared of 682? Is he humiliated,

03:26:27.931 --> 03:26:32.380
thinking of the fight he wasn’t able to
win? Or is the very idea of someone seeing

03:26:32.380 --> 03:26:38.510
his face, and him not being able to immediately
kill them, just too unbearably stressful?

03:26:38.510 --> 03:26:42.840
Seeing as the Shy Guy’s mouth is only good
for two things - and neither of them are talking

03:26:42.840 --> 03:26:48.690
- we’ll never truly know. But either way,
it’s clear that 096 has no interest in a

03:26:48.690 --> 03:26:49.690
rematch…

03:26:49.690 --> 03:26:52.021
...But what if that changed?

03:26:52.021 --> 03:26:58.120
What if 096 realized that it was wasting its
potential. It managed to get SCP-682 three-quarters

03:26:58.120 --> 03:27:02.881
of the way to death last time, what could
stop it from getting that last quarter? Sure,

03:27:02.881 --> 03:27:08.471
nothing has ever killed SCP-682 before, but
there’s a first time for everything, right?

03:27:08.471 --> 03:27:12.431
Maybe 096 would have a Rocky-style training
montage, where he rebuilds his confidence

03:27:12.431 --> 03:27:17.880
and becomes the underdog he was always meant
to be. He’d walk right up to that big, nasty

03:27:17.880 --> 03:27:20.521
reptile, and give him the rematch of the century.

03:27:20.521 --> 03:27:21.900
What would happen then?

03:27:21.900 --> 03:27:27.910
Now, we give the floor to you, the loyal viewers
of SCP Explained. Here are some of your thoughts,

03:27:27.910 --> 03:27:33.190
theories, and opinions on a potential historic
rematch between these two creeps.

03:27:33.190 --> 03:27:38.860
Let’s get ready to rumble!

03:27:38.860 --> 03:27:46.521
PinguWithAnAxe said, “If 682's adaptability
is based around his ability to develop countermeasures

03:27:46.521 --> 03:27:51.181
to any damage inflicted (or in the case of
173, a countermeasure to the cause of said

03:27:51.181 --> 03:27:57.380
damage) I imagine 682 would turn his skin
entirely reflective, causing 096 to see his

03:27:57.380 --> 03:27:59.940
own face and the feedback loop would break
his mind.”

03:27:59.940 --> 03:28:06.681
Interesting. Whether this would work is all
dependent on whether SCP-096 can actually

03:28:06.681 --> 03:28:11.971
see, or if it is merely capable of psychically
intuiting when someone else sees its face.

03:28:11.971 --> 03:28:18.381
If the latter is true, then this particular
adaptation wouldn’t do much good for SCP-682.

03:28:18.381 --> 03:28:23.061
Random User said, “If this is after their
first cross test and they were forced to interact

03:28:23.061 --> 03:28:26.881
again, they wouldn’t attempt to fight each
other. I feel like they’d form a truce and

03:28:26.881 --> 03:28:29.351
take out the researchers on-site.”

03:28:29.351 --> 03:28:34.391
This would be one of the worst-case scenarios.
Whether even the SCP Foundation could handle

03:28:34.391 --> 03:28:40.550
a simultaneous 682 and 096 breach without
bringing in Hammer Down is an open question.

03:28:40.550 --> 03:28:46.261
Ramon Voncken said, “Simple, after the first
encounter, when 096 stopped attacking 682,

03:28:46.261 --> 03:28:50.420
682 will grow eyes that filter out 096s face
the next time.”

03:28:50.420 --> 03:28:54.610
Organic SCRAMBLE goggles. Dr. Dan would be
fuming with jealousy.

03:28:54.610 --> 03:29:00.090
Koopsader said, “I really want SCP-096 to
win so I’m sending him a blind personal

03:29:00.090 --> 03:29:04.091
trainer so he can beat the snot out of SCP-682.”

03:29:04.091 --> 03:29:09.990
This is exactly the kind of help SCP-096 needs.
He’s got the rage, endurance, and strength,

03:29:09.990 --> 03:29:14.181
with a little help getting his form and technique
in order, he’d be an even more formidable

03:29:14.181 --> 03:29:15.181
opponent.

03:29:15.181 --> 03:29:20.141
Kytoe Ryu said, “I'd like to imagine 096,
after playing with 682 for so long, would

03:29:20.141 --> 03:29:26.230
try to eat 682 in a vain attempt to kill it,
only to gain 682’s abilities, and vice versa.”

03:29:26.230 --> 03:29:33.540
Either of these options would be horrifying.
SCP-096 with 682’s abilities, or 682 with

03:29:33.540 --> 03:29:38.580
096’s abilities, would likely be a truly
perfect killing machine.

03:29:38.580 --> 03:29:43.500
Autumn said, “What If they just shared a
nice cold drink while playing Mario Kart?

03:29:43.500 --> 03:29:47.000
I’m sure 096 would be the white shy guy
character in every race.”

03:29:47.000 --> 03:29:51.190
Personally, we love this idea, and we think
SCP-682 would go for Bowser.

03:29:51.190 --> 03:29:58.211
Ibraheem Temur said, “682 would win eventually.
It’s way more intelligent, adaptive, and

03:29:58.211 --> 03:30:02.030
overall malicious while 096 acts just on instinct.”

03:30:02.030 --> 03:30:07.561
This is an undeniably good point. What 682
lacks in sheer savagery, it makes up for in

03:30:07.561 --> 03:30:12.561
its ruthless, calculating intelligence. Could
this give it the edge over 096?

03:30:12.561 --> 03:30:16.650
Monomoy Mukherjee said, “That fight will
be interesting because both of them now know

03:30:16.650 --> 03:30:19.261
how they attack and what their attacks are.”

03:30:19.261 --> 03:30:25.190
Nice segue. Due to the relatively mindless
nature of 096, as far as we’re aware, it’s

03:30:25.190 --> 03:30:29.780
probably unable to learn from experience.
682, however, would have the advantage of

03:30:29.780 --> 03:30:33.090
knowing how 096 operates in the second battle.

03:30:33.090 --> 03:30:38.480
Devan Smith said, “As much as I'd like to
see how this would play out, I imagine 096

03:30:38.480 --> 03:30:43.280
can't manage more than other SCP's have in
the past. Not to downplay him, but he might

03:30:43.280 --> 03:30:46.400
need some extra friends to do a whole lot
more damage.”

03:30:46.400 --> 03:30:51.011
It’s undeniable that going in with tag team
partners would better 096’s chances of taking

03:30:51.011 --> 03:30:56.950
the lizard down. However, due to SCP-096’s
shyness, and tendency to kill everyone and

03:30:56.950 --> 03:31:01.740
everything that looks at him, he doesn’t
have many friends looking to volunteer.

03:31:01.740 --> 03:31:07.330
Nyan Tails said, “096 will cry in the corner
while 682 starts attacking it eventually it

03:31:07.330 --> 03:31:11.860
gets confused that 096 isn't fighting back
after an hour and begins to adapt to it and

03:31:11.860 --> 03:31:14.030
becomes docile forever.”

03:31:14.030 --> 03:31:18.200
Look, while it’s unlikely that this would
happen, it’s certainly nice to imagine,

03:31:18.200 --> 03:31:19.200
isn’t it?

03:31:19.200 --> 03:31:23.911
Adam Britton said, “Narratively it needs
to conclude with a reason to never try it

03:31:23.911 --> 03:31:29.760
again. So I'm going to say that 682 will telepathically
project the image of 096 into the site staff

03:31:29.760 --> 03:31:32.450
or the entire human pychospace.”

03:31:32.450 --> 03:31:38.780
Yikes. This is a possibility so terrifying
- And honestly, plausible - that it’s making

03:31:38.780 --> 03:31:42.070
us regret wanting to see this rematch altogether.

03:31:42.070 --> 03:31:45.811
Chaos Insurgency said, “I think 682 would
just think "For damn’s sake, just leave

03:31:45.811 --> 03:31:46.950
me alone!"

03:31:46.950 --> 03:31:51.471
Given he’s been through more termination
attempts than SCP-5031 can count on its fingers,

03:31:51.471 --> 03:31:54.610
who could blame him for getting a little frustrated?

03:31:54.610 --> 03:31:59.551
Michael Porter said, “I think that 682 and
096 would take each other to court for aggravated

03:31:59.551 --> 03:32:03.960
assault against the other. Either one of them
will get sentenced to death and will unsurprisingly

03:32:03.960 --> 03:32:08.450
not die or the 2 team up to kill the entire
courtroom and they walk out as a new serial

03:32:08.450 --> 03:32:09.570
killing team.”

03:32:09.570 --> 03:32:13.561
The real question is what kind of lawyers
would represent a pair of murderous, anomalous

03:32:13.561 --> 03:32:15.841
monsters in court. Better call Saul.

03:32:15.841 --> 03:32:20.200
Ari Wertman said, “Maybe if we try being
nice to them for once, we can offer them a

03:32:20.200 --> 03:32:25.420
cup of tea and get them both a therapist.
682 will talk all his hate away and then realize

03:32:25.420 --> 03:32:30.010
his wrongdoing and become an activist, and
096 would become very confident about his

03:32:30.010 --> 03:32:32.641
face and become a model. There problem solved.”

03:32:32.641 --> 03:32:36.271
Hey, when people strive towards self-improvement,
we’re all winners.

03:32:36.271 --> 03:32:42.320
But we don’t know if we can say the same
for 682 and 096. Even if this rematch ever

03:32:42.320 --> 03:32:47.851
did happen, perhaps it’d just be another
draw - An unstoppable force against an immovable

03:32:47.851 --> 03:32:53.141
object. But other match-ups may be more clear-cut.
What kind of Vs battles would you like to

03:32:53.141 --> 03:32:57.370
see us cover in the future? What SCPs would
you really love to see duke it out?

03:32:57.370 --> 03:33:01.970
Let us know in the comments, and keep an eye
on the community posts, in order to throw

03:33:01.970 --> 03:33:05.431
in your anomalous two cents for our next Vs
battle!

03:33:05.431 --> 03:33:10.311
A streak of warm blood splattered across the
snow. Steam rose off it as it fizzled into

03:33:10.311 --> 03:33:14.980
the ice crystals below. The severed leg it’d
just spurted from lay twitching only a few

03:33:14.980 --> 03:33:19.820
inches away, torn clean off at the hip like
a broken Barbie doll. I think it might’ve

03:33:19.820 --> 03:33:20.970
belonged to Arthur.

03:33:20.970 --> 03:33:25.061
It wasn’t the first time I’d seen limbs
cut off. Hell, I’ve cut a few off myself

03:33:25.061 --> 03:33:29.610
in my time. Don’t worry, I’m not a serial
killer - Just a med student. We all were.

03:33:29.610 --> 03:33:33.181
I couldn’t say the same for the nightmare
we encountered up in the mountains that terrible

03:33:33.181 --> 03:33:34.181
day.

03:33:34.181 --> 03:33:38.561
The thin, cold air was laden with screams.
Mine, my friends’, and that...Thing’s.

03:33:38.561 --> 03:33:45.720
All that it’d done to us. All that it was
still doing to us. And never once did it stop

03:33:45.720 --> 03:33:51.280
shrieking and sobbing at the top of its lungs,
like it wasn’t even in control of its actions.

03:33:51.280 --> 03:33:56.141
It didn’t want to do this. It just had to.
This violence was as inevitable as the sunset.

03:33:56.141 --> 03:34:02.010
I screamed for Kyle. The snow was coming down
thick and fast, a curtain of burning, brilliant

03:34:02.010 --> 03:34:06.080
white. I couldn’t see anyone, not even the
thing that was killing us. The water in my

03:34:06.080 --> 03:34:12.931
eyes was starting to freeze up. It was cold.
So damn cold. What the hell was going on here?

03:34:12.931 --> 03:34:17.181
Suddenly, a dark shape was moving towards
me through the wall of falling snow. It was

03:34:17.181 --> 03:34:22.641
human. Vaguely. Moving towards me with frantic
speed. I tensed in anticipation when I then

03:34:22.641 --> 03:34:27.760
saw Kyle emerging from the fog. He was splattered
with blood. His face red from screaming and

03:34:27.760 --> 03:34:33.080
exertion. His eyes pleaded for help I couldn’t
give him. The horror caused my body to react

03:34:33.080 --> 03:34:37.851
on auto-pilot with my eyes clamming closed
in terror, all I could do was open my mouth,

03:34:37.851 --> 03:34:41.320
point with a shaking, frostbitten finger,
and force out a scream:

03:34:41.320 --> 03:34:42.500
“Behind you!”

03:34:42.500 --> 03:34:47.780
My words were carried off by the brutal mountain
winds, and with the sounds of mutilation ahead

03:34:47.780 --> 03:34:53.590
of me, all I could imagine with eyes clamped
closed as I hit the frozen ground was a long,

03:34:53.590 --> 03:35:00.720
grey arm emerging from the flurry behind Kyle,
its gnarled, clawed fingers reaching for his

03:35:00.720 --> 03:35:01.720
head...

03:35:01.720 --> 03:35:05.820
It was meant to be the vacation of a lifetime.
The kind of thing that you could only experience

03:35:05.820 --> 03:35:10.220
once, but you’d never forget. We all thought,
in our own ways, that we’d look back on

03:35:10.220 --> 03:35:14.880
the photos with misty eyes when we were all
old men. We didn’t know that, within a week

03:35:14.880 --> 03:35:19.891
of arriving in Switzerland, whether any of
us would even make it to old age would suddenly

03:35:19.891 --> 03:35:21.891
become an open question.

03:35:21.891 --> 03:35:26.250
All four of us were doctors in training. Even
if you don’t know much about the profession,

03:35:26.250 --> 03:35:29.590
it probably won’t surprise you to hear that
there’s a lot of practice and education

03:35:29.590 --> 03:35:34.730
involved before you can ever really call yourself
a doctor without feeling like a fraud. You

03:35:34.730 --> 03:35:39.391
need to ace your SATs, then slog through four
years of pre-med, another four years of medical

03:35:39.391 --> 03:35:43.121
school, then the better part of a decade doing
your residency.

03:35:43.121 --> 03:35:47.431
Seems a little much, I know, but believe me,
you don’t want some cowboy sticking his

03:35:47.431 --> 03:35:50.550
hands in your chest cavity without all the
proper training.

03:35:50.550 --> 03:35:55.280
But after we finally graduated from Harvard
Med, my friends and I decided that we needed

03:35:55.280 --> 03:36:01.351
a break before the profession swallowed us
back up again. It was me, Brandon, Arthur,

03:36:01.351 --> 03:36:06.141
and of course, Kyle. A lot of people I attended
class with would probably make a tidy sum

03:36:06.141 --> 03:36:10.450
over the course of their medical careers.
Many of them would have made fine doctors.

03:36:10.450 --> 03:36:15.581
But all of us knew that Kyle Molloy was more
than that - He was going to be a truly great

03:36:15.581 --> 03:36:16.581
doctor.

03:36:16.581 --> 03:36:21.960
He was smart, confident, driven, and had the
kind of smooth, effortless charm that made

03:36:21.960 --> 03:36:27.811
you just feel safe around him. Where he went,
we followed. So naturally, when he suggested

03:36:27.811 --> 03:36:32.650
we take a week-long sojourn in Switzerland,
the rest of us were all for it. We expected

03:36:32.650 --> 03:36:38.681
an easy week of gourmet cheese, fine chocolate,
and the occasional jaunt down some old historic

03:36:38.681 --> 03:36:43.101
districts. The exact kind of relaxing break
we needed after the soul-crushing pressure

03:36:43.101 --> 03:36:46.530
of eight years studying the hallowed art of
medicine.

03:36:46.530 --> 03:36:51.400
But of course, that wasn’t what Kyle had
planned for us. It turned out that his tastes

03:36:51.400 --> 03:36:56.521
were far more extreme than what the rest of
us had in mind. His vision of a Swiss vacation

03:36:56.521 --> 03:37:01.130
was mostly forest hikes, backcountry skiing,
snowboarding, and a little mountaineering

03:37:01.130 --> 03:37:04.900
through the Swiss Alps. Apparently, he’d
spent a good portion of his childhood taking

03:37:04.900 --> 03:37:09.900
Swiss vacations, and wanted to give us a little
slice of his personal paradise.

03:37:09.900 --> 03:37:14.860
As someone who wasn’t exactly crazy about
heights or the cold, I can’t pretend I was

03:37:14.860 --> 03:37:19.051
eager at first. But Kyle always found a way
of talking us back around.

03:37:19.051 --> 03:37:23.631
It wasn’t long until we were booking our
tickets, then sitting on the plane, watching

03:37:23.631 --> 03:37:28.530
the mountains get bigger through the porthole,
then our feet were on Swiss soil. It was a

03:37:28.530 --> 03:37:33.271
beautiful country, and every bit as cold as
I feared. But soon enough, the cold would

03:37:33.271 --> 03:37:35.601
be the absolute least of our worries.

03:37:35.601 --> 03:37:39.841
As I glanced out over the breathtaking view
of the Alps, I had no idea of the terrible

03:37:39.841 --> 03:37:45.021
nightmare lurking out there, hidden in the
dark spaces of those great rocks.

03:37:45.021 --> 03:37:48.490
Something I learned in med school is that
human beings are kind of like fleshy time

03:37:48.490 --> 03:37:54.101
bombs, their fuse ever-burning. When you reach
a certain age - and believe me, that age is

03:37:54.101 --> 03:37:58.460
younger than you think - the assassin that
will one day sneak up from within and take

03:37:58.460 --> 03:38:03.461
your life is already waiting inside you. The
cluster of malformed cells that will one day

03:38:03.461 --> 03:38:08.311
turn into an inoperable malignant tumor. The
early whispers of the heart defect that gives

03:38:08.311 --> 03:38:13.080
your ticker its planned obsolescence. The
microscopic ropes of defective genes that’ll

03:38:13.080 --> 03:38:15.580
one day turn into Alzheimer’s or Parkinson’s.

03:38:15.580 --> 03:38:21.021
In some cases, it’s just a matter of whether
you can catch it in time. But in just as many

03:38:21.021 --> 03:38:26.061
cases, there really is nothing you can do.
You’re just waiting for it to get you.

03:38:26.061 --> 03:38:30.040
After three days in Switzerland, we were suiting
up in our thick coats and climbing boots,

03:38:30.040 --> 03:38:35.290
having no idea that the same principle applied
to us. Except this wasn’t that nasty little

03:38:35.290 --> 03:38:40.450
assassin within - it was something outside.
Something that none of our skills or knowledge

03:38:40.450 --> 03:38:46.592
could save us from. Something very old and
very dangerous. We were in fate’s hands

03:38:46.592 --> 03:38:49.760
now, and sometimes, fate can be cruel.

03:38:49.760 --> 03:38:54.030
Kyle was steaming ahead. He didn’t just
want us to ascend just any mountain peak,

03:38:54.030 --> 03:38:59.330
he wanted us to attempt the Dufourspitze,
the highest peak of the Monte Rosa massif,

03:38:59.330 --> 03:39:04.380
and the Alps as a whole. Kyle didn’t think
anything less than that would be a challenge.

03:39:04.380 --> 03:39:08.271
After all, this was our once in a lifetime
vacation before we fully started our residency.

03:39:08.271 --> 03:39:12.070
Didn’t we want to make this a truly remarkable
experience?

03:39:12.070 --> 03:39:14.851
And because it was Kyle, none of us disagreed.

03:39:14.851 --> 03:39:20.500
We started at the base of the Monte Rosa and
began to rise. Its lowest levels barely resembled

03:39:20.500 --> 03:39:26.391
its frosty peak, over 4,600 feet above. We
took pictures of some of the wildlife: The

03:39:26.391 --> 03:39:31.250
grazing Ibexes, Red Deer, and Chamois. Arthur
had bought a pamphlet on the local wildlife

03:39:31.250 --> 03:39:34.881
back at a tourist-friendly mountain lodge
and took great joy in pointing out the different

03:39:34.881 --> 03:39:39.330
creatures to us as he saw them. Brandon got
a good laugh about the fact there was a small

03:39:39.330 --> 03:39:45.080
bird native to the area known as the Nuthatch.
Kyle forged ahead, telling tales of his prior

03:39:45.080 --> 03:39:46.080
conquests.

03:39:46.080 --> 03:39:50.790
The climb gradually transitioned from calm
to grueling as we ascended the mountain. I

03:39:50.790 --> 03:39:55.280
could feel the blisters forming on my feet
as the cold first made my face sting, and

03:39:55.280 --> 03:40:00.410
then go completely numb. Arthur, still consulting
his pamphlet, expressed excitement to see

03:40:00.410 --> 03:40:05.530
a marmot when we finally reached 2000 feet
of elevation. Brandon started to complain

03:40:05.530 --> 03:40:10.260
vocally, pouring himself a cup of coffee from
his thermos. Kyle chastised him for wimping

03:40:10.260 --> 03:40:14.450
out already. After all, he said, the hardest
parts were yet to come.

03:40:14.450 --> 03:40:18.101
Oh, if only he knew just how right he was...

03:40:18.101 --> 03:40:22.851
We ascended further, feeling the air thinning
and our legs growing exhausted beneath us.

03:40:22.851 --> 03:40:27.470
I started to resent Kyle as he forged on,
despite it all. How could he keep going when

03:40:27.470 --> 03:40:32.470
the rest of us were faltering? It just wasn’t
fair. When the rest of us needed to take a

03:40:32.470 --> 03:40:37.291
breather, we could feel the sting of his silent
judgment. He was always so damn perfect.

03:40:37.291 --> 03:40:42.101
“We’re almost there,” he kept saying,
even though we all knew it wasn’t true.

03:40:42.101 --> 03:40:47.351
Things took a turn during one of our rest
stops. We heard this strange noise, a kind

03:40:47.351 --> 03:40:53.180
of pained wailing or keening. There was nothing
overtly menacing about it, but somehow, just

03:40:53.180 --> 03:40:58.870
hearing that noise chilled me deeper than
any icy gust of mountain breeze. It’s difficult

03:40:58.870 --> 03:41:04.420
to describe, but that terrible sobbing felt
like the purest distillation of true, bottomless

03:41:04.420 --> 03:41:08.380
despair. I’ve never heard a more hopeless
and mournful noise.

03:41:08.380 --> 03:41:13.010
Referring to his pamphlet again, Arthur speculated
that the noise could potentially be a Lynx.

03:41:13.010 --> 03:41:17.061
Such creatures were apparently native to the
area, and their calls had a bizarrely human

03:41:17.061 --> 03:41:23.101
quality to them. But somehow, deep down, I
knew this thing wasn’t human, and it wasn’t

03:41:23.101 --> 03:41:28.630
an animal either. Whatever was making those
noises defied normal metrics of categorization.

03:41:28.630 --> 03:41:33.391
To the surprise of no one, me least of all,
Kyle decided to lead the way in the direction

03:41:33.391 --> 03:41:38.561
of that awful noise. Brandon and Arthur followed
him closely over the next tundra, getting

03:41:38.561 --> 03:41:43.811
closer and closer to the sobbing. I hung back
a little further, gripped by this profound

03:41:43.811 --> 03:41:48.450
sense of dread. I didn’t want to find out
what was making this noise - I knew, deep

03:41:48.450 --> 03:41:52.891
in my guts, that if ever we found out what
was making that noise, something terrible

03:41:52.891 --> 03:41:53.891
would happen.

03:41:53.891 --> 03:41:56.300
And then, moments later, it did.

03:41:56.300 --> 03:42:01.141
I heard Kyle gasp, for the first time ever.
The snow was blinding my view, and I couldn’t

03:42:01.141 --> 03:42:05.471
make out what was actually happening, until
Arthur ran back toward my direction, and through

03:42:05.471 --> 03:42:09.930
the blanket of white snow and with a voice
barely audible through his trembling said

03:42:09.930 --> 03:42:15.561
there was a long grey creature. He said it
was naked and looked kind of human but definitely

03:42:15.561 --> 03:42:16.561
was not. He turned back in a panic.

03:42:16.561 --> 03:42:20.760
I could barely make out what my buddies were
doing ahead of me, but from the looks of it,

03:42:20.760 --> 03:42:25.670
it seemed like Kyle was walking closer. Every
cell in my body was screaming, “No, no,

03:42:25.670 --> 03:42:26.670
no!”

03:42:26.670 --> 03:42:30.160
But by that point, it was already far too
late for all of us.

03:42:30.160 --> 03:42:35.330
The creature began to turn. Perhaps it was
raw animal instinct that saved me, but I began

03:42:35.330 --> 03:42:40.950
to turn, too, almost in unison, to look away
from the creature. I just knew that, whatever

03:42:40.950 --> 03:42:45.840
this thing was, I couldn’t bear to see it.
And it was at that moment, as my body hit

03:42:45.840 --> 03:42:49.590
the snow and I forced my eyes closed, that
the screaming began.

03:42:49.590 --> 03:42:54.811
First, I could recognize the voices of Brandon,
Arthur, and Kyle screaming, and then something

03:42:54.811 --> 03:43:01.490
worse. I can only assume it was the creature
itself, making the most terrible racket. Shrieking,

03:43:01.490 --> 03:43:08.551
moaning, sobbing. And then...Ripping and tearing,
blood splattering across the snow. I just

03:43:08.551 --> 03:43:13.280
cowered and shook against the ground as a
massacre unfolded behind me. I was listening

03:43:13.280 --> 03:43:16.141
to my friends die, powerless.

03:43:16.141 --> 03:43:21.601
Kyle, all his strength and direction stripped
away by terror, was snatched by the creature

03:43:21.601 --> 03:43:27.681
as freezing, snowy wind gave it a cloak. I
heard Kyle scream, and then never heard anything

03:43:27.681 --> 03:43:34.200
from him ever again. Soon enough, it was quiet
once more. No noise save for the howling wind

03:43:34.200 --> 03:43:37.761
and the quiet sobbing of the abomination from
the snow.

03:43:37.761 --> 03:43:42.440
I don’t know for sure why the creature spared
me, but part of me has always suspected that

03:43:42.440 --> 03:43:46.931
it was because I never looked at it. I’ll
forever be darkly curious as to what that

03:43:46.931 --> 03:43:51.841
monster truly looked like, but my friends
found out, and now I’d never see them again.

03:43:51.841 --> 03:43:56.780
I wandered through the unforgiving cold for
hours, fully expecting that my frozen body

03:43:56.780 --> 03:44:03.260
would be found in the Alps hours, days, or
even weeks later. I didn’t even care anymore.

03:44:03.260 --> 03:44:07.391
The cold numbed my skin, my limbs, and my
thoughts.

03:44:07.391 --> 03:44:11.400
At some point, a helicopter descended onto
the mountainside, full of men in tactical

03:44:11.400 --> 03:44:15.771
gear with insignias I couldn’t recognize.
They wrapped me in an emergency blanket and

03:44:15.771 --> 03:44:20.601
brought me in, telling me I required treatment.
It all turned to static as we fluttered away

03:44:20.601 --> 03:44:25.940
from the mountainside, and all I could think
about was the monster that had taken my friends

03:44:25.940 --> 03:44:26.940
from me.

03:44:26.940 --> 03:44:29.211
It was still out there, waiting.

03:44:29.211 --> 03:44:34.581
Now go check out “SCP-096 - Look at a Picture
of Shy Guy in Space? The Shy Guy Questions

03:44:34.581 --> 03:44:41.750
and Theories” and “SCP-096 Shy Guy ESCAPE
- Incident 096-1-A Containment Breach” for

03:44:41.750 --> 03:44:44.610
more on the Shy Guy that haunts all of our
dreams!

